      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>NEW POST OFFICE FOR</head>
        <p>A NEW POST OFFICE FOR</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The negotiations between the Warrington Cor- poration and the Postmaster General with refer- ence to a site at Springfield-street for a new post office have been satisfactorily completed. It will be remembered that the Corporation acquired "Spring- field and the grounds adjoining from Mr Beck a few years ago. The steadily increasing postal work at Warrington has compelled the authorities to look out for a site on which to build a larger post office. The Corporation was approached and terms have now been agreed upon for the sale of a portion of the "Springfield site. The scheme provides for the continuation ot Chapel-street—the street which adjoins Bold-street and runs alongside the Wesleyan Chapel and the ministers' residences— through the Springfield grounds to Springfield- street. The Post Office will be erected at the corner of Springfield-street, and will be much larger than the existing one. We understand that plans are already in preparation and that building operations will be commenced in the course of a few months, The present Post office buildings will be retained and subsequently used in connection with the engineering department of the Post Office.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH Week ending Saturday, Jan 11th, 1902. During the week, the following births and death were registered : Warrington Registration District, 61 birtbs,!l.7 !deaths. The excess of births over deaths was 44. The birth rate was equal to 481. per 1,000. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 13.7 per I,ooo,'against 15.6, the average of the previous four weeks, and against 17 3, the corresponding period of last year. The death-rate from the seven chief zvmotic diseases was equal to nil per 1,000, against -16, the average rate ot the previous four weeks, and against -78 the corresponding period of 1901. The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. Total 5 1 1 4 2 17. The deaths included-3 from phthisis, 6 from lung disease, 1 from heart disease, 7 from all other Causes. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Scarlet Fever 2 cases, Enteric Fever 1 cases, Puerperal Fever 1 case, Err sipelas 3 cases. Total number of ps,tients in hospital week ending Jan 11th, 17. Metenrology.—Thr mean temperature was 45 Fah., and therefo:e about the same as the preceding week. The maximum temperature 53 F., wag recorded on the 9th, on the inst., and the minimum temperature 35 F. 11th inst. The Rainfall measured .45 inch. The general direc- tion was S.W. 'NAL, 1 Beall</p>
        <p>JOHN GUEST GORNALL, Medical Officer of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>EIGHTY MILLIONS IN NOTES.</head>
        <p>EIGHTY MILLIONS IN NOTES.</p>
        <p>It was on Jubilee Day that Low Covey equated on the kerbstone acd consulted a " Threepenny Guide." That valuable work informed him there were eighty Millions in notes stored in the Bank And a whole cellar- full of bars of gold. " An' nothin, between no and them but a bloomint file o' sojers. Lord ! what foolish things there if in the world ! " said Low Covey, slipping the book in his rocket. Just so. Ha might have leaned against the black iron railinT around the Bank and starved ; not a fiver would have rustled, not a quid clinked to help him. " Hard lines," thought the amiable Young thief ; "money enough to make 3 poor mug, honest, and yet Not a Penny of it Mine And in baek do ache with the tramo and carryin' the 'eavy barsket for the woman' Any-'ow I can come by a Benson's Planter for that to-night." Let us hope he could and did. Became Benoen'el Porous Plasters cast be had without robbing the Bulk : and for pains, aches, coughs and colds, and for spots that are dull, chilled and weak, they are better than bars of gold or the crisp bits of white paper so jealously guarded by the Old Lady of ThreadneedP-sZreet. And then, you know, there are rheumatism, lumbago, influenza, pneu- monia ; more common—even in eurnmer time—than 'busses at the Mansion House. Ben Son's Plasters relieve and cure these ailments while other remedies (0-ealled) are wondering what you bought them for. You need no oils, ointments, salves, &amp;C., nor any other plaster. Bensen's only ; they will do the trick,—for the coach- man on the box or milady who anueglea inside. For Sale in Warrington by H. M. Ashton, 63, Sankey-street ; Boots, Ltd., 5 and 35, Bridge-street ; H. Miller, 77. Bridge-Street ; and J. R. Young, 40, Sankey-street.</p>
      </div>
