      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>DINNER INSTEAD OF DUTY. The Mayor Entertains the Warrington Police Force.</head>
        <p>DINNER INSTEAD OF DUTY. The Mayor Entertains the Warring- ton Police Force.</p>
        <p>-- The Mayor (Mr. J. C. Parr) generously entertained the members of the Warrington Police Forco to dinner on Wednesday and Thursday evening last week The first continent sat down to dinner on v.'ednesday The Chief Constable occupied the chair, and there were also present Dr Reginald Smith (police surgeon), Mr Ilallard (clerk of works, new police buildings), Dote c live Superintendent Monaghan, Inspector Taylor, and a large muster of sergeaLts and constables, some in uniform, but the majority in plain clothes. in excel- , lent dinner was provided by Mr Powys, Town Hall, and I was done full justice to by those present. After the tables had been cleared the Chief C-mstable I rose to propose "The King. Queen, and Royal Family." ' He said the toast which he had to propose was one , which needed no oratory to recommend it to the assem- blies of patriotic Englishme.n. and where would they fine patriotic assemblies if not in Warrington which had lalways been loyal and abundantly proved its loyalty during the present war. There were none more loyal than the members of the Warrington Police Force who were not only subjects of the King, hut were his ser- vants and proud to wear his uniform. Without further ' Preamble he asked them to drink to the "King, his Queen Consort and the member 3 of the Royal Family."</p>
        <p>(Applause.) The toast having been duly honoured the Chief Con- stable said that before proceeding further he had two letters to read. The first was from their ex-Chief Constable, his respected and venerable predecessor, Mr Samuel Hunt, who wrote, expressing his thanks for the invitation and stating his sincere regret that by reason of great weaknoss and continued ill-health, he was unable to be with them. , He (Mr Talbot) WSS sure they would have been pleased to have had their ex-Chief amongst them and Mr Hunt would have been glad to have been there and to see some of the old faces. The next letter would be inter- esting to them all and was very gratifying to himself. It was from their worthy and respected Mayor, Mr J Charlton Parr, whose guests they were 'that evening. The Mayor wrote,--•• Dear am truly sorry to be obliged to forego the pleasure of put ing loan appearance at the dinner I have the pleasure of giving this evening to the Warrington Police Force. I feel it a satisfaction to be able to recognise the services rendered so efficiently and intelligently to the town and neighbourhood by the active and well disciplined Force under your command.! I thank you personally very sincerely, for the trouble you are taking in carrying out my wishes, though I know it is a labour of love to assist me in giving credit I where we feel credit is so well deserved. Will you kindly express my regrets at being unable to look in upon sou this evening ? Hoping the men may enjoy themselves, and wishing them all a very Happy New Year. I am, Yours faithfully. J. Charlton Parr. Mr Luke Talbot, Chief Constable." (Applause.) After songs had been sung by PC Stuart and Detec- tive Meacock, The CHIEF CoNsTatitc submitted the toast of " His Worship, the Mayor." He said, the Mayor in giving them the dinner had acted perfectly spontaneously and without any suggestion whatever being made to him ; it was not even as if it had been an annual affair. Re came to him (Mr Talbot) and asked whether be thought the police would like an invitation to dinner from him and he (Mr Talbot) assured him there was nothing they would like more. The Mayor thereupon gave instruc- tions for the necessary arrangements to be made. The I police were invited to balls, concerts and functions of I the like kind, but it was to work and to stand in outer darkness seeing other people enjoy themselves. That night it was their turn. A large section of the public seemed to think the police should never play, and that they could not smile and enjoy themseloPs. If they had been pre- sent that night they would alter their opinion. It should afford them (the Police) great grounds for satisfaction to think that the Chief Magistrate] of the Borough and Chairman of the Watch Committee, a gentleman whose narneiwas honoured not only in Warrington but ,through out the length and breadth of the country, should ask them to be his guests and pay them the compliment he had done in his letter, He (Mr Talbot) was sure in asking them to drink the Mayot's health he could rely on their doing it with the enthusiasm for which the Warrington Borough Police were so well known. (Ap- plause.) The toast was drunk with musical honours. Mr BALLARD, Clerk of Works, Police Buildings, next entertained the company with some of his humorous i and musical selections, n his inimitab!e manner, and he was deservedly and enthusiastically encored. Mr HALLARD proposed the health of the Chairman,Mr Talbot. He said the longer he knew the Chief Constable the better be liked him, and he was sure it was the same with the members of the Police Force. When he saw him day after day in the Police Court and heard him say such nice things to the magistrates on the Bench he (Ma Ballard) almeat felt that it would be a pleasure to</p>
        <p>Ihim to" get a month." (Laughter). He was amused sometimes to see those poor unfortunate fellows stand behind the iron spikes(which they were going to remove) and aosdre the magistrates that they had not been there before and if let off would never come again, until the Chief Constable read from his sheet offence after offence, and then their heeds wenc down and they could say no more. He actually beard one prisoner say to an officer •• How the—do they get to know things." (Laughter). The CHIEF CONSTABLE, in replying, said be thanked Mr Ballard for the toes': so well proposed. He thanked them sineerely for the manner in which they had received it. He knew it was not merely a matter of courtesy on their part, bue that they wished him good health, and as one man remarked, better than he had had for some time past. He would take this oppor- tunity of saving publicly that during his absences from duty throug'a sickness it was a comfort to him to think that he had about him a body of men whom he could trust, knowing that every man would do what he could to make the ship sail streight while the captain was on his back, and when he returned to duty, weak mentally and physically, he found that everything had worked as well as if he had been in his place in his cffice. It showed that the Warrington Police did not give eye service but were faithful servants and honest men. No better body of meta existed inside the blue coat through,- out the length and breadth of the King's dominion. Be was also pleaeed with the esprit-de-corps which existed in the Force and which caused its members to act almost as one man. (Applause.) Sergeant WILLIAMSON proposed the health of Mr Milani, He said tney all felt greatly indebted to Mr Ballard for his kindness in coming ti ere and entertain- ing them as he had done. They had heard of his fame as an entertainer but that was the first time they had had hie company. As Clerk of Works at the police buildings they almost reckoned him one of them- selves, and judging from his powers of observation displayed at the Police Court, be appeared to be gifted with a considerable amount of detective instinct. Though he was not a policeman he was good enough to be one. (Laughter and applause.) Mr HALLIARD in responding said he had come into contact a good deal with policemen at the 'Aew Police Buildings and had always found them WIN pleasanteend had to thank some of them for little courtesies received at their handy. He hoped they would feel comfortable and contented in the beading when completed. There were places for them all there and be was sure they would be found in their places when required. Dr Seine!, Police Sturgeon, was obliged to leave early, but towards the close of the evening the Town Clerk entered the room. The CHIEF CONSTABLE said he had one MOTO toast to propose and that was the health of their worthy and popular Town Clerk. It was impossible to say how much they owed to Mr Whittle or hie constant and continuous support of the police on all occasions. No public function took place without the Town Clerk having a very great deal to do with its arrangement, and his sympathies were always with the men who did the work. He had proved himself a true friend to the Police Force and there were very few privileges gained from the Watch Committee that were not in a great measure due to his assistance. The TOWN CLERK briefly thanked those present for drinking his health. He said he was always glad to do what he could for the pol:ce, but was sure their Chief MRS their very best friend. In addition to Mr Ballard and those already men- timed, that following rendered songs during the even- ing:—Sergeant Cheney and Constables Hamblett, Clough, Nowey and Chrimes. Mr Percy Randall acted as accompanist. . 1 The party broke up in time for the night duty men to be a t neje posts. 1 THURSDAY'S PROCEEDINGS. ' At six o'clock on Thursday evening the second con' 'agent of the Police Force sat down t 0 dinner at th Town Hall. There were present, the Chief Conetabl 1 (Mr Luke Talbot), in the chair, Mr Ballard, Clerk 0 Works, New Police Buildings, Chief Inspector Phillips Inspector Rowbetham, and the whole of the sergeants, and constables who were on duty, or unable to be present on the previous night. Mr Fowls, Town Flail again served an excellent dinner which was done full justice to by the representatives of law and order. Chief INSPECTOR PHILLIPS proposed the toast of the "King, Queen and Royal Family." He said he knew the toast would be well received. The King was a kindly,courteous English gentlernan,and the Queen was a charming lady. The toast was duly honoured after which Constable Houghton and Sergeant Evans enter- tained the company with songs. Sergeant Evans soon proved himself a favourite and Wet; heartily encored. • The CHIEF CONSTABLE proposed" His Worship the Mayor." The toast was drunk with grout enthusiasm. At thisotage the Town Clerk entered the •room and received an ovation. He thanked the company for their very hearty reception, and said he was sorry he could not remain with them as he had an important meeting to attend. He hoped they would all spend a pleasant evening. Mr EIALLARD again proposed the health of the Chief Constable, and Mr Talbot replied at some length and in the same happy vein as on the night previous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Festival.</head>
        <p>Festival.</p>
        <p>- --- Services in celebration of the annual festival in con- nection with the above guild wore held in Held-street Wesleyan Chapel on Sunday. There were good conere- gations, the preacher at both morning and evening services being the Rev W L Tasker, of Liverpool. The musical portion of the ser- vices was creditably rendered by the choir, the anthems being " Arise, Shine " and "The day Thou Gayest." Hymns were sung to old Methodist tunes and the cervices were of a very hearty description. The colleotions were on behalf of the Guild Funds—A ITOPC leg under the auspices of ths Guild was held in the schoolroom on Midday evening when the Rev W L Tasker delivered a lecture entitled " Oliver Cromwell •, or some lessons for to-day." The chair was occupied by Dr Harcourt who was supported on the platform by the Rev r Bishop,seperintendent at tee circuitarhere- was a largo attendence. In the course of bra remarks ' the Chairman spoke of the useful purpose which was fulfilled by the Wesley Guild. tie said he remembered the time when there was very little to beriefia their young folks; now they had a Wesley guild and a juvenile society. It eller fed him additional pleasure to preside at that meeting because hie 'rasher, the lecturer, , came from the circuit where he (the Chairman) first joined a class as an English Wesleyan. (Applause).— The Lecturer, who was heartilr meived, said he did not regar 1 the large audience that evening as a tribute to himself hut ae a tribute to Oliver Cromwell, whom they might regard as one of England's greatest sons. He thought that whether they regarded Cromwell as a military genius, as a practical politician or as a religious zealot, a fair and impartial examination of his character would compel their admiration and evoke their gratitude. Cromwell, like many another grist Englishman had suffered very t/P3Oll at the hands of historians. Very • soon after his death ' his bones were dug up and hung upon a gibbet at Tyburn and he Ole lecturer) thought historians had been gibbeting hie character and his tnemery ever since. He though far more of Warrington and of Warrington people trims° they hail had the courage aud common sense to erect a statue of the Lord Protector in such a public place, although he understood that Cromwell, like Nichodenaus Came by night. (Laughter.) Prob- ably that was an act of prudence on the part of the COrpOretion of the town. When tteliwas a boy the only thing his was told about Cromwell was that he cut off People's heads and he must mind he did not do that. (Laughter.) He was afraid that teat idea of Cromwell survived for very many years after his death. Hi; faults arose out of the century in which lie lived, and they would be compelled to admit his essential great- ness and moral worth. lie was great as a p aitician but not as a party politician, and he carried out the truth which burned within him, like a fire. lie was a man impregnated with the word of God. It judged the course of his whole life. Cromwell taught freedom 6f speech and to-aay there were representatives of Cromwell in the churches who were opoome to saner- , dotalism and ritualism.—A. vote of thanks was accorded to the chairman and the lecturer and the proceedings closed with the pronouncing of the Benediction by the Rev T Bishop. Tare Daus SPEY DISTILLERY, situate in the finest Whtsky-produelng District of Scotland, is the property of W and A Gilbey. This Whisky Is made entirely from liorne-groWn Barley and is kept absolutely unblended in Hts Majesty's Bonded Warehouses ti naature,and 501d1139 W cud althey's agents in every town atl es 6d per ;bottle.</p>
