      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>PROPOSE° UNDERGROUND CABLE TO WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>PROPOSE° UNDERGROUND CABLE TO WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Inspector llownomast proposed the health of Mr Fla Hard and Mr Hallard replied in a bright and racy speech, bristling with humorous anecdotes. Later in the evening Mr Alderman Hutchinson entered the room and was enthusiastically received. The CIIIEF CONSTABLE proposed the health of the members of the Watch Committee coupled with the name of Mr Alderman Hutchinson. Mr Talbot said they were fortunate in having a Watch Committee who were thoroughly in sympathy with the Police. He never brought before them any scheme for the betterment of the Force that did not receive every con- sideration and almost always confirmation. In 1 everything pertaining to the welfare of the men, , that is in pay, clothing, hours of duty or anything else, they were always willing to do all they could. They had now, at great expense, provided a new Police Station in which every provision had been made for the com- fort and convenience of the Members of the force. (Applause.) Mr ALDEIVIA,N HurcarxsoN replied, thanking the company for toe manner in which they had received the toast. no CMAILI assure them the Watch Committee would in the future, ao they had in the past, give every consideration they could to any proposals tor the benefit of the meth Ile knew they had a smart and efficient Police Force. As a Volunteer officer he had always admired the appearance of the men in uniform. So far as the Evening's programme of music, etc., was concerned Mr Ballard was again the lion of the evening, and with song and story kept the table in a roar. Song,s w ere contrilmtul by Detective Barlow, and Constables Houghton, Woitny, Dennett, and Howard. Mr Percy Randall again acted as accompanist. The company, before breaking up, passed hearty votea of thanks to the Chief Constable, for presiding, to Mr Hallard, to Mr Randall, the accompanist, and to Mr Powis, of the Town Hall, who was caterer.</p>
        <p>OUR LOOM- CHURCHES. Primitive Methodist Chapel, Legh-street At tha above place of worship, on Sunday, morning and evening, the preacher was the Rev 0 Parkin.M. a., 8.0., principal of Manchester College. In the morning I be took for his teat the Words " ELI shall drink of the I brook in the way, therefore shall be lift up the head." The subject was treated in a highly interesting manner, and was listened to with marked attention. Speaking in the evening upon the life of Joseph he contended that God had a plan in every Man's life. The attendance at both services wail good, espesially in the eventog,when - the dispel was crowded. The collections amounted to I about £9.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. There was a very satisfactory attendance at the meet- ing of thig S 'ciety on Siandav afternoon. Mr. J. Haw- thorn presided, and spoke briefly urrm " Citizenship." Mr. D. Neis, of M tooneszer, delivered the address. His subleet was " The Power of Purpose " and he showed , how this was exemplified in the life of Dmiel. He point -d -Jut that if we had a purpose in if and meant to carry it through, th:ra must be downright hard work and resolute purp)se. There was no failure except in ceaElog to try.—The Iv. John Yonge (president of the Society) in the course of a few observations, asked tee membqrs to corsider the advisability of forming a P.S.A. Sivings Club for the purposll'of providing the members wit° a decett sum of money to take their holidays. Similar clubs had been formed elsewhere arid had bean found to be a great success. He would be glad if the members would consider tbe matter at as early date and y,onas to some decision on th) matter.—Mr Arthur Griffiths gave an exeeptionally line rendering of the solo "If with all tour hearts." the service was also taken part in by Mr Geori,e Wallington and Mr John Atkinson.</p>
        <p>"Congregational Federation and Reform." This was the rinbjnt of an address by the, Rev John Yonee in 'Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sunday evebing. The object in view was tn lay before the ; congregation a brief outline of the scheme proposed by Dr Parker for securing greater unity of action amongst the congregational churches of England and Waler. He pointed out that there were 4,600 convegatlonal churches, branches and misaions in th 9 country providing sittings for 1,641,000 persene. There were 403,350 church members, 652,000 scholars, 62,000 teachers, 2,887 rs,e.ognised ministers. 215 evangelists and 5,031 lay preachers. Of the 4,600 churches 2,300 were self-supporting and the rest were supported by grantr from the various churches through the County Unions. The preacher showed that eho present orge.nisation, known as the Congregational Union of England and Wales,was not an effective one in dealing with the questions appertainlng to Congregationalism. Dr. Parker now sought by his</p>
        <p>Ischeme of a United Congregational Church to secure greater unity aroongst the Congregational Churches, , The scheme hael obtained the approval of many leading Congregationalists in this country, and ' was recommended for tho thoughtful consideration of all Congregationalists. The preacher thought this agreement in opinion by men regarded as leaders should commend the proposals to their sympathic considera- tion. Let the aim of it all be steadily kept in view. It was a worthy aim. It was a religious and spiritual aim. There was nothing behind that was to be sue- peeved or distrusted. It was no subtle bid for power wherewith to cripp:e the freedom of the Congregational churchee. It was an earnest and an honest proposal to make the churches more effective, the ministry more effective, and the means of grace, the preaching of the Goepel, more effective upon Ins coescience 'and heart of the nation. They should waet Congregationalism to become more aggressive as a spiritual force in the land, and it was believed by men who had prayed and thought and consulted that in the direction of these proposals lay the path of duty, of service, and success. At the close of the service a special meeting of members of the church was held, when certain important proposals that have been sent to all churches thvuehout the Congrega- tional Union of England and Walea Were considered. The meeting approved of a Federation of the Churches on lines suggested in the letter, bat wished the proposed title "The United Congregational Church" to be" The United Congregational Churches," A proposal that the title should be "The Federated Congregational Churches" was lost. Bold-street Wesley Guild.—Annual</p>
        <p>Direct Communication with Important Centres. Sir John Lentz, M.P. has received a letter from the Postin•aeter-General dealing with the recent interrup- tion of the telegraphic communication and the mime a- tiers that a aubmarine cable should be laid along the cat coast of Great Britain. Toe Postiu tater-General points out the diflicu:ty of 'eying a system of submarine lines as suggested, and compares the merits of such a scheme with the advantages of the department's partly-constructed underground line to tha north very favourably to the latter. He says that the latter underground lino to the north will extend, in the first instance, to Birmingham, Liverpool, Mancheeter, Leeds, Newcastle, Glasgow, and Edinburgh, and will afford security against interruption of the telegraphic communication, not only to these important cities, but also to many intermediate towns, while it will give practical security to Sheffield and toe towns on the east coast, and in- crease the stability of the telegraphic services genera , ally. The underground line is now approaching Stafford, and will be extended as soon as possible to Warrington, whence there are under ground wires already to Liver- pool and Manchester, and the scheme will be so carried out as to fit in throughout with the overground system, and be available for making good interrupted aerial wires at a great many points. The effect of storms on telegraphic communication was under discussion in the House of Commons on Tues- day evening, Mr Renwiek salted the Secretary to the Treasury aS representing the P istmester-General, if he would state approximately the cost thatthe Telegraph and Telephone Department of the Pest Office had been put to in repair- ing the damage done to tie overhead wire; by the storms which took place in November and December last, and the length of the delay which had occurred in the transmission of telegraphic messages between Lon- don and Clean° London and Liverpool, London and Newcastle-on-Tyne, London and Cardiff, Newcastle-on- Tyne and Cardiff, Nevrcastle-on-Tyne and Glasgow, and vice-versa, caused by the said storms. Sir John Lang aske I the Secretary to the Treasury, as representing the Pommaster-General, if he would state how many days and hours the telegraphic service between London and the principal commercial centres of the country wore interrupted during the storms of November and December ; what was the estimated loss of r.:venue from each day's interruption ; what had been tee coet of repairing the wires and renewing the telegraphic eirvice,and what measures were:being taken to prevent a recurrence of similar interruptions in future. Me Auaten Chamberlein, who answered the two ques- tions together, said :—The November storm caused no total interruption between London and any commercial ' centre in the country except intermittently eluring the night of the 13th with places in Scotland. On six desys in the month, however,the delay on the Glasgow circuits amounted to one hour and upwards, and this was also the MO en four days in the month on the Newcastle circuits, on two days on the Liverpool circuits, and on one day on the Cardiff circuits. In December the wires I to Glasgow, Liverpeel, and Newcestle were totally in- terrupted for the greater part of four days. Cardiff was not entirely cut off. On the direct wires between New- castle and Cardiff the interruptions in November were from the 12th to the 15th,and in December from the 9th to the 19th, but except for a short time communication was maintained by other wires. Between Newcastle and Glasgow there was no t;tal interruption, but in December there wae a delay of thirty-five minutes on the 16th. The less of revenue from the Nc- vember interuptione was about £2,500, and from the December interruptions about £5,500. The cost to the Post Office of making good the November breakages was about £2,500. and the December repairs will probably Cost; nearly £27,000. These some do not include the eaneners incurred by the railway companies in carrying ont their obligations to the department. It will not be poisible to prevent interruption, but the Postmaster-General hopes that the measures which have been taken to strengthen the lines, and to provide re- serve wires will render them less serious, and the con- tinuation northwards of the Birmingham underground line will be of great help, There</p>
        <p>is no safer business under than selling Fels-Naptha soap at 2,:sd a bar, your money back if you want it. Your crroceris in it.</p>
