      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>MUNICIPAL TRADING.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL TRADING.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pemberton Again on the Warpath.</p>
        <p>We understand that Councillor Peacock bns been served wita a notice by Mr John Pemberton of his in- tention to sue him for penalties for having acted, it is alleged, in a corporate OffiC3 (i.e. Councillor) while disqualified. We understand that this action has been taken against Councillor: Peacock on the assumption of Mr Pember- ton that Councillor Peaccck is interested in the firm cf. ST Ward and Co., which has recontily obtained a cpa tact from the Corporation.</p>
        <p>Alderman Shaw Green and Mr. Dodds' Charges. [To THE EDITOR OF THE "WARRINGTON EICAMINER"] sir—Mr Dodds' long personal letter is as unjustifiaol as it is unworthy of him.,ln my remarks at the Caun ci meeting I refuted the charges against me, and shewed how I had offered to prove their untruthfulness. I did no; intend to say more, but Mr Dodds' letter leaves ma no alternative. It is indeed on the same lints and is of the same character as the suggestive speeches, against which I so strongly protest. I said at the Council meeting that just as we were preparing our selves for an enquiry before our own special Court, Mr Dodds sent in the resignation of his appointments. Mr Dodds says he resigned his offices two years ago. I know he resigned his office of circuit steward abbot that time but he still retained his membership of the society and so far as I knew and believed, his office as a trustee. If he did not hold thew, ' what was he doing in the quarterly meeting at Penketh in September last and why does his name appear as an officer and member in the Circuit Directory issued last October, which list is before me as I write ? When I was told near the end of December that he had sent in his resignation I inquired as soon afterwards as I could from the only authority (Mr Dodds knows of whom I speak) if it was Sc'. This was on the 20th December last or about 13 days ago. The reply being in the affir- mative, I concluded it meant that he bad resigned everything. He admits however, resigning his member- ship in December. This proves my statement for it was his resignation of that position and that alone which deprived us of the opportunity of proceeding with the enquiry. Another matter. Mr Dodds has been continually asserting that lam the third largest shareholder in Buckley and Co,and suggesting by putting it thus that I must be receiving large profits. This is another phrase that is true and yet wholly untrue. No one knows better than Mr Dodds that the re are other ways of rais- ing capital than' by shares, and the capital of Buckley and Co consists of different classes of securities. My entire cipitai in the company is only £240 or about one fifteenth of the whole. In the last five years all that I have received from the company on my Bhares is £34 81, or £6 16; per annum. Need I comment further ? It is simply sickening to be compelled to defend' one's' self in tnis way, when, if it toad been wished, the slightest enquiry would have saved Mr Dodds, myself and others much annoyance, but I ask, can any I honourable man sit calmly whilst his good name and his reputation are being recklessly assailed ? If Mr Dodds did not authorize these statements and insinua- tions of Mr Williams' he did not protest or endeavour to Stop him—he refused me the opportunity of replying when asktel by Alderman Hutchinson to do so. My offer to put the evidence of the falsity of these state- nsents before Mr Fleming.their secretary,has not been;ar- Cepted and;now after specific denial instead of that frank expression of regret for the pain he has caused, which d hainch We had every right to eapect, his answer jg a further attack, a column long. EVidently I ex- pected too much —Yours truly, J. SHAW GREEN. Warrington, 17th January, 1902. Fire at Dallaan Farm. A fire whizh worked extensive damage broke out at Calland Farm, occupied by Mr Warburton, at Dallam otabout five o'clock on Tuesday morning. I Information was sent to the Fire lgine Station and Superintendent Turner and a complement of firemen turned out with ti' Major. On arrival at the farm the contents of a large corrugati slating of oats and compress flames. A good supply of wate the brigade were soon effective] The damage to stock amounts thrashing engine, valued at rendered useless. CADBURrS Cocoa is a pure, rear stimulating, and digestible. The sents the standard of highest purity admixtures, such as kola. malt, ho upon having CADBURY'S, As otb. substituted for extra prefit. Ia Pat'</p>
