      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>:141Y DEAR NELLIE, I recently saw two such charming hats. The first is of pastel blue silk, the brim lined with guipure, and bordered with a narrow band of sable, being laced through with soft blue silk ribbon, tied in a cluster of loops towards the back at the left side; a large white camelia with its glossy green leaves is placed on the top of the crown. The second hat is of white beaver, the upturned brim and the crown banded with broad bands of black whilst a spray of chrysanthemums is laid along the centre of the crown from the back. A very quaint hat was in cream silk tamel's hair cloth, trimmed with chrysanthemums, in rich brown and gold shades, arranged between the crown and the brim of the hat. The hat was caught at the back and turned over the crown, Where it was fastened with a smartly-tied ribbon bow across the back. A picture hat in the new black beaver cloth was trimmed with minor vel- vet, and handsome feathers, while a fascinating toque in ermine had the brim composed entirely of frills of black miroir velvet and white chiffon, the effe.ct being quite original and chic where it suited</p>
        <p>very graceful evening (hem was made of crepe-de-chine, soft silk, or thin nun's-veiling. The yoke, cuffs, and band that borders the skirt are in guipure of a creamy shade that is rather deep and pronounced, but without a tinge of yellow. The bow that is fastened on the left side is made of silk of any colour. It is carried across to, the right of the waistband, which is made of similar silk. The skirt is tucked knee-high above the band of lace. The bodice Is also tucked across. This dress would look very well in chiffon or silk muslin, whether white, ivory, or in a pale shade of any colour. Flower.painted satin is much used for smart evening dress. A white satin has the front panel painted with yellow roses and grey- green leaves. The overdress is pale yellow chiffon, yellow roses being painted or printed round the edges that fall from the waist to the feet, on either side of the central breadth. Another even- ing gown is made of the finest ivory . batiste, printed in a design of single pink peonies, most delicately pale. The lovely colours of the tinted gold jewellery, which is greatly developed in beauty since its first introduction at the end of 1901, seems to have set the keynote for the floral on muslins, chiffons, and batisteP.</p>
        <p>,attire is be:rig matte of lovely evening -gowns , dinner wear, and I have seen some beautiful specimens this week. A model gown was in pale blue brocade, the front panel of the skirt and the revers of the bodice exquisitely embroidered and trimmed with lace insertion and chiffon. It was, of course, low, and had very little in the way of sleeves. A charming creation was in embroidered net over pink satin, veiled and profusely trimmed wiih pink gauze and black ribbon velvet, the black velvet giving a pretty contrasting touch to the pink. It was very elaborate in make, with full train and elbow sleeves. A dainty evening gown for a young lady was of white silk, fringed with innumerable silk frills, edged with lace insertion and lace. The bodice was full, and a spray of Bowers across the left shoulder was the only other</p>
        <p>A dress intendei _ . Saddle with made- wiintdbooat wear is composed 41 1. M caracul. , simulated double skirt and trl. The bodice is made with ia shoulders, carried into a round collarrea‘ters on the The waistcoat is cream-coloured cloth enlthe.back. in colours. The cuffs are carried out to rotiqered saddle revers, and they button back on thenn the part of the sleeves, which are trimmed with a rde of the fur. The chemisette is pleated white laand over silk. The hat is caracul with folds of White 1 silk and a rosette of the same. Cloth and fur are the accepted materials for skating in. Velvet is worn by some of our best-performers, but it clings o to any other material that it is liable to catch In the gowns of passing skaters.</p>
        <p>pink</p>
        <p>Your loving JENNIE.</p>
        <p>Foreshore mud is to be sold to cement manufac- turers at a farthing a ton by the Yarmouth port Muthorities.</p>
        <p>VVednesday was the amniversary of the death of Queen Victoria, and a memorial servics,at which the King and Queen, the Ryal family, and a large number of the Royal Household and personal friends and attendants of the late Queen were present, was held at the Frogmore Mausoleum. Similar services, as well as services in celebration of the Accession of King Edward, were lm.ld in different parts of the country. Mr Agnes gift of £lO,OOO to the National Rifle Association is to bo spent in creating hundreds of rifle clubs in rural districts throughout the country.</p>
