      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Our late Queen did not like long sermons,end, accord- ing to the Rev Dr Tullock who used occasionally to preach before her late Majesty, at Balmoral, many ministers found their first summons to be their last, because of their offending in this way. Most of them when summoned te the Royal pulpit, for the first time, had devoted long hours to the preparation of their db.- coursie-and could not find it in themselves to" skip" their most eloquent passages, even though it had been intimated to thein, that brevity was one of the principal requisites in the Queen's estimate of what a sermon should be. The conseepeence was, that those who went on to undue length, were never asked again. A favourite sermon of Queen Alexandra's is that which was preached by Canon Fleming at Sandringham Church on Jenuery 24th 1892, after the death of the Duke of Clarence. It was published by Messrs Skeffington and Song, of Ticcadilly, London, and the rale of nig sermon on " Reneenition in Eternity " has resulted in a profit of over £1,500. All of this has of course hem devoted to charitable purposes, and the Gorden boys Flome,and the British Home for Incurables have both benelted from a;share in the profits realised last year. In Sax ma a petition was recently presented to Parlia- ment, to allow physicians in attendance upon incurable Invalids, to put an end to their sufferings, by the administration of poison. Even these racked by pain, and with no hope of recovery, usually cling to life, but there are some who are net afraid to meet their Pilot face to face, and often long to be released. It is not however permissible to ishorten human life. All we can do is to afford the utmost relief that medical science C 3,2 sureeest and for the reel: remember that as the Revised Version has it " What I do thou knowest not now, but thou shalt understand hereafter." Frances Ridley Havergal had learned this Person, and we can imagine with what horror she would have shrunk from a proposal like that presented to the Chamber in Saxony. The alarming increase of smallpox in London, from whence it seems likely to spread in all directions over the country makes ic important that more attention should be given to the cleansieg and disinfecting of railway carriages, traies,and omnibuses. Cattle trucks have to be ihnewashed after each journey, but for passengers, very little ever cecina to be done, to properly cleanse the carriages. Then, adding to the danger of infection, are the quits inadequate arrange- ments for ventilation in the construction of railway carriages, and the persistence with which the public object to open windows at this thne of year. Fresh air is the best preventive of infection as well as the most efficacious of nature'a remedies, but, in spite of all that ha o been written and preached on this tubjeret for years past, the average person still SEMIS to prefer reused windows, with the resalt that railway travelling is nine times out of ten accompanied by nauseous and un- healthy condition,. There is a general belief that it is best to patronise the smoking compartments when railway travelling in I times of epidemics, and many women boldly adopt this plan, in spite of its disagreeableness. They are of course generally looked upon as intruders, especially if I accompanied by children, who are apt to get into high spirits over the excitement of a railway journey. There is however, a certain amount of chivalry amongst men, who suffer thus in silence, whereas in a compartment with lady passengers, one hears under similar circum- stances that the railways ought to provide " Nursery carriages" and so on. fleece the smoking compart- ment is often invaded by intruders, and that tobacco is a safeguard against infection is a very old belief, is shriven by the fact that the gravediggers at the time ef the great plagues, were always enjoined to smoke while I at their work. Electrical railways may be a great improvement'as a I means of transit, but the prospect of the multiplication of" tube railways" in London, to the exclusion of other means of locomotion, fills some people with dis- may. There are many who cannot bring themeehres to travel on the underground " tubes." Apart from the foul air, and the sense of suffocation ms ny people and especially women are effected by the electricity just the same as they are in a thunderstorm, Constant travellers also find their watches affected by the electric current. Then there is the damper of fire, which is always pre- sent when powerful electric currents are in use,and the Utter impossibility of escape under such circumstances. Many people are therefore coming to the conclusion that they will have to stay at home when there is nothiny else but a network of "tubs railways, to travel through in London. Thickly jetted gowns have been little seen of late, but a great impetus has been given to this particular mode by the Queen, who appeared at the opening of Parlia rnent in a princess gown of black silk muslin covere with jet peillettes. Many of us have experienced t h difficulties in the way of rendering a black even in dress effective if all glitter is denied us, and will wet come eagerly its reinstatement in the world of fashion The Princess of Wales, too, has revived another charm log idea in the network of jet which formed the sleeves of her beautiful gown, and which ended at the elbows in frills of black chiffon. Nothing could be mere becom- ing to a good arm and the fashion will doubtless be widely patronised. Of the very overtrimmed skirts we are decidedly weary, especially the cloth variety which ft om band to hem is smothered with tucks, strappings of divers materials, traid,and various other forms, of ornamenta- tion (?) Reaction is sure to come and then we shall be vieing with one another, as to who STUMM us can wear well, the plainest of plain gowns. This state of affairs, applies less of course to rimming dresses, but even here reaction is apparent. Many leading modistes are build- ing gowns of chiffon, silk muslin, net, or crepe-de-chine, which fall sheer from waist to hem, uninterrupted by ruche or flounce of any description, and the effect cer- tainly make, for restralnees and good taste. Very tempting are the prices asked at the sales for istribrellas,en-tout-cas, sticks, and sunshades, which we should do well to secure. They are being cleared out at often ridiculous prices, to make room for" Corona- tion goods," and these the fastidious will not regard with great favour. The majority of the new handles will bear either the crown, sceptre, coronation chair, figures of a robed king and queen, ere, and will doubt- less be patronised extensively by the thousands of , Americans who are due here for the all-important cere- mony. For ourselves we prefer articles in better taste, and that do not date us. Toques are very large and wide at the moment, and it t invariably seems to be the very shortest women who wear the largest and widest headgear to be found. If they could see themselves as others see them they would for ever after choose their hate and toques in better proportion with their own dimensions. It might also occur to them to view the matter objectively and to reflect that it is a real inconvenience to passers by in frequented streets to find a large hat or toque jogging their shoulders- Weather prophets predict much severe weather to come, but the toques and hats in Course of preparation would lead one to suppose that summer, or at least late spring was close at baud. We are all very previous now-a-days.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>--- LATCHFORD V. TIMPERLEY "A." These teams met at Latchford on Saturday, and a very fast and vigorous game ensued. Tile visitors were the first to score but directly after Hallows ran down'the wing and put U a beautiful centre, which Spensley converted with an excellent shot. Just before the interval the visitors notched a Second point. After the restart the visitors bad the best of rotate S rs putting on three more goals. For Latchford. en leY and Banks were the pick of the forwards, whilst Wilk. ',ll"u in goal saved some good shots. The home defence "as hard goals so sound as usual. Final :—Timperley, Ave goaus ; Latchford, one. PLTXTON •• "V. LATCRFonD "A." This matt% *Was Flayed at Filaton on Saturday. The ground was not in the best ef conditions. making play rather heavy. The ving team had the best of the play, goals for Latchford were with the result Latchford. six goals •, Flixton, none. The and B Wilkinson. scored by IC Cliffo, William Smith, STOCItTON REATH v. SEFTON. Ihtsl match was played at Sefton on Saturday last, an soon equalised through Gandy who put the esxtocekllteenat Heath game being witnessed. Sefton seored first but finishing touch on a brilliant run by Owen. R Pierpoiat aided a second from a corner, and at half time the visitors were leading 2-1. In the second half Stockton Heath maintained their fine form and Pierpoint netted two more goals, both being splendid erterts. Final, Stockton Heath 4 goals; Sefton 1 goal. SToCirreN HEATH 2nd v. SEFTON 2,1 d. Played at Stoskton Heath on Saturday, and resulted in a draw clone goal each.</p>
