      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WRONGFUL DISMISSAL. s</head>
        <p>WRONGFUL DISMISSAL. s</p>
        <p>Heavy Damages Recovered from the</p>
        <p>'Erasmic Cornpany, Limited.</p>
        <p>Action in the King's Bench Division.</p>
        <p>In the King's Bench Division of the High Court of Justice, London' on Monday, the case of Bernhard v. the Brasmio Co., Limited, came before the Lord Chief Justice and a special jury. It was an action by the plaintiff, Edgar Hugh Bernhard, now of London, to re- cover from the defendants, who carry on business at the Bank Quay Soap Works,Warrington, damages far wrongful dismissal. The defendants pleaded justifica- tion. Mr Gill, K.C., and Mr Lush appeared for the plaintiff. Sir Edward Clarke and Mr Shearman were fir the defence. . .</p>
        <p>Mr Lush having opened the pleadings, Sir Edward Clarke said : My Lord,—it will, I hope, be saving the time of the Court if I say at once that there is no question of justification here. I don't justify. The only question will be one of damages.. Mr : My friend has taken the only course open to him by making this statement now, at the very latest minute that it was possible to make rt.. But, though the only question is to be that of damages,it is necessary .in order that you may have same material to form a judgment upon as to the proper amount of damages that I should give you a brief history of the case and of the relations between the plaintiff and the defendants. The Care is one in which I submit that you ought to give as large a sum of damages as you can, having regard to the position which the plaintiff occupies. I hope you will see fit Co give him the last shilling possible because of his treatment by the defendants, or by one of the defendants, for whom the company is responsible —the chairman of the company—has been about as bad as it was possible for it to be. It would be difficult to exaggerate the conduct of that individual,whe appeared te have been able to carry with him the other directors of the company. The plaintiff is at the present time about 29 years of age, and he was educated for business at King's College, London. The Lord Chief Justice . Is it possible for the defen- dants to go further than Sir Edward Clarke has already gone? f,This question led to 'a consultation between counsel and solicitors on both sides, which resulted in a settle- ment of the action. _</p>
        <p>Sir Edward Clarke, in announcing the settlement, said understand my learned friends desire, in justice to the plaintiff, to have this matter investigated in court, so that it might be publicly shown that the Plaintiff had not be guilty of any conduct which could justify his summary dismissal from the position which he held in the employ of the defendant company. I am bound to say, and my clients, the directors of the company, ace bound in honour to say, that they are not in a position to find any justification for any statements injurious to the plaintiff which were made and which lcd to his dis - missal. The statements set out in the pleadings in justi- ficatien are not very injurious to the plaintiff's character they merely state that he neglected his duties, and did not devote the whole of his timo to the service of the company, and such like; and they contain no imputa- tions upon his character. Undoubtedly, however, there are circumstances connected with the severance of the plaintiff from the company, which render it necessary lin the plaintiff's interests that a verdict for a suestan- tial amount should be entered In his favour, and then- fore I am authorizsd be my clients to consent to a verdict for the plaintiff for £2,250. in satisfaction of damages and costs, in addition to £44 into court. Mr Gill : I think I ought to say that the real case I here was not upon the pleadings at all. The plaintiff 1 was dismissed upon information that he had accepted some commission inconsistent with the interests of the defendant company, but there was not the slightest 'foundation for such a charge. Circulars were,however, sent round by the defendants stating that the plaintiff had been summarily dismissed, which statements would of course, if unexplained, be disastrous to the character of tun plaintiff. It was therefore necessary that the plaintiff should come into court, not for the ' purpose of obtaining money, but to clear his character from any imputation which the conduct of the de fendant company had cast upon it. The statement- however, which has been made by Sir Edward Clarke, and the substantial nature of the ver. diet agreed to by the defendants, are sufficient to attain the object of the plaintiff, and I accordingly agree to the settlement indicated by my friend Sir Edward Clarke. The Lord Chief Justice ; I think this is a very proper settlement. This is one of those cases in which long experience has convinced me that parties act wisely in accepting the advice of the counsel when there is no real justification for the statements or acts of a defendant ; and hero there is not a shadow of evidence to justify any charges brought against the plaintiff. His Lordship then entered judement for the plaintiff for £2,250, in addition to £44 paid into court, to include costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>ACHING BACKS.</head>
        <p>ACHING BACKS.</p>
        <p>Lam. aching backs, that make stooping or any move- ment of the body a torment, and render hundreds of men and women unfit for work, are not cured by em- brocation, linaments, belts, sr local applications. They do not strike at the cause of the pain, and you must go further. Dodd's Kidney Pills cure aching backs because they do go further ; because they go as far as the cause of the weakness—the kidneys. First, however, read what Mr G Bentley, of 3, Lees Walk, Walton-street, Hull, says : " For some time I suffered a lot with pales in my hack and thought I was never going to get better. 1 was at home from work for some time and nay doctor told tlle it was a family complaint. I tried several remedies, but nothing did any goodond I did not know what to do. I started to wear a belt, but that did not cure me." Finally, Mr Bentley heard of Hasid's Kidney Pills and gave them a trial, and here is the result in his own words : " Bsfore I bad taken two boxes, the pain left me. I felt a new man, and when I had taken tour boxes I felt as strong as ever. They have been a God send to me and saved me a lot of pain." This is Mr. Bentley's experience. What is yours? What was wrong with Mr Bentley was his kidneys; hie backache was only a symptom. What Mr Bentley needed was not a belt or a linemen: for the back, but a medi- cine for his unhealthy kidneye: He found the remedy in Dodd's Kidney Pills, which did cot only Jure the , backache, but cured the cause of the backache, the kid- neys. Once this is done, disease in a hundred forms is I banished from the system. If you have pain in any form rheumatism, sciatica, aching back, neuralgia or cramp, see to the kidneys, for they are causing all the trouble sad they need prompt attention. Dodd's Kidney Pills have made thousands .of men and women strong, by curing the cause of their weakness—the kidneys. Dodd's Kidney Pills are 25 91 per box, of all chemists, or post free en receipt of price from The Dodds Medicine Co., 23 Farringdoa-avenue, London, B.C. Be sure of the name, D-o-d-d-'s.</p>
        <p>In a case heard at Suffolk Assizls in which a xvoman named MKillen a native of Liverpool, was sentenced to fifteen months' imprisonment with hard labour for theft and fraud, it transpired that she had been mas- querading as Princess Soltykoff, and that during her remarkable career she wont under several aliases.</p>
      </div>
