      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WITHOUT INTIROD-6CTIONI</head>
        <p>WITHOUT INTIROD-6CTIONI</p>
        <p>HALLI WELL SUTCLIFFE (Author of " The Eleventh Commandment," ac.)</p>
        <p>PART I.</p>
        <p>" Ah !" said Oswald Wynne_, on the first morning of his arrival at Traviston. He had risen an hour after sunrise, and was sitting on the river-bank. He was innocent of clothing, and the marks a a recent bath were lapon him. _ _ _</p>
        <p>" Ah," reiieated Oswald Wynne, at the end of five minutes. There was yet greater fervour than before in that useful monosyllable for he had lit a mighty briar, and had stretched himself on the warm sand, naked and unashamed.</p>
        <p>A red ant came to investigate matters presently; he was followed by a goodly band of comrades, who one and all set about climbing the great white mountain which confronted them. And the great white mountain was stirred to its foundations, and spoke with strange tongues and thereafter disap- peared beneath the waves. This added the spice of a gentle moral to the Pagan irresponsibility of the morning; it chewed how much a union of tiny en- deavours can effect. The great white mountain rose to the surface.again, puffing and blowing: it sought the bank, and plunged hastily into a it of flannels that lay on a rock hard by, and moved off into the woods.</p>
        <p>"Nothing wanting: upon my soul, nothing want- ing," cried Oswald Wynne' as he returned for a well-earned breakfast. " Thank heaven I'm a landscape painter ; Nature's so much better at the floral than the human; and flowers don't talk while you're at work which is considerate of them.— Good morning, Mrs. Pinder. Breakfast ready?" „</p>
        <p>"It's just 'going in, sir." "Has the Major got down yet ?" " Yes, sir, not five minutes ago." " Ah—lticky I'm in such excellent time. The old boy does so hate one to be late for meals," mur- mured Wynne, as he strolled into the breakfast. room. "Good morning, uncle; you seem to grow your weather for show here, like your cattle and " H'm—passable—not cold enough to freeze the marrow in one's bones, which is the most one can ex- pect of this confounded climate," growled Major Fordham, seating himself in front of an omelette— a sleek, delicately-browned affair that did credit to the Major's cook. The nephew laughed impertinently into the uncle's face. What is your notion of a decent climate, if one may ask, sir ? India you say is worse than—" "So it is, worse than Tophet. When you get to my age, boy, you will realise that all climates are confounded. If a place isn't too cold, it plays games with your liver, and if it's neither too hot nor too cold, it is lukewarm; heaven preserve me from lukewarmness."</p>
        <p>Exactly: so hadn't we better negotiate that omelette? There's a tide in the affairs of omelettes which—and, really, the one before you looks too good to be lost—thanks, uncle, don't give me quite all the contents of the dish."</p>
        <p>Drop that mock modesty; I won't have it, I *ell you—though your appetite is a thing to be as- hamed of. Yes, it is—indecent, boy : I believe you came down here just to insult me • you've no right to look so abominably healthy. What have you been doing this morning? Mooning after dawn effects, I'll be bound."</p>
        <p>"Yes, partly. Then I had a swim, and got stung by a nest of red ants ; then I went for a stroll again, and thanked Heaven that there were no women about this snug little place of yours." The Major grunted approvingly. "Quite right, Oswald, quite right. I wish other young men had half your sense in the matter of petticoats. But you'll go under some day, like the rest of them." "Not likely, sir. You have shown me the rose- coloured side of freedom too well to admit of—" _</p>
        <p>"Rose-coloured, did you say? You dare to talk of rose-colour to a man with a liver—still, you're fairly sensible, after all. No dictation, no interfer- ence--why the deuce do men get married?" "Give it up, uncle. Let me help you to some bacon." This was on Tuesday. Towards the evening of Thursday Oswald Wynne grew conscious of a void. Before Friday was half through, the sense of some- thing wanting had grown almost painful. He wor- ried about it a good deal, as people will worry about absurd conundrums: tobacco he had in plenty, and the avuncular hospitality was profligate of cups that cheered and that might, on occasion, inebriate; his picture was getting on satisfactorily the repose of the little village was a new and altogether delightful experience: what on earth was that troublesome wanting factor? On the morning of Saturday Oswald Wynne was enlightened. He had finished a long spell of work fand. was sauntering homeward .through the dingle that lay between the river and the village high road, when he became aware of a something occupying the three-barred stile ahead of him.</p>
        <p>The thing was a mixture of yellow and white—a gauzy, impalpable coolness, it seemed from a dis- tance. Oswald Wynne paused, and felt unaccount- ably relieved. " Perhaps," be murmured, "the missing item is just—" Then he went on again. The yellow, as he drew nearer, proved to be the back of a pretty head; the white was a mass of billowy muslin. The artist finished his sentence---" just a woman," he said, and smiled contentedly. The occupant of the stile was deep in the perusal of a green-backed novel ; her back was turned to Wynne, and she did not hear his step until he was almost at her elbow. She Hushed a little, slid gracefully to earth, and waited for him to pass. "Thank you," he observed. These Bohemians are always so original. " Not at all," she responded, with the artless cleverness for which our country maidens are rel nowned. Then the artist went home to lunoh. " There's a stranger in the village, uncle," he oh. served casually later in the day. The old man glanced sharply at him. "Sex ?" he aueried. "'The other."</p>
        <p>" I guessed as much. Pretty ?" "A poem." " H'mph. Then I shall not have much of your society for the next few weeks, I presume. Pity, boy, a great pity." "But, look here, uncle' I don't even know her." "You will do soon. Then you'll paint her, and spoil an otherwise good landscape. Then you'll marry her.. Then—'</p>
        <p>"Aren't you going just a trifle fast, sir? Of course if you can contrive an introduction—" "Not I, not I, young man. I have no intention of being an accessory before the fact of' any criminal undertaking. What do you propose to do F" " Smoke another of your cheroots." "You'd better smoke the box full. She may ob- ject, you see, in a month or two." " The old boy is fairly right, after all," mused Oswald Wynne, as he went upstairs to dress for dinner. "I would give a great deal to see that yellow-headed poem again. She might talk every bit of the time she was sitting to me, too • one couldn't mind a voice like that, whatever it cared to The Major was apt to grow more genial during after-dinner hours •, it is a habit of old gentlemen who can afford to keep a good cellar. " Oswald, my boy," he said a few evenings later, drawing his chair closer to his nephew's, " it was the best idea you ever had in your life—to come down here and brighten up the place for an old fel- low like myself." " The jolliness is mutual, uncle. I can't remem- ber anything left to want, and that argues contact- ment."</p>
        <p>" Absolutely nothiug, boy F" " Well—er—"</p>
        <p>"Precisely. Now I have a hazy notion that I was once young myself—l won't swear to it mind, and you are at liberty to disbelieve it if you like. But I seem distinctly to remember a curious sensa- tion in the spine as connected with the flutter of skirts."</p>
        <p>"Yes, uncle? To put it crudely, you were just about as fond of women as they were fond of you ?" The Major frowned his approbation of the stealthy compliment. The Major crossed his left leg over his right, and lit a fresh Havannah, and chuckled at some resurrected episode. The nephew walked wari- ly, and led the old man on to recount several little stories with an Indian flavour about them.</p>
        <p>"But that Cs7;:s-Ton-pT;i-0,--1);i,;;--1; finished ; "don't go and dolikewise, or my experience will all pass for nothing, and I hate to see a thing wasted. Women are worse than weather a good deal; take my word for it. Miss Penderby cw—"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
