      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>9 V 4, – .• – -•%;;!i ,:k., , ". ~,., A V 5‘,1.4. .At —': •All,;i aA, , ~. ... 4' .11., a0k,,,, ..., • la</head>
        <p>9 V 4, - .• - -•%;;!i ,:k., , ". ~,., A V 5‘,1.4. .At —': •All,;i aA, , ~. ... 4' .11., a0k,,,, ..., • la</p>
        <p>• it, 061 1,1 pr, 1 1!, .'' '''l ' A Ogliti '4. .0 • ..11,0,,E4 '''' . ilk .:i ', At le</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>"Who is Miss Penderby, uncle 2" "Letitia Penderby, sir, is a lady who conspicuOutt- ly failed to value my friendship; she lives at the other end of the village, sir, and 1 recognise the hand of Providence in the placing of a clear half mile be- tween our abodes ; she possesses every femininei weakness, sir, and enjoys a mental incapacity ~reater., than falls to the lot of even the majority of her sex.. She dresses in a middle-of-the-century costume of a. peacock-blue colour, and cloaks wrongheadedness under the guise of eccentricity. That is Letitia, Pend,,rbv." _ _ - -</p>
        <p>a-SO-3-Fon knew her once 7" Oswald Wynne had grown oddly interested in his uncle's description of Letitia Penderby. "Knew her? Yes, when she was a toddling imp of five. Grew up with her; thought I was—but never mind what follies I thought at the age of twenty." The nephew mixed the uncle a liberal glom of toddy. "You were saying, sir ?" he murmured pre- sently. "Well, if you will have it, I might as well make a clean breast and have done with it. Just before I went out to India, there was a tender scene--you</p>
        <p>know the variety—with a trysting-tree and honey- suckle and all the rest of it; I thought I meant a deuce of a lot, and set sail for India with a fine feel- ing of broken-hearted luxury; used to write square miles of note paper, and tried my hand at amateur poetry. Did all the usual things—yes, by Jove, every one, when I come to rake up the past." "And then—some misunderstanding ?" "Just so ; if lever allowed myself to admit a mistake, I should tell you I was in the wrong. But I never do admit such a thine-, sir. It was all the fault of that confounded climate out there." "But had you no opportunities of patching it up again ?" " Not until it was too late. Her people were , against the match from the start, and they took par- ticular care that she should be away when I came home on furlough. Then I learned bachelor wis- dom—sound common sense, young man, such as you may attain to when you've served your time at the feminine treadmill' and when I came back here for good, I had settled myself very comfortably into my groove. Miss Letitia was still living at the old house, though her people had died long before ; it was a trifle awkward at first, but I was gradually deceived into thinking her a highly intelligent woman, and we struck up a somewhat intimate friend- ship. The old, disturbing passion was gone, of course, and we derived a sane, level-heaed satis- faction from each other's company." " Of course, uncle," put in Oswald Wynne, with the suspicion of a smile. " Don't you hurl innuendoes at me, sir ! I tell you, we were just friends—just friends. Why, it was an absurdity to think of anything—anything tender, you know—between an old fellow of sixty and a•—" "A lady who bore her years with equal ease. Was it so sera absurd, do you think ?" " Quite absurd. But she played a good hand at whist, and I got into the way of dropping in for a quiet rubber of double dummy in the evening." "Yes. sir ?" prompted the nephew, as the Major seemed disinclined to proceed. " I know to what your interest is due, you young rascal," chuckled the old man. "You expect me to describe how chilly the thought of home grew with- out a woman's hands to put a finishing touch to things? You are waiting to picture me on my knees before Miss Letitia, and you intend to laugh at the spectacle of an antique gander saying Bo' to an elderly goose ? Oh, yes, I Imow you young men, know you thoroughly. That was my motive in telling you the story—l led you to expect all the funny situations, just to disappoint you with the climax. We quarrelled; kindly understand that, " Yes, but that was merely an accident. As you were saying, uncle, home must have grown to seem rather an empty affair. Then, by degrees, it dawned on you that bachelor habits might to a large extent be maintained, with feminine comforts added. You re- membered how prettily—pardon me—Miss Letitia used to kiss you in the old days; little tendernesses, which you thought you had buried peaceably, sat up in their graves and talked to you. Oh, yes, I am old enough to understand all that perfectly, though I could not put it as well as you did just now." The Major stared aghast at his nephew. "Con- found you, boy—confound you, I say !" he sput- tered at last. " I said no such thing. I—l only thought it—that is—egad, sir, you would try a saint's patience. We don't speak to each other we haven't spoken for these twelve months past , tenderness, forsooth !" The nephew smoked on contentedly until. rti Major's purple had faded once more to a smoulder, ing red,. Then, "May I ask how the quarrel alma</p>
        <p>, about ?" "Curry, just curry. Double dummy, yoo xv. is not at its best unless a good dinner has prer-Merii: it: Miss Letitia had 4 rezaarl;ably good cook</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>His Cough was Consumption.</head>
        <p>His Cough was Consumption.</p>
        <p>MR. MATTHEW PEST, Of 27, Wellington-Street, Southwick, Sunderland, has experienced a re- markable rescue from a premature death. To a Sunderland Herald reporter he said : •• I had not been strong for some years. I noticed a cough at first, and then that my strength was slowly but surely failing me. Often I could not sleep at night, and profuse perspirations caused me great distress. I was soon unable to go about at all, for I frequently felt so weak and giddy that if I had not sat down I would certainly have fallen down. When I did take a little food it made me sick, and I could not retain it. I consulted a doctor, and took a great deal of medicine, but it did not seem to make me any better or stronger. The climax came when I was refused ad- mission at a leading local hospital, being told that I had consumption, and was not likely to . _</p>
        <p>get better. You can judge that I was truly alarmed. However, now lam following my work, and am all right in health, which is en- tirely due to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. My appetite is better now thafi it has been for years, and, what is more, I thoroughly enjoy my food. All the symptoms I have _ described have practically disappeared, and I am altogether a different man. A relative of mine had symptoms similar to mine, and was cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. He then recommended them to me. I felt an improvement after taking them for a week, and I am now, as you see, fit and well. The wasting was stopped; I began to eat better, and put on flesh, and was soon strong enough to go back to work. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills have done for me what other medicine could not."</p>
        <p>dappenea, very occasiouraiy, razz 7. afternoon call until Miss Letitia's ridiculously early dinner-hour surprised me; it seemed absurd to go home just for dinner, to return afterwards, so I used to stay on such occasions. A very fine cook, indeed, but she knew nothing about curry; how many women do in this benighted country? Now you may trust a woman, Oswald, to pride herself, every time, on the one quality she hasn't the ghost of a. chance of possessing : if she is pretty, she gets into her head that cleverness is her strong point : if she's clever she fancies herself a beauty ' "And suppose she happens to be both ?" " Then, sir, she imagines that Heaven is her forte, and dies young; that explains why you never come across the combination," chuckled the Major. " But that is by the way. Miss Letitia, you must know, when I began to drop in rather frequently, took it into her head that she would make me some curry with her own hands. This is a painful stage in any story, and one which makes sue yellow to this day when I think of it. She insisted on my taking double helps—she did, by Jove--and because I kept my face in order for politeness' sake while eating it, she conceived that her genius lay in the direction of curry." "Poor old uncle! What did it taste like?" "Taste like? Ours is a poverty-stricken 'ling-lingo at best, but, well, never mind. I began to dine le•R fiequently at Linden Cottage, lest discretion shoull be merged in candour. You know what a good- tempered fellow I am, but my liver works independ- ently sometimes, and people are apt to think it is I who am talking. One afternoon, though, I was fool enough to stay on in the old way; I had vague hopes that the monstrosity which walked abroad in the name of curry would not appear, and I forgot the havoc that a week of east winds had wrought with my constitution." "And you made a few remarks on the subject at dinner-time ?" prompted the other. "I did. Miss Letitia had given me enough the first time to poison a whole regiment ' • when she pressed me to pass my plate for a second dose, I de- clined, courteously enough at first. She pouted like a girl, and said what a pity it was I let the one thing in the world for which she had a talent go practically untested: she -wheedled, and coaxed, and caressed me to undergo another martyrdom' but I was not in a fit state to die, and I refused point- blank. Truth to tell, I believe that the east wind had touched us both up a bit, for Miss Letitia got quite raspy about it. They have spoiled your palate over in India, I can see, Major,' she chir- ruped, bridling her neck; you have eaten those barbarous native dishes for so many years that you cannot appreciate true curry when you taste it.' I lost my temper a little—a very little—at that ; after I had suffered and been strong so often, after all I had compelled my palate to undergo, this was too much! 'Madam!' I cried, thumping my fist on the table so that the dishes chattered—l have said. that I was a little angry—' Madam, I have every respect for your needlework, for your management of feathered animals, for your manipulation of a gar- den' but let us understand, once for all, that you will never master even the rudiments of curry." I think it very good, and you are forgetting yourself, Major,' she responded tartly. Then—well, then, that confounded east wind had its say. I can forget myself more easily than that misnamed dish of yours; I know they sell a mixture to you in a bottle, and call it curry, but is that any reason why you should fail to boil the rice sufficiently ? It may be magnificent, Madam, but it is not curry.'' " ll'm ; a trifle impetuous of you, uncle ?" corn. naented Oswald Wynne, diplomatically. "Perhaps it was—yes, I believe it was. No gen. lemanly liver would have said such a thing, but how can /help it? Miss Letitia grew very frigid, and rose from the table, and I went for my hat. She had the last word, of course. It seems a great pity, Major' that so slight a thing as a spoiled palate should ruin a pleasant friendship.' I was cool enough to swear before I reached home, though, so it is clear that 1 should have been ready to patch the matter up, if only she haa-ziven me a chance. .Ent I had touched her on her most sensitive spot, and she cut me dead the next time we met. From that day we have never spoken. I occasionally surprise a stealthy glance during church-time, which shows that she takes more interest in the matter than 1,, butsf course I can make no step towards reconeilia- --- " It does seem a pity, as Miss Letitia observed," . said Oswald Wynne reflectively. He was smiling behind his moustache at the note of regret in the out gentleman's voice; he sounded almost wistful at times. "Just like a woman. No proportion, no balance. Would you find a man, now, spoiling a friendship like that just on account of a wretched dish of curry? Humph, humph; no sense of fitness what- ' ever." " Would a discreet mediator be likely to be of any service, uncle? Surely it is time you both cried pax ' about tlurt cause of dispute; you mist miss these sluiet rubbers dreadfully-"</p>
      </div>
