      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Girl's Neglected Cough.</head>
        <p>A Girl's Neglected Cough.</p>
        <p>THE case of Miss Rosa Chard, a young lady of nineteen, living at Crook-street, Ilminster, Somerset, is a lesson to her sex.</p>
        <p>"Eighteen months ago," she stated to a Chard News reporter, "1 was taken with an illness caused, I believe, by standing about in damp clothes and boots. I began to lose all energy, and became very thin. I had no appetite for meals, was taken with frequent sickness, and often felt faint. At last I went to a doctor, who gave me medicine, but in spite of his skill I felt worse, and I was obliged to give up altogether and rest at home. I had a very bad cough, which troubled me terribly, and my face was so puffed that I could scarcely see. The weakness grew on me so that I had to take to my bed, despairing almost of what would be the end of it. One day the postman brought a book about Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, which I had often read about in the newspapers, and I wrote to the company telling them all my symptoms, and asking whether they thought the pills would help me. They advised me to try them. I purchased a box of the pills and commenced to take them: en about a week's time I seemed to get better, and that decided me to go on with the pills. The improvement continued; I could take my food, and my strength came back to me." _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Yes," said the mother, "I began to think that Rose's health was ruined for life, but she soon got quite jolly again—just as you see her now.'</p>
        <p>The pressman could see that Miss Chard was in the best of health and good spirits. "Itookfourboxesofthepillsaltogether," said Miss Chard, 'and then I was as well as ever."</p>
        <p>Ncit-a-at, fibt a-bit: - rhope I have not readVe% my present age without finding ample orectifig4kOit for my leisure ; I am sure I never feel in the Wit lou.el2_-_ca,--or short of interests-" "Still, it wo=a-ake--iiiii little to—" "Boy, what are you at F" queried the Major sharply. " Young men are too casual now-a-days to be over-eager to rush into peacemaking ines,..3es. You have something behind yourconfounded appear- ance of good-will, I'll be bound." The nephew reddened slightly. "Er—not at all. uncle—your happiness—" "There is a useful body called the horse-marines, Oswald; it is their business to listen to all polite- fictions; would you mind reserving them until— have you taken to portrait-painting yet, by the way P" The other reddened still more. he stammered.</p>
        <p>" Not—not yet," ,</p>
        <p>" I see it ! I see your game !" cried the Major, delighted at his own astuteness. "The :poem you told me of is staying with Miss Letitia. The poem is living riotously on curry every day ; the poem stands in danger of becoming prose, and dead prose at that, in a very brief space.', "My dear uncle, nothing is further—that is—you see—"</p>
        <p>" Of course I do. If I haven't hit the mark why do you gro iv peony red, and boggle your words, and look a consummate young ass Eh, sir? Now, come, make a clean breast of it; no use exercising guile on me; you're a fool to the gentle Hindu in that department." " Well, frankly—she is 'staying at Linden Cot- tage. 1 was passing the gate this morning, and she came out with a little old lady." "With a huge white sun-umbrella, and a pee- cock-blue dress P.' _ _</p>
        <p>"Yes, I recognised Bliss Letitia from your des- cription just now, and—well, couldn't you, uncle ? You have no idea how adorable she is."</p>
        <p>"Which of them? Have you changed your al- legiance from the poem? Is Miss Letitia the present object of your regard ?" "Yes, indirectly. I want my passport." "So you played on an old man's feelings, did you, puppy? You pulled out the melodramatic stop, and babbled about the touch of a woman's band, and the delights of a fireside; you were minded to go on to Home, sweet Home,' and My Sweetheart 'When a Boy,' it I had let you? You did all this just to secure an introduction to a flimsy bit of girlhood who will probably have the good sense to throw you overboard." "I should like to give her the option, sir," ob- served the nephew whose wonted self-possession had returned. "Can I mix you another glass ?" "You may, though you don't deserve the privi- lege. What a pity, boy, that I tun less innocent than I look. You worked that little game of your" prettily enough." "I'm glad you think so- Then you think that marry matter Gan be righted? How would it be if you dropped in at the Cottage to-morrow, and ex- plained— " /s/o. sir, a hundred times no! Women have no logic. Miss Letitia Penderby would never be in- duced to see that she was absurdly in the wrong. I have washrl my hands of the_sex. ' 4.-ttm</p>
        <p>be Concluded:l</p>
      </div>
