      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S re- Mr. Chamberlain's Answer to Chesterfield ply to Lord ROSEBERY should do something to rally the Liberal party. He can hardly expect that his honeyed words for the Liberal Imperialists in the Commons will win their favour when he accuses Lord ROSEBERY of suggesting "extremely bad diplomacy" in the Chesterfield utterance. It is rather curious, too, that the Colonial Secretary should seek to trounce the members of the Liberal party because they are unable to exhibit the har- mony of an orchestra when his own political heart- strings have in the course of his career vibrated to so many different tunes. The Liberal party has never professed to have the rigid consistency which is to be found, for example, in the Primrose League. Liberals are frank in acknowledging their differences, but they recog- nize that the differences as to the origin of the war that separate them from each other are as nothing to the gulf that yawns between thern and the Government position. If any Liberals had not realized this before, Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S speech last evening helped them to do so. Mr CAWLEY'S amendment to the Address is admittedly in the nature of a compromise, but it shows adherence to a broad indictment. The complaint against the Ministry is that it cannot make either peace or war, and meanwhile the resources of the country are being exhausted, our interests in various parts of the world are being jeopardised, and the seeds of bitterness which follow on a pro- longed struggle in arms are being sown in South. Africa. By the flux of time and the immeasurable superiority of our military strength the war- making problem seems to be nearing solution. The Government can hardly have the audacity to claim credit for that after Sir Charles DILKB7S exposure of their slackness in reinforcing our troops in sending reliefs. The important matter before the country to be determined is the nature of the sattlement. Mr BRODRICK repeatedly informs the country that great progress has been made in wearing down the enemy, and he has gone so far as to give figures to prove that the Boers themselves must know that their resources now are exceedingly limited, that their prospects are hopeless, and that all they can do is to carry on an irritating guerilla warfare. As soon, how- ever, as this statement is taken seriously as it was by the amendment moved by Mr Cavanv last night the Government professes alarm. The argument of Mr CAwLEy and Mr RECaNALD MC' KENNA was that as the Boers are practically neaten we might let them know informally that, if they are dispesed to sue for terms, the negotia- tions will be conducted on our part in a generous spirit as victors who can afford to be magnanim- ous. That suggestion, however, is regarded bY Ministers in such a manner that we are almost led to think the campaign has not really been so effective as the Government represent, and i lead country s being bluffed by a policy of optimism.</p>
        <p>that.</p>
        <p>The contention of the Liberals is that if the enemy's forces have been reduced to comparative impotency and despair it would be well to encour- age the leaders to submit themselves to a volute tary settlement, and so obtain their bond that they themselves would do their best to maintaia' its terms. This is a sound and wise policy, an ' 'lO</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>our strength no longer needs to be asserted. 01 annexation of the two States must be accepted g</head>
        <p>our strength no longer needs to be asserted. 01 annexation of the two States must be accepted g</p>
