      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <p>tunity arises. This is the idea which underlies Lord RosEn ERY'S suggestion of the wayside meeting, and it must be remembered that, although our pre-eminence in the field may now ne deemed to have been pretty clearly asserted there are outside elements which make it desirable to end this matter with as little friction as is consistent w; th a firm declaration of our strength. The Boer prisoners have to be I reckoned with, and there is the difficulty of getting English people to settle in the agricultural dis- tricts of the two States. What is Mr CHAMBER- LAINS answer. At a time when we are told we have to all intents triumphed, he draws back the terms offered by Lord Kr TCHENER to General BOTHA, and condemns Lord ROSEBERY'S assump- tion that they are still open a s extremely bad diplomacy. He conveys the idea that so far from these terms having been offered from a laudable desire by the Government to encourage a good feeling among the enemy, now we can make them more severe we will do so. Absit omen ! After that who can be surprised that the Boers hesitate about unconditional surrender, guarded only by Mr. CHAMBERLAINS indefinite assurance that political rights will be granted when the Govern- ment thinks them consistent with the safety of the country ?</p>
        <p>BOTH the American and Prince Henry's Visit German Press just now to America. are devoting most of their attention to the approaching visit of Prince HENRY of PRUSSIA to the United States. It is rather amusing to find that the German EMPEROR has had to moat the same sort of idle gossip in regard to the journey of his brother that our own KING has suffered in silence as to the forthcoming presence of the PRINCE of WALES in Berlin. It is said in New York that the KAISER, who there has the sobriquet of " the best commercial traveller "in the world, has thrust his representa- tive on the attention of White House. Seeing how coolly Some of the German newspapers have been hinting that the PRINCE of WALES is going on his mission without being asked, the annoy- ance they manifest at the suggestion is entertain- •na It was,of course, impossible that the KAISER should ask the PRIN CE Of WALES to come to Ber- lin to pay him a camp liment, and when the initia- tive was takaa at Marlborough Hmse the KAISER expressed his warm satisfact ion that the British Heir-Apparent would j 3in his circle on the festival occasion. In our own case the idea eman- ated from King EDWARD ; in the case of Vie Royal visit to America it emanated from the KUSER. There need be no secret of the fact ; but the inter- est lies in the development of the American mission.</p>
        <p>It may sound very vulgar, but when one reads the account of the elaborate arrangements being made by the United States Government for the reception'of Prince HENRY when the steamship, the Kron Prinz, reaches the place with the awful name of "Tompkinsville," it is impossible not to say "high jinks." It is all vary wall for the Ameri- cans who will not participate in the function to sneer at it, much as people do at the champagne luncheon before a land sale t but American society is dazzled by the prospect of the Royal function. Prince HENRY is to be accompanied by General von PLESSEN, by Vice-Admiral von TIRPITZ, Sec- retary for the Navy, by Captain von MUELLER, by Captain von Guotorn, Naval Aids-de-Camp to the EAPEROR, by Captain von TROTIIA., and by the Court Marshal,Baron von SECKENDORFF. Every one of the six gentlemen, it will be observed, has the proud prefix of" von," which is claimed to be a sign of good bitth, and One may gather that the flowers of the Garman nobility will be, figuratively speaking, spread at the feet of Miss ROOSEVELT when she christens the EAIPEROR'S yacht, and the E.NIPEROR'S own Court Marshal will be there to see that they are distributed gracefully. To least that the Americans who are being so honoured can do In return is to arrange a great naval demonstration.</p>
        <p>Cut bow? It is beyond dispute that America and Germany must be keen commercial rivals, and that every year their compatition will get sharper. The friction which this will unconsciously cause will make anything like an alliance imposs- ible; and the Americans, who, as we know, have had rather a trying experience in the Philippines, are not so eager to mix in the welt politik as they were. It is tolerably certain, therefore, that the KAismt cherishes no illusion as to obtaining their active support in the years to come. There is not the slightest prospect of that. What, however, the KAISER does see—and, as a business man, safeguarding the interests of the Fatherland, he is not to be blamed for taking the view—is that the Anglo-American sentiment may b 3 checked by fostering some German sympathies among the American people. The nation across the Atlantic has a large leaven of German blood. No one can say when the interests of Great Britain and Ger- many may clash in diplomacy. It is the sincere desire of the EMPEROR to preserve peace, we believe, not only with Great Britain, but among the European Powers; and he has a lurking fondness for England, the birth-country of his mother. Bat he holds to the axiom that the surest way to secure peace is to prepare for war, and that is not done only by accumulat- nag armaments. The business of PRINCE HENRY is to provide a counter-current to the stream of Anglo-American oratory.</p>
        <p>Tuesday completed a year since the accession of King Edward, and it is pleasant to reflect how smoothly the duties of Sovereign have been dis- charged in the period, and the good omen which the/ otter for a useful and constitutional reign. The mourning of the Court compelled the King to pass the earlier days in comparative retirement, but in the autumn it was possible for him to indicate more clearly the course he intends to take. There will be no slackening of the rules of ceremony so far as all the State functions are concerned, and the regulating of presentations will be more closely controlled by the Sovereign than was possible in the later years of Queen Victoria. On the other hand the King intends to cherish the rights, which he enjoys with all his subjects, of a private citizen. A small I brougham frequently conveys him through the West End, and he hopes that it will excite little observation and less remark. The quite country life at Sandringham is also to be maintained for part of the year, and the country gentleman's avocations, such as a visit to Smith- field Cattle Show, will not apparently be slighted. The King's influence as a Constitutional Monarch may not be more apparent than was that of Queen Victoria, but, as in her case, it will have considerable potency. The eulogy of Lord Salis- bury comes to mind. A Minister, he said, felt a grave responsibility when he advised her Majesty to modify her opinion. King Edward will have greater opportunity of diplomatic service for the nation of which he is the head. His proposal that he Prince of Wales should attend the birthday</p>
        <p>festivities of the Kaiser comes within that cate- gory. Again, it is evident that he will in home affairs interest himself in the movements to alle- viate human suffering. The strength of that sympathy has already aided the funds ot the great hospitals by bringing in special donations.</p>
        <p>The announcements of the Railway dividends as they come out are seriously disappointing the money market. It had been thought that with the removal of several adverse conditions, and noticeably the cost of fuel and materials, a new era had dawned for British railways. The last dividends were depressing, but it was confidently believed that a turn had been taken for the better, and that this great and important industry was— not exactly reviving, for there has been no diminu- tion in the volume of business done--but about to prove remunerative to the proprietors. Hope told a flattering tale, and the financial papers vied with each other in producing rosy estimates of the coming division of profits, all agreeing in giving on the stocks of all the principal companies substantial and, in some cases, sensational dividends. Then came the real announcements, and tha pleasant dream has been shattered. Very few shareholders will receive the dividends they have been led by these mistaken prophets to expect, and instead of sensational ad- vances there are in one or two cases quite sensa- tional declines, So far the shareholders of the London, Brighton,and South Coast Railway Com- pany are in the worst plight; but there may be bad news yet for many who have cherished rosy expectations. These alterations of hopes and fears are rather to be regretted, as they operate, it may be feared, in preventing the Railway Companies from taking a really large view of their position, and considering whether anything can be done to lift their undertakings from the slough into which they, as profit-earning concerns, have fallen. If depression continues, and there is any danger of increased rates or lowered efficiency, it will not be long before politicians begin to use the ominous word "Nationalisation."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Provincial volunteers must regret the fact that Mr Brodrick does not hold out any Dope that the new regulations:affecting the force can be with-, drawn or altered. For it will be a serious matter if it should be found that the military enthusiasm which has produced such a force is allowed to cool down under the chilly treatment of the War Office authorities. Everybody recognises, of course, that if the Volunteers are to be of any use at all they must be thoroughly efficient. But it i 3 equally ob- vious that the life of the movemEnt is involved in its voluntary principle. One would naturally imagine that it was quite enough to expect men to sacrifice their time to become " efficients " ; and when it is repired of them, in addition to this, to sacrifice nearly the whole of their wages when they are in camp, it need not surprise any of us to hear them ask what is to become of the wives and children in the meantime. For a large proportion of the Volunteers are married men, and very many are assuredly not in a position to yield up to the nation so much as the War Office makes out that it requires them to do. The question has more than once been asked of late, if the authorities are not in reality feeling the pulse of the country, with a view to the introduction of conscription?</p>
        <p>" Sons of Rest." This is the style and title of an organisation which, if its headquarters are not in Bristol, appears to flourish there. The name is eminently suggestive of lands where it is always afternoon, and all the dreamful picture which Tennyson painted for us in his " Lotus Raters ' But the Sons uf Rest as known to the police in the city of Bristol would scarcely fill in the details of that idyllic scene. Dr. Watts was not far wrong when he referred to the ultimate employer of lazy girls and boys, and two of the " sons of rest " were very rightly sent to prison the other day for aiding cruelty to the work left "for idle hands to do." The Sons of Rest I it is said by the local police, have entered into a :covenant not to do any work, but two of them at least are now undergoing a term of enforced " hard labour" in a place where the: blessedness of their own peculiar institution is not known, or, if known, is not appreciated. The title of this new organisation is certainly preferable in every way to that of "The Hooligans," but the school in which the members of both appear to graduate, is evidently the same. The author of the phrase " Sons of Rest " is a genius in Hs way, like the first schoolboy who described a crib as " a student's help."</p>
        <p>Cannot the doctors leave us alone? pathetically asks a correspondent, who then proceeds to make complaint somewhat as follows. They have now discovered that death lurks in the frying-pan, thus giving a grim meaning to the old adage about jumping out of the frying-pan into the fire. Wh a the doctors say is, that the cheap and thinly enamelled iron saucepans, frying-pans and kettle are responsible for many of the internal trouble which dyspeptics mostly know. The enamel on those parts of the frying-pan or saucepan not covered by the boiling water flakes off in minute sharp-edged particles, which are swallowed with the food. But the correspondent furtber needs to be reminded probably, that from his youth up doctors have inveighed against the too frequent use of the frying-pan, for other reasons than teat men: tioned. It is the rough-and-ready resort of the workman's wife, whojis either too busy with other matters, or too fond of neighbourly gossip,to notice the flight of time until close upon the dinner-hour, when the frying-pan is the friend in need and is often hurriedly used,no matter what the quality or kind of meat to be cooked, to the injury and up- setting of the consumer's stomach. It is many years now since one heard a busy doctor exclaim that if he could have his mind, he would banish every frying-pan from every workingman's home, adding that those who were better circumstanced could please themselves and very seldom allowed the cook to use it,</p>
        <p>For some time past there have been dissertation more or less serious, in certain provin-ial dailv , , papers on the decadence of English Rugby Footbk," as shown by the way in which England is defeate' by the other countries. But this should not prove a difficult problem for footballers or their admirers outside the metropolis to solve. There is really no de- cadence of English football; all that has happened being that the best English footballers are systematically overlooked by the Selection Com_ mittee. Of the two most successful English clubs this year, neither has a representative in the Eng- lish team. Of course, the thing is absurd, and pro- vincial footballers ought to see to it, if they can that some alteration is made. Granted that the difficulties of selection are great, yet it would be well if next season one of the earliest trial matches to be arranged could be, say the West of England versus the Rest. If such a match were played in London, it is believed that the result would be a liberal education to certain officials, and to journal- ists also.</p>
        <p>Boy Smokers have for some time past been objects of considerable attention on the part of School Boards and local authorities scattered over the provinces, but nothing was said in the King's speech last week to indicate that the British Lei.,is- lature had any intention of taking the matter up, while the private member's chances of dabbling in legislative efforts of this or any other kind, appear to become more uncertain every year and every Session. It would, moreover, be more than could reasonably be expected that an English Parliament Should deign to take a leaf from the book of the Manx House of Keys, ancient and venerable as that asseirody is ! Bin even if this were feasible, it is not suite certain as yet whether it would be of any avail. For it has hitherto only been proposed in the House of Keys, that legislation of a draconic character shall be taken with a view to checking the prevalence of smoking by juveniles. If the Bill shou d become law, however, tobacconists will find the new edict to be one that will not allow of much trifling; since no Manxman will care to be fined ten pounds, or go to prison for a month,or both pay the fine and go to prison, for selling a packet of cigarettes to a boy under eighteen years of age,and no boy under eighteen will like to incur the risk of being fined two pounds or sent to prison for a week, or have a whipping. Yet if the drastic measure referred to should pass, these are the penalties that await youthful offenders and those who supply them with these cheap, and sometimes " nasty," whiffs. It has still te be seen, though, whether in the first place the proposal will be carried, and if it is, bow far it will succeed in eradicating the habit of smoking in youthful circles.</p>
        <p>Ilall</p>
        <p>The proposal to legislate is manifestly an outcome of the agitation which has been going on, both in England and Scotland, for several years, against the growing and pernicious habit of cigarette end tobacco smoking by mere striplings everywhere.</p>
        <p>People in the provinces must be getting a little tired of hearing so much, from one quarter and another, about their Decaying Industries. A good half-dozen a these could be mentioned in regard to which the official returns tell a quite different story. There is no substantial reason, for instance, for stating, as has been done, that our fisheries are decaying: though the figures in this instance, relating to the value of fish landed on the Welsh and English coasts during 1900 an] 1901 ratty be calculated at the first glance to convey that impres- sion. The total value of all kinds of fish landed in 1900 was £6,981,285, and last year £6,875,121. show- ing a difference of £106,664. But the strike at Grimsby will aczount for much of this decrease. It is interesting to learn that the haddock and the plaice occupy an honourable position among British fish, while the homely herring appears third on the list. The oysters landed, of course. were countless millions. Crabs reached the respectable total of five millions, while thel lobster family yielded six hundred and fifty thousand specimens. The figures, in short, indicate that the " harvest of the sea " is still very extensive, and very profitable withal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
