      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <p>THE EXAMINERI FRIDAY JANUARY 24, 1902.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>• RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>• RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.I</p>
        <p>The reception on Friday, on the occasion of the presentation of a silver cradle to Mr and Mrs Posnett, was really a brilliant gathering. Every one looked supremely happy but the most gratified faces present were naturally enough, those of the chairman of the Council and his wife. The vice-chairmau Mr Handley made quite an oratorical effort in making the presentation, while Mr Posnett, wits a touch of humour underlying his remarks, responded in quite as happy a vein. It was an unique gathering that only happens once in a lifetime. And the probab- ility is that before Runcorn ever witnesses a similar ceremony the select coterie of youths and maidens who were witnesses will be portly out gentlemen and staid matrons. "So runs the world the way," as Shakespeare puts it.</p>
        <p>It is gratifying to note, despite the fact that life is lived at a tremendous rate even in Runcorn, that tradespeople and others are found anxious to do something for the aged and infirm. The aged per- sons' treat, which is one of Runcorn's hardiest annuals, has again been a pronounced success, and has brought a gleam of sunshine into many lives upon which the darkness of the shadow of death has already commenced to fall. It is a good work to help the infirm, and succour the widow, and the gentlemen who have pioneered the proceedings have done a charitable and a honourable perform- ance. The committee and its officers are to be thanked fur their unselfish exertion.</p>
        <p>There was but little business at the Free Library Committee meeting on Tuesday. An interesting piece of information was however, made public. A lady who, at one time, resided in Runcorn has be- come an authoress and her aunt who lives in the town has presented a copy to the Library. Tnere ought to be a rush for the book:written by a local authoress.</p>
        <p>Sherlock Holmes has now got a formidable rival in a local constabledt has always been conjectured that only Runcorn could breed such a man and at last he stands before us pardonably proud, The peg on which the story bangs would give Conan Doyle a splendid opportunity for a half- intoxicated labourer makes a furious and unprovoked attack on a stone wall. After doing an enormous amount of damage he de- camps, leaving,he thinks, no silent witnesses to his crime. A detective constable, however, notices certain clog marks on the soft soil which have the distinct mark of a three cornered protection on the sole. Making a tour of in-pection he finds a man at work whose imprint is exactly the same. The circumstantial evidence is strong enough to justify arrest. And the prisoner confesses his guilt. What shrewdness and observation ! There's a policeman for you!!!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The same old tale in regard to the match at Leigh. Won by a goal was the verdict on Saturday. as has been the case on several previous occasions. There ie no doubt which wai the best team. The balance of the play was in favour of Runcorn, but mistakes at critical junctures and ill luck robbed the Cheshire lads of a splendid victory. I was not present at this match, hut I have it from the mouth of an old player that the youngsters recently appro- priated justified their selections. Moran missed a glorious chance of a try by " 104ing his head " at the critical moment, but more experienced players also made mistakes, and Moran may take courage and try again. The committee's confidence is shown by the fact that he is chosen to journey to Leeds to-day.</p>
        <p>Houghton will son be himself again. He intends to turn out with the A team to-morrow on a kind of rehearsal for the Oldham match a week hence.</p>
        <p>In the Lancashire Junior League, Runcorn Recreation still lead the way with the Runcorn Hornets cloiely treading on their heels.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Cciunty have been grantAd .C5O by the N.U. for the encouragement ot junior football in the Shire. Runcorn Hornets are short of shekels to pay the rent of their field and home applied to the County Committee for help. There is much speculation as to what the reply will be.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>RUNCORN SAVINGS BANK.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SAVINGS BANK.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the tru3tees and managers of the Bank was held on Thursday evening at the Bank, High-atreet.—Mr Inaison (chairman of trustees) was in the chair, and he was suppott 3d by—Messrs John Pritchard, F Beck, J H •Weston, W Jones, S C IJ.clrett, J W Lightburn, 3 Fennington, R Morris, and W Handley (actuary). THE ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr W Handley, the actuary, presented the annual report, which we published last week. The Chairman moved that the report be adopted ; that an abstract be advertised in the local papers ; and that the auditors should be thanked for their services and re-appointed at an honorarium of p£4 per annum. The report, he said, would have been listened to with satisfaction. The back played a useful part in the thrifty institutions of the town and was largely used by the working people. Then, again, the amount in- vested in Consol Stock showed a permanent thriftiness which was very admirable evidence of the investments of the country. They could only regard the last part of the report with great regret. Mr Haddock, be under- stood, had sent a letter, stating that ill•health was the cause of his being obliged to sever bis connection with the bank. They all hoped that be would soon he re- stored,to his normal state of health, and that his suc- cessor would continue to serve the bank in the same agreeable way that Mr Haddock had done hitherto. The auditors' report showed conclusively that depositors need have no fear of their money being available af any time they required it. The auditors' report, and the additional security by periodical inspection by a Government official, was the very best security that anybody could wish for. He would like to take that opportunity of saying that the public and the depositors were very much indebted to Mr Handley and his son for the close attention they had invariably paid to the business uccier their charge. (Hear, hear.) Mr Beckett seconded. Anyone who had watched the progress of the bank during the past twelve months would quite understand that they had a bank which served its purpose very well. He was very pleased on Saturday r verrings to see so many small deposits, as it showed that the poorer classes were saving their money and they had full confidence in the Runcorn Savings Bank. A few years ago there was a difference of opinion as to whether that bank was as safe as the Post Office Savings Bank, but any doubt in the matter had disappeared, and the public now were well pleased with the conduct of the bank. He thought it would be well if some of their managers would look in at the bank on Saturday or Wednesday evenings. If they did they would find evidence that the Runcorn people were not behind the people of other towng. in saving their money and in making provision for a rainy day. (Hear, hear.) —The resolution was approved.</p>
        <p>MET HADD2C K TEN-DEUS HIS RESIGNATION._</p>
        <p>Mr Haddock wrote tendering his resignation as treasurer.—On the motion of Mr Beck, seconded by Mr J H Weston, the resignation was accepted.—Mr F H Shaw, Mr Haddock's successor at Part's Bank, was appointed to the vacancy on the motion of Messrs Light- burn and R Morris. A vote of thanks to Mr Imison concluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>H. Id &amp;SHTON'S LIFII TONIC Is THE KING- OF TONICS.-63. SANKEY-STREET Warrington.—(Advt II M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC Is THE KING OF TONICS —63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—(Advt.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>AN UNIQUE CEREMONY AT RUNCORN</head>
        <p>AN UNIQUE CEREMONY AT RUNCORN</p>
        <p>The Chairman of the Council and his Wife Receive a Silver Cradle.</p>
        <p>Io3e of the most unique ceremonies which has ever yet been beld in Runeorn was celebrated on Friday night at me Technical Institute in the presence of the most prominent men of the to vn. It is now a matter of history that a few months ago the birth of a sturdy son and heir came to gladden the heart of Ur Posnett during his term of office as Chairman of the Urban District Council. Such a signal event has not occurred to any of the many presidents of the city fathers who have guided the destinies of Runcorn this last fifty yeare. Accordingly. therefore, as per precedent and custom, it was arranged that a miniature handsom 3 silver cradle should be presented to Mr and Mrs Posnett as soon as a fitting occasion presented itself. The time arrived on Friday evening last when there was a brilliant galaxy of local ladies and gentlemen assembled in tee art room of the Tectinieal Institute. Under the touch of the decorators' hand the somewhat bare room had been transformed into a gentlemen's spacious drawing room. Toe approaches were draped with scarlet hangings and prettily hung were streamers and flags va hich formed as neat an alcove to the door as one could wish to see. The room, too, was fittingly in harmony with the occasion. If the sober attire of tee gentlemen and the gay drapery of the ladies looked brilliant iu coutrast,the embellished drawing room looked equally so. A somewhat artistic appearance, also, ores added to the rooms by reason of the Titati-iike statues which stood, mute spectators of the past, in various portions of the room gazing blindly on the scene of festivity. As the guests arrived they were re ceived by the Vice-chairman of the Council, Mr W Hand- ley, the reception being considerably enlivened by tee stirring tones of a string band which discoursed sweet music under the coaductorship of Mr H W Handley. After a tew choice selections of music had been given, which conspired :o put everyone in a merry humour, the eventof thelevening tooklplace—Mr Posnett and Mrs Postiett, who was charmingly attired, stepped forward to the front of the company Mr W Handley leading the way and in an instant the hush of achces ceased. Mr Handley, addressin; the gathering, said it gave him great pleasure, in tie name of the Council, to ask Mr and Mrs Posnett's acceptance of a silver cradle in co.nmemnration of the birth of their son. It was due to his fellow councillors to say that towards its pur- chase all had willingly and cheerfully contributed, and this bad been eqaally true of their executive officers. Not only on accouot of this complete uoa eimity would the gift have a value ia their estimation, but they le3deved that such an interesting commemoration carried with it the good wishes uf the town of ft ancern to themselves and their infant son. For fifty years Runcorn had had a representative governing body, and its deliberations tied been presided over by twenty- one of its honoured townsmen, in the capacity of chairman, but during that long period ot half a century such an event as they were met to celebrate that evening was unique, and, so far as memory Eerved, it was the first time during his term of office that a sea aid heir had been teem to the chairman. It avae also unique, he believed, from the fact that Mr Posnett had attained to the dignity of the chair at an earlier period of life t eau any of his worthy predecessors. and he (Mr Handley) was sure he was voicing the feeling of his fellow councillors in acknow- ledging his urbanity, his liberality, his work, and his constant desire for the progress of Runcorn in the dis- charge of his duties as chairman of the Urban Council. The heraldic device of their town was a full-rigged ship driving before a favouring breeze, emblematic of good luck. If by this was symbolized good fortune,resulcant from integrity of character and the respect and esteem of his fellow councillors and the inhabitants of Ruecorn, then all wished Mr Posnett and his wife the largest measure of good luck,acc empanied by the most favour- able and healthful breezes all through the voyage of life, and they expressed the hope that their son and heir might grow up a joy to his parents and an honour to the town of his birth. (Applause.) The inscription on the cradle was in the following terms : —" Presented to R H Posaett, Esq„ J P, and Mrs Posnett, with every good wish, by the members and officials of the Runcern Urban District Council, in corn- nvAmoratioa ot the birth of their son, November 16 ;C, 1901, during Mr Postnett's year of office as its caair- man." (Applause.) Mr Posnett, addressing the company as fellow towns- men and townswomen,said as Mr Handley was speaking! he felt it was getting more and more difficult for him to ' reply to everything he said, but if he could say not ling better he would heartily thank them, on behalf ot himself and bis wife, for all the kindness which they had shown to him, and of which their gift that evening was a token and emblem. One interesting coincidence had been brought before his notice since he cim3 hate the room. Mr Fisher had kindly given to him the phetogrants of the first chair- man of the old B ard of C ommiss on re, who were the predecessors of the present Urban Distria Council. The first chairman of the Commissioners, exactly fifty years ago' was 'his (Mr Posnett's) predecessor at the tannery, the late Mr John L Wri,ght. That was an interesting coincidence which came to his mind that night. (Hear, hear.) The office of chairman of the Council was not all a bad of roses. It was the grave of a good rn any hopes and ambitions. One began his year of office thinking that he would do something, that hew )old set a magnificent esample, and he fouad as the year went on that it took him all his time to come up to anything like his predecessors. As months went by one found himself humbled by the feeling that he was but a poor successor to men who had filled the chair before, and filled it with great henotir and credit to themselves and the town. He felt, however, that he could truly say on behalf of himself and his wife that their best desires, their best wishes, and their be it interests were closely connected with the town to which they belonged,ani he believed there was nothing which would give them greater joy or greater pleasure than to be able in any way to add to the comfort and welfare of the place which bad proved such a blessing to them- selves. And they hoped that tht ough providential circum- stances they might be able to train up another tanner who might carry on the bright succession. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) He again thanked them for the gift and the kindly feeling which had prompted it. (Applause.) It was stated that Dr McDougall would have been present, and taken part in the presentation on behalf of the officials, but he was prevented owing to indisposi- tion.</p>
        <p>The company were then serv2d with light refresh- ments, and a very pleasant evening was wound up with further music from the band. The cradle is a beautiful specimea of the silver- smith's art, supplied by Messrs John itpund and Son, of Sheffield. The design is that of an eagle's b]dy,shaped in the form of a nautilus, the head of the bird forming a graceful prow. The cradle swings between two curved pillars attached to a fluted base, and standing on an ebony plinth. The amount of silver used in the manufacture of the cradle was 337 ounces. The in- scription is affl red to the plinth. The arrangements for the presentation were in the hen is of Mr W Handley, the vice-chairman ; Mr E Marshall, secretiry ; and Mr J W Wilding, surveyor. 0•0•••••••••••10MNIIMr1M1 DEATH OF A PROMINENT .RUNCORNIAN,</p>
        <p>The remains of the late Mr William Heatherington, of Brackley-street, Runcorn, who died rather suddenly on the 12th hist, were interred at Runcorn Cemetery on Saturday afternoon. It appears that the deceas3d, who was a victim to bronchitis, had been at work only a week prior to his death. He was employed in superin- tending the loading of bpata at the Ship Canal Docks, Runcorn. Besides being an active worker in connection with the Congregational Church, deceased was for many years a member of the Runcorn Division of the ' Order of the Sons of Temperance, and on Sunday, when his mortal remains were committed to their last resting place, a good number of his colleagues in that society attended the obsequies. Those present included Bros T J Holt, grand scribe; Thomas Cook, PG WP; J Slade, PWP; A Kgsnion, PWPtT Whyte, P W P ; T Clarke, PWP; E Key, sec. of cadets ; E Wilkinson. J Hayes, J Hindley, J Evans C Beswiek, J Gandy, J Dunbabin, M L Jones, F Salon, J Rowe, W Dutton, J Hughes, .1 Walker, J Mercer, and J Johnson, FS• The last rites were performed by the Rev J oAoorne, M A, pastor of Bethesda Congregational Church. —On Sunday evening, at the Bethesda Congregational Church, the Rev J Osborne referred to the deceased in the course of his sermon. The rev gentleman said that he wished to give a few words of comfort to the be- reaved family. For many years the late Mr Heather- ington had been ta worker in connection with that church,and previously he had taken much interest in the cause at St. Luke's. He was also an active worker in the temperance cause. The deceased was of a quiet and unassuming character, and was faithful in the discharao of every duty. The choir sang " What are these which are arrayed in white robes," and at , the close of the service, Mr Pritchard, the organist, played the Dead March in " Saul." The following officers and members of the Sons of Temperance attended the service out of respect for deceased's memory :—Messrs T Cook, D Bisbrown, T R Whyte, T J Holt, T Clarke, G Hayes, J Johnson, and J Hindley. At a meeting of the Sons of Temperance on Saturday night reference was made to Mr. Heatherington's death by Mr T Cook. The Secretary, Mr J Johnson, stated that the deceased had been a member of their order for 20 years.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOE UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. PING PO NG, Latest society game. All prices. OARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge-street.—Advt. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH.CLASS MINERAL ATERS.—Advt BEST BOOTS voRALL.—UNDBR THE' CLOCK.—Advt ANTIQUE Wrought IRON and COPPER WA B. for wedding presents. etc.—GARLAND, Bridge St,—Advt. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR GOOD BOOTS.—Adv ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>OLD MEN'S TREAT AT HALTON.</p>
        <p>The old men's treat at 13.alton was held on Tues- day. Mr T Wright was the organiser and over 33 Eat down to a substantial repast.</p>
        <p>EXCITING INCIDNT.</p>
        <p>An exciting incident occurred at noon on Wednesday. A lurry was standing in Cooper-st• when the horse attached became startled and dashed up King-street into Church•street which was crowded with scholars. Doubtless many acci- dents would have been recorded haa not Messrs T Briscoe and C Watson gallantly captured the animal and brought it to a standstill after a struggle. HOME MISSION ANNIVERSARY._ _</p>
        <p>This anniversary in connection with St. Paul's Chapel will be held on Sunday and Monday, when addresses will be delivered by the circuit ministers. The Rev J Posnett, of Leiuster, will attend and give an account of his great mission in Leinster, wsich is one of the mast thrilling experiences of home mission work ever related. ST. PAUL'S WESLEY GUILD.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Literary and Reading Circle, the members continued their study of Charles Kingsley's Works on Thursday evening (last week), in the Guild Parlour, Brunswick school. There was a satisfactor3 attendance of members and friends. Mr George Hayes occupied the chair. A paper was given by Mr T Saunders, subject—" Two years ago." The study of this book proved extremely interesting. There was a good discussion afterwards in which Messrs George Hayes and H Dodd took part. The essayist suitably responded, RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>The Library Committee met on Tuesday evening, Mr S Pope in the chair,—The librarian (Ntrs Jones) reported that the total number of issues for the month of December was 2,059. an increase of 33 in the reference department, and a decrease of 161 in the lending department.—lt was turther reported that Sir John T Brunner, Bart had presented the library with two volumes. One was entitled, "The war against consumption," and the other " With Rimington," Mrs Branch, Greenway Meadows, had also presented "My Island" by Miss Eilean Hughes' The authoress, was Mrs Branch's neice and Niro a former Runcorn lady.—The report was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>AGED PERSONS TEAT AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE AGED PERSONS TEAT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon tee annual tea for the aged of Runcorn was held. It is pleasing te record that it is one of the most successful of its kind. There were no less than 126 males and 248 females.—an increase of 60 on last year—with ages ranging from 60 to 91. As in previous years there was an excellent repast provided for the old folk and theee was no lack of willing workers to wait on the tables. While the feasting proceeded Mr Wingfield's gramaphene played several selections which were duly appreciated and which formed a very pleasant feature. As soon as the tea was finished an ounce of tobacce, given by Messrs Ogdene,aad two 0110C(33 of tea were served out.to the old couples. After tea a capital concert was provided over which Mr R H Posnett, chairman of the Council presided. He was supported by several prominent local ladies and gentlemen. In opening the entertainment the chairman elid that his thanks were due to Mr Handley for having done his work prior to the meeting. He came in at the end when it was all done. He had received a letter from one of the best friends that Runcorn ever possessed,Mr Samuel raylor. (App'aese.) He was very pleased to have such a pleasant opportunity, in the midst of other duties, to take part in such a pyous gathering. Sloes they last met "Qeeen Victoria the Good' bad gone to the better land. Over the old folk Queen Victoria had reigned many years'and looking on the past they thought with kiedly love of the face that had disumeared from I the land for ever. " Tee Qaeen was dead ! Long live I the King." Oa behalf of the younger race be thanked the old folks for making the town what it was and he hoped the rising race would fill the place as well as they had done. He had on many occasions rejoiced in the kindness and esteem of his fellow townsmen and ' at the req fest of the committee ha had brought down the cradle which had been hresented to him by the Runcorn councillors and the officials. It stood on the table where all:might see it. He thenked all who had pioneered the treat to such a splendid success. The programme was then proceeded with. The Male Voice Choir gave several well chosen selections which still further added to their reputation. The most amus- ing feature of the evening was the selection by Mr A Grounds entitled " Take your umbrella with you John " and," Heigh up it's coming." Miss Turner was cordially applauded for the humorous recitation "Hang- ing a picture." " Alone on a raft," and" Home Sweet Home," by Miss Humphreys were deservedly encored. Mr En:lien's humorous songs were CFO duly appreciated Miss Temperley's violin Solos evoked much admiration "The Owl critic" was recited capitally by Miss Saun- ders, while Miss Lily Shaw with " You did it " won a fair measure of applause. Master Harold Yarwood's mandoline solo was executed very well indeed for so young an artiste, and Miss Lily Shaw and Miss Amy Coward were recalled for "I don't want to play in your yard." Votes of thanks closed the meeting. The following ladies presided at the tables during tea : Mesdames Hayes, T Jones, Bellamy, Bell, D Little- more, Walton, Dudley, Bowyer, F J Norman, E Lightfoot, A R Norman, Percival, S Littler, W Leath- wood, Morris, i' Leathwond, Coventry,T Lea, T Wood, Richardson, Moore, T C William;, T Cooke, Farwood, Clare, W Shaw, jun., Reynolds, Seragg.Ellison,Winfield, Temperley,W Shaw,sen., Hailwood, Weston, J M Frith, and Wells ;: Misses Norman, Coward, Riley, Farragher, Temperley, Lunt, Beck, Rowland, Broadhurst, Sweet- man, Humphreys, and F Shaw. The following gentlemen formed the various commit- tees, which managed the proceedings :—Chairman, Mr R H Posnett ; treasurer, Mr W Handley ; Collecting Committee, Messrs T Wood and W B vfleld ; "ea and provision committee, Messrs Wood, S Coventry, W Shaw, M J Sweetman, T C ;hail hall committee, W Morris, T Riley, T Williams, W Shaw, W Byfield, T Leach, W Ocksworth, E Gatley, D Littlemore, J Hayes; scrutiny committee. Messrs J Hall, T Cooke, S Littler ; stores committee, Messrs Sweetman, H ughee, W Byefield ; cab com- mittee, Messrs Williams, T RileY, S Littler, W Byefield ; 'entertainment committee, Messrs W Milliugton, T Cooke, P Wood, S Coventry, W P Timperley, H W Handley, WrShaw,jun,F Ellison. Messrs Booth and Yarwood very kindly lent carriages to convey a number of infirm persons to the ball, Mr Howard is to be congratulated on the manner in 'which he carried out the arrangements.</p>
        <p>VIADUCT WORKS OFFICIAL ANNUAL DINNER The annual dinner promoted by the office staff and foremen of the Viaduct works, Earlestown,took place on Saturday evening at the Victoria Rotel, Liverpool. As on previous occasions thp party were conveyed to Lime-street in a special saloon which was attached to the 120 express. On reach- ing Liverpool the party dispersed to pass the after- noon, some taking the opportunity of an appetiser by a visit to New Brighton, whilst others visted the theatre.lgAt 6 30 dinner was partaken of,and being of :an excellent character and capitally served,those who sat down did hearty justice to the good things provided. The subsequent proceedings were pre- sided over by Mr T Ormand the vice-chair being occupied by Mr C W Emmett. A number of toasts were proposed and duly honoured, and a convivial evening was spent, songs and recitations being given by several of those present. The party re- turned to Earlestown by the train leaving Lime- street at 11 15 p m, after one of the most enjoyable dinners held.</p>
        <p>'CRAWFORD'S "A LEXANDRA " BISCUITS. JUST</p>
