      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>By " °waist."</p>
        <p>It was just the turn of the luck, as I predicted, that did the trick for Warrington on Saturday. They would have won had Dame Fortune smiled upon them for one moment, but no. She looked the other way and the Blues scrambled home by the small margin of a penalty goal. Warrington played in gallant, skilful style, and though Swinton opened the scoring, Warrington rallied finely and responded with a try. Again when Swinton got ahead, their antagonists proved themselves equal to the emergency, and at half-time the score was two tries each. In the second half Warrington had to do without W. Swift who came under the ban of the referee, and though the Blues were kept out the referee won them the match by awarding a penalty against Warrington which was entirely undeserved. Such is fate</p>
        <p>There was a great deal of interest taken in the encounter and the Manchester folk went down to Swinton in large numbers,the Warrington match being the only class fixture. in the district. Speculation was rife as to how Fish and Hampson, the elect and the reserve for the County would:shape against one another. Rather curiously Fish has never played at. Swinton before, injuries or illness keeping him out of the team on previous occasions, and last season but one when the margin was only a try, I believe we should have won comfortably had Fish been on in place of Hocken- hall. He was accorded a warm welcome from the crowd, who evidently thought him worth the honours he has won. Throughout the game, too, he was a match for Hampson, and as he scored on the Swinton idol's wing his superiority was clearly manifested. Considerable interest was also shown in Dickenson,but the young Warrington centre failed lamentably to come up to expectations except on the defence. He was a long way behind Valentine in point of skill and useful- ness. It was unfortunate that he was not in his best form at Swinton because young " Val "is Dickenson's only rival for the centre position.</p>
        <p>The game itself was a magnificent one, being thor- oughly fast, keen and open. Neither side loves the kick and rush game, and Swinton from the outset went off with lightning-like passing movements which com- pletely baffled the Warrington defence but failed at the critical time. Cooper was twice at fault in taking the ball before he at last opened the scoring, and alto- gether I reckon that he should have had five tries before the interval instead of two. This looks as if Warrington were much out of the hunt, and so they were to begin with, for Swinton's opening display was remarkable. Once scoring started and Warrington pulled themselves together. They drove the Blues to their own quarter, and here Burgess artfully punted across to Fish when the home backs expected a pass. Fish fielded with his usual cleanness, and doubling round Hampson and Chorley equalised matters beyond the corner flag. Not five minutes later Cooper was over again, though Ratcliffe tried to stop him on the line. In this case Harris made a poor attempt to collar the right man when the pass was given at half-way. Another five minutes elapsed and then Allen threw across to Harris, who beat Cooper, and kicking over Chorley's head scored a clever try in nice position. It was quite the best piece of judgment I have seen Harris yet display, for the position of his opponents was such that his usual bull-at-a-gate methods would have re- sulted in failure Fish should have converted but didn't,and the value of that kick was not fully realized until later in the day.</p>
        <p>Warrington had the advantage of the slope in the second half and went off as if they meant winning. Swinton were sorely pressed, and then to the surprise of the crowd, the referee suddenly brought W Swift out of the scrimmage and sent him off the field instantly. I , believe the Warrington man had been struck in the pack and landed out in retaliation, but, unfortunately for him and his side the referee saw the second blow and promptly punished the offender. There is a lot too much of this hitting up in the " scrums " and it wants rigidly putting down. The Warrington men had better be careful for Saturday's incident spoiled their chance of winning. Swinton gradually worked their , way to the Warrington quarter and here Morrison was foully tackled. The referee saw half the incident and hastily concluding that Warrington were to blame again awarded Swinton a penalty kick and Evans placed the goal which won the matcli. To begin with I do not think a penalty can be awarded for roughness or fighting between players, and in this case the Swinton man was certainly the aggressor, so that the kick should have been given to Warrington if given at all. It was an unfortunate blunder for Warrington on Mr Bruckshaw's part, for though they played hard they had small chance of loaning and Swinton attacked frequently towards the close. A draw would have teen the fairest result of an excellent game, but I still think Warrington would have proved the better side had they been at full strength in the second half. -- Ratcliffe, for Hallam, was the only change from the selected team on either side. The Warrington full back was taken ill on the Friday night, so the "A " team skipper once more filled the vacancy. Right well I did Ratcliffe play too, his fielding and kicking being quite up to that of Chorley on the other side, and he was guilty of only one small slip throughout the game. He is an excellent understudy for Hallam, but lacks ' the latter's physique and tackling power. Burgess was in his best mood on Saturday, and did quite as good work as the renowned Dai ravies. The Swinton pair, as a pair, were certainly the best, but individually there was not much in it. Fish, Isherwood. and Harris played very well, though I have seen the latter tackle much better. Dickenson was the one failure. The forwards played finely, Boardman, Morrison, Edmond- son and Taylor being the pick. G Jolley made a good impression. Swinton seemed to be a finely-balanced team with great scoring power. Wallwork is making almost as good a centre as R. Valentine. while Cooper, though poor at taking the ball, is a dangerous wing man. Being a six-footer he naturally takes a lot of stopping.</p>
        <p>The record for the past few seasons of the games between Swinton and Warrington is as follows 1898-9.—Away: Swinton, 1 try ; Warrington, 1 try. Home: Swinton, 2 goals 2 tries ; Warrington, nil. 1899.0.—H0me: Swinton, 2 tries ; Warrington, 1 try Away: Swinton, 1 try; Warrington, 1 goal. 1900-I.—Away : Swinton, 1 try; Warrington, nil. Home: Swinton, 1 goal 3 tries ; Warrington nil. 1901-2.—Away: Swinton, 1 try; Warrinton, nil home Swinton 8 points; Warrington 6 points. War- rington's leeway is getting considerable. They must not allow the Blues any more points at Wilderspool. I , wish we met them in the cup ties !</p>
        <p>By the way the Swinton ground was about 3 inches deep in oat-husks. This sort of thing handicaps a visit- ing team considerably and ought I think, to be pro- tested against. Warrington once made Broughton clear their playing pitch of such a nuisance.</p>
        <p>The League table up to date is as follows For Ardnet.</p>
