      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>EARLESTOWN LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the above Association a meeting was held in the Co-pperative Hall, Eirlestown on Tues- day evening, for the purpose of bearing an address by the Hen, I A Brassey on " A policy on which all Liberals may write." Mr U W Rawlins presided, and there was .a very good audience.</p>
        <p>The Chairman- s'aid they had beard a good deal about disunion amongst the Liberal party, aro.: there was no doubt that on one questioa certainly the Liberal party was not altogether united. He never was, and he hopei. he never should be, a pessimist, so far as the Liberal patty, and its connection was concerned. Lord Rose- hers', talking about Great Britain, said that it always came out on top in the end, and he thought they could safely Eay that the Liberal party would:come out on:top. We made history very rapidly in these days, and had been doing for the last thirty years, and the o d Liberal watch cry of Peace, Retrenchment, and Reform did not quite satify them in these days. Peace they all wanted. Retrenchment they wanted, to, and while a great pro- portion of tee working class householders in the country ware practical), disfranchised they wanted referm, toe. They wanted to know how they could best advance the interests of the country and they did not want little , cliques to carry on their own little affairs to the detri- ment of the whole nation. Mr Bras,ey was kind enough to come there that evening to make them a speech on the subject of a programme that might unite the Liberal party, and be had no doubt they would listen to what be had to say with great interest. Mr Brassey came of a good old Radical stock, h 3 had fought several Parliamentary tests,and in his last battle he very nearly brought about a successful issue, as he lost by only s very few votes. (Applause.) The Hon T A Brasses, said he was very much pleased to learn that they possessed such a flourishing Co-opera- tive Society in Earlestown. He had long taken a great interest in the Co-operative movement and was always glad to find a flourishing Co-operative Society in exist- ence in any town he visited. (Heir, heir). The sub- ject announced for discussion at that meeting was " A policy on which all Liberals may unite." A somewhat ambitious tills, some might say, but he and those who worked with him thought they had good grounds for claiming that the title was justified. That was the first tinblic meeting held during the present year to discuss the policy which he was there to advocate. Like their chairman, he held the opinioa that Liberals must agree to differ in their opinion as regards the origin and effect of the war. There were and must be differences of opinion in the Liberal party as regards the wir and it was only by mutual toleration of differences of opinion , that they could get the Liberal party together. They must tolerate honest difference of opinion on a subject which he maintained the L'beral party were not re- sponsible fur, and the Liberals muse think what were their objects for the future. He then proceeded to re- view in detail the initiation and progress of the Home Rule movement for Ireland, which began in Dublin in 1870, and alluded to the various bills which were from time to time introduced. Toe question, he said, had yet to be faced, but they must endeavour to face</p>
        <p>it on proper national grounds. He would like them to consider whether Parliament was effectively discharg- jog its duties at the present time, both as regards the questions which immediately coneerned the people of these isles and of thecelonies. Was there anyone who would say that our educational system was on a proper footing. The education qurstion was a vital one to the people of this country and unless our people were pro- perly equinpei to meet the competition from Germany and the United States, they were going to fare very badly. The Temperance question also had not received adequate attention by the Government. The problem of how they were to deal with the aged workers of the community required every attention. The question of the Mercantile Marine was he coming a pressing one. The proportion of foreigners in the Mercantile Marine was increasing. It would be a serious question in the case of war, and it was a question demanding the earn- est consideration of Parliament. The House el Commons had to deal with matters aff,ctiag all parts of the em- pire and of imperial defence. They all knew that the question of the organisation of the Army deserved vary serious consideration. It was a very big question and a very difficult, one, and one that was not Likely to re- ceive a -lequate attention under present circumstances, Then there was the control of national expenditure. He had devoted a considerable amount of study and time to naval questions. For many years he had worked :on the navy, and he had done something to popularise naval questions amongst the general body of the people of tnis country. Dr his opinion the want of peeper criticism at the bands of the legislature on Naval Expenditure In recent years could only lead to extravagance. The naval estimates had risen very rapidly. and they now amounted to £30,000,000. Then there was the expenditure on naval works, which meant the expenditure on such things as Dover Harbour, Gibraltar Harbour, construction of Docks in Hong Kong &amp;c., for which last year £27,000,000 was proposed. The expenditure before it was granted ought to have been very much more criticised by the representatives of the people in Parliament, and he did not think the wheee of that expenditure was justified. On August 9th last year, £67,000,000 of public) money was voted in three hours. In the fees of such facts as those mid anyone Maintain that Parliament could effectively diseharge its dutia Under such conditions. 01 all hands it was coming to be admitted that our Parliamentary Govern- ment was breaking down. Some said it was due to the obstructive tactics of the reeposition ; others said it was due to the incompetent way business was conducted by the leader of the House. He was not there to defend the way Mr Balfour filled his place in the House of Commons. In his opinion the real cause of the break- down was due to neither of these thiGgs. It was due to the one simple fact that Parliament had too much to do. (Hear, hear.) We were attempting to deal with the affairs of the greatest empire the world had ever seen and who could wonder that the attempt had broken down. What was the remedy? It was sug- gested to them in the resolution he would read and it was suggested by Lord Rosebery in his great speech at Chesterfield. The first thing the Liberal party had to lock to was their Parliamentary machine. It was said Parliament was on its trial, in his opinion it had long been on its trial, The resolution before him was "That this meeting regarding the cengestion of business in Parliament as a fatal obstacle to progress in social and domestic legislation islof opinion that this obstacle can best be overcome by the establishment of local legis- lature in the several countries of the United Kingdom, having power to deal with its own internal affairs, leaving to the existing Imperial Parliament the manage- ment of those matters which affect the United Kingdom as a whole and ef all Imperial business. It was further of opinion that the Colonies should be invited to send representatives to the Imperial Parliament since they desired to share with the mother country the burdens of the empire." The meaning of the resolution was that we should establish Parliaments in Ireland, in Scotland, in Wales, and England for the transaction of purely Irish, Scotch, Welsh, and English business, so that the Imperial Parliament should go on dealing with questions which affected the who;e of the kingdom, until such times as the Colonies were prepared to make a perman- ent contribution to the expenditure of the empire,when they could reasenably claim to have a voice in the Par- liament on that expenditure. Speaking of the eangestion of business in the House of Commons at present, he asked had it never occurred to them what an appalling waste of time it was to ask Irish, Welsh, and Seotchmen to come to Westminster to help us to manage questions which only affected England. The tendency ' to deal with the requirements of each country en special lines had very mien increased of late years. The waste of time which had already become a hindrance to our legislation in Parliament would become more serious than it had in the past. Most Liberals were in favour of mending or ending the House of Lords. They anew there was a considerable agitation going forward some 6 or 7 years ago in connection with the House of Lords. As the son of a Peer, of course be did not like to end his father. The effect of this abolition would be that the control of social and the domestic questions would be removed altogether from the House of Lords. Some people woulfbe in favour of a direct attack on the Reuse of Lords, but Ise ventured to think that the House of Lords would be a more difficult body to overcome than some people thought. But the adoption of the policy set forth in the resolution would remove altogether from the House of Lords the control of secial and domestic legislation. For the present we should con- centrate tour efforts upon the establishment of local legislatures for dealing with internal affairs. (Hear. hear.) _</p>
        <p>The Chairman then proposed the resolution which hal been read by the:Hen. Mr Brassey., Mr B Harrison seconded, and the Per E C Bocock spoke in support. The resolution was put and carried witnout dissent. Dr Latham moved. and Mr W Davies seconded, a vote Of thanks to the:Hon. Mr Brasses,' which was heartily carried. H vote or thanks to the Chairman concluded the meeting.</p>
