      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>THEFT as RIVETS FRoM WALTON ARCHES. At the Warrington Police Court on Motility. before Mr F Monks and other magistrates. William Melia. 67, Boatock- street, was charged on remand with stealine from the New Mersey Viaduct Worke. Walton, some time betwer n one o'clock on January lelth and half-past six the following morning 6ewt of steel rivets. valued at £5 the property of Messrs Heenan and Froude,Manchester. Mr!,Steel appeared for the prisoner and pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable said the prisoner had been remanded singe Thursday when he was elaar2ed with having the rivets in his possession. The account he then gave of them proved to be false. Prisoner went to the works of Messrs Monks, Hall and Cons pany early on Wednesday n.erning arid offered the rivets for sale as scrap to an employe named John Chippendale. He (Chippen- dale) saw that the rivets were not scrap but valuable articles and lee made a communication to those in authority with the reeult that informationawal given to the pollee. The prisoner was seen and said that on the previous night he was in a public-house and " treated" a flatman who subsequently took him in a boat to Walton Arches and gave him some rivets which were in a barge moored there. He subsequently modified his story of " three men In a boat" because the rivets had been identified by a representative of Messrs 'Bastian and Froude's RS having been stolen from the Viaduct Works at Walten. It seemed that the prisoner had gene to the place in his own boat and brought them away.—Mr Steel said that prior to this prisoner had been a respectable scan. He had been In the employ of Messrs Monks. Hall and Company for 19 years And had never hid a stain neon his character before Ha hid been in the habit of walking to Walton Arches and saw the rivets lying about there. He thought they were of no value. On Tuesday night he was in the company of same other men, and had had some drink when it oecnrred to him that he might bring the rivets away and obtain some money *for them. He went in his boat and did so. He left them in the boat during the night and in the morning took them in a handeart to the works of Messrs. Menus. Flail and Co., and offsred them for sale. He (Mr Steel) submitted that prisoner believed, the rivets were of no value and there was net the felonious Intent one generally found in them cases. The prisoner took the rivets openly in a handcart to the works and that showed what his idea was with regard to them. He suggested that the prisoner might be dealt with under the First Offen- ders Act.—The Chairman salt the Chief Conat able bad been misinformed as to the value of the rivets. —The Chief Con- stable said ills information we from Messrs Heenan and Fronde.—The Chairman said the red value of the rivets was about ss. Prisoner knew whet hey were worth, and he would be committed to gaol for a mouth with hard labour. A WOMAN'S VIOLENT CONDUCT—SMART FINE. Sarah Jane Wilson. a woman of respectable appearance who resides at 322, Knutsford-soad, was charged at the War- rington Police Court on Tuesday, with having been drunk and disorderly at Market Gate at 12.29 that morning and with assaulting P.C. Hewitt and Richard denks.a cab driver. —Mr Steel appeared for the prisoner —P.C. Hewitt stated that he was on duty at Market Gate and saw the prisoner who was drunk and acting in a very disorderly manner. He requested her to go home and she refused and struck hire on the jaw with her clenched fist. He took her into custody and she threw herself to the ground and strug. gled vary violently. P.C. Millington came to his assistance, add toeether they got the woman to the Police Station. She made use ef disgusting language all the way. She was very violent in the c-Ils at the Police Station.—P.C. Millington corroborated.—The Chief Constable then read the report of Sergeant Williamson who was on deity at :the Bridewell. The officer stated that pelsoner's conduct in the cells was out- rageous. She kept the place in WI uproar, and he was com- pelled to have her boots taken off and remove her to a cell sae for away as possible from the other prieoners.—Richard Jenks, a cab driver in the employ of prisoner's brother, stated that shortly after midnight he was going along Friars'. gate te put his horse in the stable when he saw the prisoner, who asked him to drive her home. Re refused to do so and she kicked hires—Mr Steel said he was instructed by the prisoner to express her very great sorrow for what had occurred. Nothing would have happened if the entoman had driven her home. She was extremely sorry for having assault° t the constable and hoped the Bench would deal leniently with her. Prisoner, who) made her fifth appearance, was fined 5s and costs for the first offdnoe. El and costs for assaulting the coLstable and ICs and costs for the assault on Jenks (£2 12s in all.)</p>
        <p>THEET or SPELT ER FR,M LONGFORD WIRE WORKS-THREE</p>
        <p>MoSUBS IMPRISoIiKEIIT. At the County Borough Police Court on iThursday. before Messrs John Fairclough (in the chair and Charles White, David Lee, Oliver-street, was charged with stealing an ingot of spelter. value es from th works of the Longford Wire and Iron Company Limited,sometime between Deeember 15th and January 11th. He pleaded gailty.—Prisoner was brought before the Court on suspicion on Monday and was remanded in order that inquiries might be made.—The Chief Constable informed the magistrates that on Satur- day evening. January 11th. P.O Lewis, who was stationed at the Winwlck-road Police Statism was on duty near Jockey Bridge and saw the prisoner in company with another man. He watched them go down Jockey-street, and shortly afterwards he saw ~hem return, each one carrying something under his arm. He followed the men into Watkin-street and as soon as they perceived the ollicer coming towards them they began to run The Constable followed them and as he was about to seize the prisoners. Lee dropped a plate of spelter from tinder his arm, doubled under the oflicer'e arm and for the time being escaped. The officer knew the prisoner and subsequently arrested him at his residence. The man then statisl that he had found the spelter in passing the manure works and did not know where it had come from. Since the day of his remand las had admitted stealing the ingot from Longford Wire Works. In- quiries bad been made and it had been found that between December 16th and January 11th 57 ingots similar to the one mentioned In the charge bad been missed from the works the total value being about £l7. He (Mr Talbot) felt certain —and he realized the seriousness of the statement—from In- formation in his possession, that if the prisoner liked to open his mouth he could disclose a system of methodical robbery from that works and other works and also the nefarious system of reseivisag stolen goods. Being a man of experi- ence in these Matters prisoner perferred to plead guilty to the offence in which he was caught redhanded aid bear the brunt of the punishment. Ile (the Chief Constable) should like to say with regard to those who had been acting in collusion with the prisoner, that the police were conversant with what was going on and were familiar with their methods. Prisoner hat a bad record, having been convicted seven times for warehouse breaking, house breaking and other offences. A number of brass taps which had been stolen were found in his possession but the owner declined to prosecute. He (the Chief Constable) believed the man had been acting as a go-between between the receiver of the property and some of the workmen at the works. Pri- soner was committed to gaol for three months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>MINOR OASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY—Before —Councillors Smethurst On the chair) and John Roberts and Mr Thornton. A DANGEROUS l'EsuticF..—Herbert Moorcroft.art engineer, residing at 48. Forster-street, was summoned for discharging a gun within fifty feet of the highway near Orford Hall, on January 4th. Denis Donlan, horse dealer. Kendrick-street, stated that 11 o'clock on Saturday morning he was near</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>SOZODONT</head>
        <p>SOZODONT</p>
        <p>- Will Sweeten ths Breath all day, and make all the difference be. tween— Good Teeth anti Bad Teeth. whtea Teeth and Yellow Teeth. Pretty Teeth and Ugly Teeth Ctimplete in Toilet Tco*Pt.wder.</p>
        <p>Orford Hall, on horseback and saw the defendant discharge a rifle within a hundred yards from him on the road which ran parallel with the Cheshire Linea Railway. The contents ol the gun came very close to him (witness)—Defenclant stated that a cartridge bad got fast in the right barrel and he could not get it out so he fired the gun in the air on the spur of the moment, He knew he Was doing wrong but he did not see anyone in the road.—A fine of 103 and cost was Imposed. MoNDlY.—Before Mr F Monks (in the chair), Aldermen Sutton and Francomb, Councillor Smethurst, and Messrs Bird:rail, Garnett, Hayward, Thornton and Wright. Tau POLICE3IIII'B LOT.--Kiehael Moran, of no settled abode, was charged with having been elrunk and disorderly at Market-gate on Saturday night and assaulting P C durner. C Turner said he was on duty at Market-gate when the prisoner threw two slices of bacon in his face, and said he would punch his head in. Prisoner was drunk, and witness took him to the Police Static.... On the way he tried to trip him and kicked him several times.—Prisoner was committed to gaol for a month. 'Bus HOREIER' SUART PERFORMANCE.—Patrick Fitzpatrick, 'bus driver, 78 Longford-Areet, was summoned for driving I furiously along Winwick road on January lltb.—Sergeant ! Smith said he saw the defendant driving three horses ' attached to a 'bus in the direction of Winwick. The horses Were on full gallop, and were going at twelve miles an hour. Defendant was beating them. Witness called upon him to stop, but be did not. Witness sawlhim when he returned James Moffatt. a carter, said the horses were travelling at 13 to 14 miles an hour. (Laughter.) Defendant was fined be and costs (165.) A DMERTER.—John William Neild was charged with deserting from the 146th Battery of toe Royal Field Artillery, on January 17th.—P C. li-ennett gave evidence of prisoner's arrest and the man was remanded to await the arrival of a military escort. —The officer Was recommended for the usual reward.</p>
        <p>Tunana-r.—Before Aldermen Shaw Green (in the chair) and</p>
        <p>Hutcbinson and Dr. Fox. IMPRISONMRNT FoR NEGLRCT —Alfred France, Gaskep- street,was charged on a warrant with neglecting to maintain his two children, whereby they became chargeable to the oonastann fund of the Warrington Union.—Mr B Shearer prosecuted and said that prisoner's wife and two Children were admitted to the Workhouse on July 17th. hiving previously been inmates ot the institution for ,a considerable time. They were first admitted in October 1893 and in October of the following year prisoner was sent to gaol for deserting them. When he came out his wife went to Ilve with him and they lived together until last July when prisoner was sent to gaol for neglecting the children His wife and children went to the Workhouse and since that time he had hardly ever worked.— Prisoner's wife was called and said that the prisoner kept himself mainly by poaching. He spent most of the money that he obtained in this way in drink.—Mr Shearer said the family had cost the Guardians over £47, exclusive of out- door relief, during the past two years.—Priac.n or was sent to gaol ter three months with hard labour. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr T Birched! fin the chair), Alderman _ .</p>
        <p>Monks and Hutchinson. Botts BIRCHED F3it THEFf.—Five boys named Arthur Davies, 15 Albert-street, Thomas Harris, 3 Plarpoiat-street. Joseph Harris, 3 Pierpoint-street, Thomas Joseph Howell. 13 Albert-street, and Francis Burtaft, 34 Pierpoint-street were charged with steMing from an unoccupied house in a brick- field off liewsey-road,on Monday afternoon, an oven and iron grate, valued at Li, the property of the St. PAlli'S Church Trustees. They pleaded guilty. —Superintendent Monaghan said he was in Scotland-road about four o'clock. He met the first defendant wheeling a barrow covered with an oilcloth. H. etopoed him, and found that the barrow contained an oven and grate which had been broken up. The other boys who were with Davies ran away. It was found that the iron had been taken from an unoccupied house which belonged to the St. Paul's Church Trustees. Mr Smethurst, church warden, would tell them that everything that could be taken had been taken from the house. The bench decided to order Howell, who had been preyionsly convicted for theft, to I receive twelve strokes of the birch, and the rest to receive six strokes.</p>
