      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before !Ir. A. R. linrnaan (In the chair) and Mr.</p>
        <p>S. Taylor. A VICCIdATIoN EXEMPTION.—In applioant for the neces- sary certificate. named Joseph Meaminn, Wellington-sirec, who claimed to have studied ‘ both sides of the vaccination question." was granted the exemption CertifiC ate for his child Cacti. CIIaCKED BY His CLoG9.--lhoinal Ainsworth, Clifton. was summoned for damaging a stone wall the property of Samuel Rowland.—Datendant pleaded guilty and added that he was drunk at the time.—Prosecutor statei that the stone wall was all right on the ICth inst, but the following day he found it had been knocked down. A. portion of the coolno had also been removed. Thera had been no quarrel between witness and defendant.—P C Wylie stated that he noticed, when prosecutor showed him the wall, certain clog marks. Later on he saw defendant working on some soft soil. Comparing the impression he found they coincided even to a three cornered boot protector on the sole of the clog.—Prisoner could give no rests ms why he cinamitted the damage and was tided the costs 9s 6d and the cost of the damage 118.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>DOMESTIC STAGNATION.</head>
        <p>DOMESTIC STAGNATION.</p>
        <p>" Altv%ys doing. never done— 'Ls thus t good wife's life is run."</p>
        <p>Virien the wife is taken from the working man's horno, or she is texporarily laid asi.le through. illness, then it is with that home as it is with a clock whose mainspzing is terotca, Nearly everything COM,e,EI to a standstill ; or ahatever continues ts go, goes wrong. No hired help will for a moment compare with the ,ving kindoes of the mother, who by natural insti:..et anticipates the needs and wishes of husband and chil- dren. It is sad to thiok how these admirable qualities should often lead to a breakdown though evei-w4rk— how the one who is al wayS FO keenly watchful fsr the welfare of others will allow her own health to be sapped, with no word of complain:, until—the collapse. Mrs Annie Mumbaison is the wife of a collier, and lives at Bolton Gate, Mealsgate, near Wigton, in Cum- berland. " Three years ago," said Mrs Mumberson on August 28th, 1901, " I had a serious illness. Often dur- iag the day a stabbing pain seiz •d me in the stomach, lasting foe a quarts? of an hour or more. Sometimes the pain ebot through my left aide so that it was only with difficulty I could catch my breath, I often went without my dinner and tea from fear of the pain that would follow. Even a glass of en lk or water would sometimes cause ma to vomit, so that I lost flesh rapidly. With barely strength to walk, restless nights, and a wretched depression of spirits, it was clear that I was in a very bad way. A doctor whom I coasulted said I was suffering hum weakness of the stomach, and gave me some medicine but I got no good of it, and had to give up my house duties and go to stay with my mother. It was while on a visit to my mother that one of those interesting little pamphlets which are occasionally issued by the pro- prietors of Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup.was brought to the house. I was so impressed by many of the Cures described (some of the cases resembling my own), that I resolved to give it a trial, and ths first bottle I obtained seemed to put new life into me. I became lass nervous, and felt better and brighter. One great thing—l was now able Co retain food, and so grew stronger. In all, I took seven bottles before being restored to sound health, and sines the 3 never feel safe unless I have a bottle of Mothi r Seigel's Curative Syrup in the house. If any of any chiloren are bilious, or otherwise ailing, a dose or two of Mother Seigel's Cura- tive Syrup never fails to set them right." It is never too late to do well ; but had Mrs Mumber- , son taken her case in hand earlier, and availed herself of the means by which she ultimately found relief, she would have averted /touch of her suffering.</p>
        <p>" Our Neighbours in Space." This was the subject of an astronomical lecture on Monday evening last in connection with the Wycliffe Young People'a Society, by Mr John Wat- son, who is a Fellow of the British Astronomical Society. There was a numerous attendance and all present must have had an intellectual treat.—The Rev John Yonge, in introducing the lecturer, said that the study of astronoiaoy was, he believed, Mr Watson's pet nobby. He (the speaker) thought it a very good thing for all young men and also young women i to have a hobby wherewith to occupy their leisure hours after the business of the day was fin- ished. The thorough study of any branch of knowledge could but lead to a lifelong pleasure in that subject.—Mr Watson, before exhibiting his lantern slides, gave a full and detailed account of the results of the researches made by all the emin- ent astrologers past and present. The lantern slides consisted of some very fine photographs descriptive of ;the solar system, taken by the most eminent astronomers of all nations, those showing the interior of the sun and moon being exquisite pro- ductions. The last slide shown illustrated the fine , comet which was seen in the year 1858, the sight of which caused many members of the audience to acknowledge their pleasant recollection of it. The best thanks of the meeting were accorded to Mr Watson, upon the proposition of Mr J Gould, who said the lecturer had taken great pains to obtain many of these fine photographs from the Royal Astronomical Society —Mr Bindloss seconded, and the meeting closed with the singing of the Doxology.</p>
