      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WARRINGTON EXPERIBNCR HARD LUCK AT SWINTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON EXPERIBNCR HARD LUCK AT SWINTON.</p>
        <p>Though Swinton have been going very strongly of late the Warrington men went away with the intention and ex- ' peetation of defeating the Blues on their territory, and they came very near accomplishing so unusual a feat. Swinton's margin woe only that of a penalty goal at the finish and 1 Warrington hod played a man short nearly the whole of the seeend half. Botts sides were well represented. Swinton 1 turning out at full strength, and Warrington being without 1 Hellam. who was taken ill during the previous night, Rat- elide filling' the vacancy. The weather was fine, but hazy, and this being the only match in the Manchester district a crowd numbering about 7,010 turned up to witness the con- flict. Mr Bruckshaw of Stockport was the referee, and the teams lined out as follows : Swinton : A Chorley ; V Hampton, It Valentine. W Wall- work, and J Cooper ; D Davies and F Morgan ; H Barnett, J Preston. C Pollitt, ii Yates, J B.ackburn, H Tickle, T Pom- fret. and J Evans. Warrington Ratcliffe ; Fish. Isherwood, Dickenson, and Harris : Burgess and Allen ; Morrison Boardman, F Swift, W Swift, 0 Jolley. J dodos?. Edmondson, and Taylor. It Valentine started the game for Swinton down the slope, and Chorley was early prominent with two marks. 'the visitors' forwards lost much ground through off Ado play. From a passieg movement Wallwork ran through the thick el his opponents, and with no one to pass. Cooper failed2to take the bell only two yards from the :ins, a certain try brine missed. Burgess ran cleverly from the ecrummage. easily eludl ig Morgan aid Davies,but pas e wildly to Fish, the ball going into'touen. Davies and Morgan started several splendid bouts of passing. but Cooper failed to take his passes and failed again on the line. Burgem was playing in fine style for ',Farrington, and repeatedly saYed his Side. Fr mm a splendid pass- ing 'movement hcwever, between Davies,Korgan, Wall- work, sae Cooper the last-named had a couplets walk- over. Bob Valentine failing at goal From the kick out, Warrington were penalized for off-side, and play ruled for a time at half-way. Lob Valentine had his k.ck charged down and it seemed as if the visitors would score, but Hampson ran across and savel in splendid style. From the scrummage Burgess kicks I across to Fish, who gathered the ball on the bounce and, eluding Hampton. romped over the line, the same player failing in his attempt at goal. Immediately after Morgan ran through the thicklisf his opponents and gave to Cooper, who again scored, Valentine failing at goal. Harris and Cooper were prominent for their respective sides with good runs, but the tackling on both 'Bees wee very keen and the players ware not allowed to travel far without being well looked after, A slight stoppage pieurred through Bo trd- man coaling in contact with the Swinton left wing, Cooper being badly shsken. Bob Valentine, in running round his opponents, was Burt, but resumed Hampton ran down the popular side and looked like a scorer, but Fish brought him down. The game was of a very fast description, the com- bination of both teams being splendid. Harris received from W Allen and Dickenson, and when he reached Chorley he kicked over the letter's head, and following up at top speed scored a really clever try, thus putting the sides on equal footing, as Fish missed thegoal from all easy position. Reif- time s Swinton, 6 points; Warrington, 6 polite. J. Swift restarted and ?Farrington were early pressing,and it took the home team all their time to keep them out. W. Swift. was here ordered off the field for etriking in the scrim- mage when his side were peppering the Swinton line. Cooper relieved with a good rsopand !lampoon was applauded for a splendid catch. Fish lost ground through appealing for off-tide play when Waliwork had clearly pet his men on side, Swinton were now warming te their work, and gave the visitors an anxious ten minutes. Morrison was bowled over by a Swinton fer ward, ant in the fracas that ensued the referee pen:intact Warrington wrongly, and Evans to the Joy a the home supporters put the leather over the bar,thus put- tin Swinton bwepoints ahead. Warringtoe W tre ha alicepped in :.the scrimmages but worked pluckily,Fish in particular. putting in seine fine runs and kicks. Swinton hewerer,were often iu the Warrington half. Conner once running half the length of the field before being brought down. Nearithe close both ;wings attempted to cross and Hampson only failed through putting his feet in. touch Final, Swinton, 1 goal 2 tries ,8 points); Warrington, 2 tries (6 points).</p>
        <p>LEIGH V. IiVNCOR,T. _ _</p>
        <p>At Leigh before 3,, 0) spectators. Danhavin and Johnson were still absent from the Leigh team, whilst Houghton, of Runcorn. did not turn out Buncorn started, and Bennett replied, but Leigh were immediately penalised for off-side, and the first scrimmage was forward at the Leigh quarter. The booze forwards got possession In the pack,and Melyneux cleared his lines by a long kick, a burst of running on the part of Jolley, Itiehardson and Butterworth looked danger- ous, but Horan knocked en the final transfer. Leigh were penalised, and Farmer tried at goal from a good position, the leather falling short. The Leigh backs got their passing machinery in motion. and after the ball had passed through the hands of Hilton, Molyneux and Powers, it reached Davies, who gallopei strongly for the line, but could not pass Wilkinson. A moment later Davies was again served and once more he overcame all opposition except Wilkinson who tackled him finely. By long kicking and good follow- ing up, Runcorn paid a momentary visit to the Leigh and. but fine puntiag by Pearson drove them b.ck: A dribble by Causer and Bennett looked dangerous, but they lost the ball and' Wilkinson removed the dsnger by a big kick. Leigh dribbled back again, and from a scrimmage in the Runcorn quarter, Molyneux dropped a fine goal for Leigh. Runcorn paid another flying visit to the Leigh end and after much passing between Jolley an 4 Butterworth, Flanagan tried to score but was felled by Bennett. Davies. again galloped down the field, only to be knocked Into touch near the line and at half-time Leigh led by one goal to nil. Runcorn showed greatly imprond torus in the second half their lofty punting and following up keeping Leigh chiefly on the defensive. Davies,missed catching the ball near the Leigh quarter flag, and six Runcorn forwards had a glorious opportunity of scoring, as they had no one between them and the line. Bennett, however, rushed across the field and ' dropned on the ball, and averted a certain try. Runcorn swarmed round the Leigh goal line, and as their forwards were now beating Leigh for possession a score before long seemed Inevitable. Quick passing gave Flanagan a chance, but Bennett collared him, and then the other wing got going finely, brit Davies cleverly intercepted the Anal pass and saved his side from disaster. Attacks by Norbury, Hilton, and tdolyneux prayed very useful to Leigh, and Davies at length ran the ball to centre, and the long tension came to RR end, for as the result of fine combination by Causer, Bennett, Towers. and Molyneux, the Runcorn quarter was reached, and the whistle soon afterwards went, Leigh thus winning for the first tine shore December 7th. Final,:— Leigh, one dropped goal (two points) ; Runcorn, nil. BRADFORD v. BROUGHTON RANGERS DEFEAT OF TICE CHAMPIONS.</p>
        <p>The Rangers journeyed to Brad'or to p'ay their return match with that club. The greatly improved form of the home team made the fixture much more attractive, and frilly 16 003 people assembled so see the game. Unfortun- ately for the Rangers they were without the services of Hogg ar_d his abseaoe considerably weakened both their attack and defence. On the other hand Bradford were strongly represented. and they played such a capital game that in the end the Rangers suffered their second defeat this season. The opening stages were greatly in favour of the Rangers, and the ball was quickly taken across the home line by Oram, but no score resulted, owing to the ball having been previously knocked forward. Maredec and Murgatroyd even- tually placed Bradford in a good positien, and both Mosby and lingers tried to place penalty goals, but were unsuccess- ful. W James also missed placing goals from two attempts. and when half-time arrived nothing had been scored. The second half has only been in progress a short time when Marsden and Heseltine commenced a rapid passing move- ment, whieh ended in Bagers scoring a try, which Mosby Improved. The game then became rather rough, and the referee had to order one of the Rangers' forwards off the field. Bradford attacked st-ongly, and Marsden scored an unimproved try, the final score being—Bradford, one goal and two tries (eight Feints); Broughton Rangers, nothing. SALFORD V IHUNSLET. This return match took place at Hunslet, before about 5,000 spectators. Lomas i,svas in !splendid form, and scored all the points obtained 11 the match. lie dropped two goals in the second half and also scored a try, which he converted, and Salford won by tour goals and one try (eleven points) to Hunslet one try (three points). - - - -</p>
        <p>BRIGHOUSE BANGERS V HUDDERSFIELD. At Brighoose, before 7.000 epeetators. Marsden threw wide to Kitchen, who literally walked in with an easy try. Chapel haying left his side unprotected. Kitchen failed at goal. Huddersfield pressed and Marsden scored under the posts, but Larard failed at goal. Result r—Hudderafield,two tries (six mints); Brighouse, one gad (two points). HULL V BATLEY. Playing ow their own ground. before 6,C0) spectators. Hull early in the game, through the agency of Jacques. landed a penalty goal. The borne team penned Batley throughout the greater part of the first half. The defence, however, proved equal to the strain. After the interval Batley gave a better display, and the game was strenuously contestml. Batley placed a penalty goal, and towards the close Batley pressed, Hull's display being disappointing. Result :—A drawn game of two points eaoh. HALIFAX V OLDHAM. At Halifax, before 10,000 people. Prater started, and there being no reply Oidham attacked. The home backs cleared to centre, where Little landed a splen- did penalty goal. Manias now had the advantage, and from a pass by Morley Itigg scored, but Little failed to convert. Immediately afterwards Malllnson, Morley, and Riley co- operated, Riley scoring a try iter a grand run. Little again</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>UTE EXAMINER FRIDAY, JANUARY 24 1902</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <p>failed Brady missed a penalty, but afterwards Halifax attacked strongly. Half-time score :—Halifax. eight points; Oldham, nothing. On resuming Oldham made repeated visits to the home citadel. A most dangerous rush was saved by Little, and then a penalty resulted in Oldham's first score. Thomas kicking a goal. Strive as they would Oldham could not get much in hand, the home tactics being sharp and clever. Oldham were penalised, and Little kicked a goal. Result :—Halifax. two goals a ud, two tries (10 points) ; Old- ham, one goal (two points).</p>
