      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>NEW VICAR OF ST. PAUL'S.</head>
        <p>THE NEW VICAR OF ST. PAUL'S.</p>
        <p>The Rev W Bracecamp, formerly curate-in-charge at St. Peter's Church, Warrington, and now curate at St. Barnabas, Sheffield, has been appointed t) the living of St. Paul's Warrington, in succesaion to the Rev E R Tarbuck, 8.A., who has accepted the living of St. George's Altrincham. It will be remembered that Mr. Bracecamp left War- rington a very short time ago to t .ke up the curacy of S:. Barnabas, Sheffield. On leaving he was the recipient of a handsome illuminated address from the Vicar and church officers. He was very popular in St. Peter's parish, and many of his Warrington friends will cordi- ally welcome his return to the town, to take un his new and more important sphere of labour in St. Paul's parish. St Paul's Church was consecrated on the 31st of October, 1831, so that the church is now 70 years old. Ten years later it was formed into a separate parish. The incumbents have been as follows :-1831 to 1857, Rev Thomas Lowe ; 1857 to 1863, Rtv William Wood ; 1863 to 1872, Rey Dr Massingham ; 1882 to 1890, Rev S ilkinson ; 1890 to 1899, Rev H V Pigot ; 1899 to 1901, Rev ER Tatbuck. The Vicar of the Parish also fulfils the duty of chap- lain at the Workhouse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WARRINGTON ASSOCIATION F.C.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ASSOCIATION F.C.</p>
        <p>DISBANDED.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Association Football Club has been formally disbanded. The members resolved to take this step on Thursday last week when a special meeting was held at the Patten Arms Hotel, to consider the position of the club and decide as to future operations. Mr. W. L. Williams presided over a fair attendance.</p>
        <p>Ira opening the proceedings, the CHAIRMAN said that the Committee at their last meeting decided to call a special meeting of members to consider as t 3 what the future of the club should be. They carefully weighed and took everything into consideration, and they found it was impossible for them to carry on the club in the manner in which it ought to be carried on unless they received a larger share of public support. They found in the meantime that they had a debit balance of some- thing like £l5, and that together %dal the expenses that were likely to be incurred in the remaining away matches would bring the total deficit up to about £5O towards the end of the season. They had a match to play with Workington which would cost at least from £lB to £2O, and other away fixtures which remained to be played , were Southport, Bacup, Darwen and Rochdale. The ' Committee could oot see their way clear to go on without taking the members of the club into their confidence, GO they decided to call a meeting to ask them what their opinion was. Woen he first came to Warrington, some- thing like nine or ten years ago, the Association game was in its infancy, but lie could very well remember that the keen rivalry which existed between St Elphin's Clutathe Warriogton Cricket Club,and the then Warring ton Club,wae euch that enabled either of these clubs to realize a gate of from £lO to £2O. The largest gate which the club had had this season had realized only about £7 104, and that was in the Southport Central match. Teey had to play Barrow away which cost over £l3 and in the return match they took a gate of only £4 153. They would therefore understand that in the position in which they were placed it was impossible to go on. He had before him the balance sheet and he would read one or two item'. The donations they had received amoucted to £9 83 63, members' subscriptions, £l7 19a, the net proceeds from the draw at the com- mencement of the season £25 4; 101, and total gate re- ceipts or all matches played at home £23 33 id. The chief items of expenditure were incurred in players' wages and travelling eepenses which amounted to £53 133. Other expenses included £2l for the ground ,£1 15; 61 ford awing up the lease Sze. of the ground, and £8 3.3 entrance fee to the Lancaehire League. There was a deficit of about £9. It now reeted with the meeting as to what should be done in the future. Mr ELLIOTT eaid that as an old committee man he had faced the music before and it was very hard to keep asking ;he members of the deb for subscription&amp; Be thought that when they had once paid 5s it was suffi- ci-nt. There were mane' things against the Association game in Warrington. The Rugby code was going very strong and in his opinion always would do and the ground of the Association Club was not suitable from a spectator's point of view. They could not force the members of the club to pay any more. The committee had a certain amount of pity for the few Sup- porters of the game in Warrington and he was sorry to say the supporters were not sufficient to keen the club going. They had to face at least £5O or £6O before they could finish the season and if they were going to carry the club to a successful issue what were they going to Jo to get the£6o together? If they could not raise the money they would have to disband. Mr F BATE said the only thing they could do was to disband. He moved a resolution that the club be formally disbanded. Mr. ELLIOTT 3ecoreled and said he was very sorry to see it had come t that. He had been a loyal supporter of the game ever since he came to Warrington, and be thought at one time that they would have risen to the occasion but his hopes bad always come to the same end. There were not the people in Warrington to sup- port the game as it should be supported. Mr Elliott went on to complain of people who posed as the pioneers of the game in Warrington and yet would not support thelclub,acd said the Warrington and District Association had been throwing dirty water into their faces. The motion was unanimously agreed to. On the motion of Mr Catterall seconded by Mr R Dick the balance sheet was passed and a nommittee was ap- pointed to deal with the deficit of £9. • In thanking the members for their attendance, the Chairman said he hoped that if anything was attempted to revive the game in Warrington they would not fail to rally rotted it in future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Children's League of Pity. A meeting of the Warrington branch of this league which has for its object the enlisting of the young and happy children of the land in the service of the unhappy was held at the Town Hall on Saturday afternoon when an address was delivered by Miss Parton, of Wimbledon. Miss Broadbent,of the ldollies, Latchford, who continues to take a great interest in the work, briefly introduced Miss Parton to the members present. Miss Parton, in her address. said she was sure the members of the Warrington branch would do their best. to spread the work in the town. Tie local inspector was greatly in need of extra assistance, and she hoped they would do their best to raise sufficient money to justify the appointment of another inspector. In the matter of clothes, &amp;c. he was also in need of help, and he would gladly receive any articles. which they might give, The society had recently had a gift of £lO,OOO, for the purpose of building new offices in London. There were 100 members of the league in Warrington, and she was sorry to say that the sums raised by them were decreasing , instead of increasing, In the firm, sear they col- Ilec'ed £42 and that had gone down to £22, which was last year's figure. There were many children in this district who needed taking care of,and there was quite sufficient work for another inspector. In conclusion she asked her hearers to do as Much as they possibly could in the Inatter ef raising funds to help on the work of the League. At the close, several new Members were enrolled, and afternoon , tea, which was served by Mrs Powis, having been partaken of, the assembly dispersed. Accident at Cockhedge Mills. A workman named John Pollard, who resides at 14, Clare's Buildings, Lythgoes-lane, and is em- ployed as a grinder at Cockhedge Mills. was follow- ing his employment at the mills at 7,45 on Monday morning when he was caught by one of the belts. His arm was broken and injuries to his head were sustained. He was taken to the Infirmary and detained as an in-patient.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
