      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>THREE MONTHS WITHOUT SLEEP.</head>
        <p>THREE MONTHS WITHOUT SLEEP.</p>
        <p>Stranger Experience of a Preston Woman. ASINGULAR experience was that of a Preston woman, Mrs Helm, of No. 6, Ernmett-street,who did not sleep either night or day ter three months. That she survived a strain so severe is remarkable, as many persons have succumbed in a much shorter period of distressing insomnia. Mrs Helme's ease created a let of interest among all who heard of it. Speaking to a reporter about it, she stated "Yes, it is quite true," said Mrs Helene in answer to the questions of the reporter, "that I never. slept for three mouths. I can tell you that I was so bad that I used to come downstairs in the middle of the night and wander about the house in a distracted state. Of course I got weaker and weaker, and was at times per- fectly helpless. When I went into tho street, which was very seldom, the neighbours used to say, ' Why, what's up ? ' At last I took to my bed for rood as I thought, as I never expected to got up again."</p>
        <p>HELME. (From a photograph by Bleeedall Preston.) ' "And had you no medical attendance." "Oh, yes, I had two doctors, one after the other, and the second one raid I must get up and drag myself, however painful it might be, into the street, it I wanted to live. My vitality had been reduced 60 low, and I wan in such an extremely depressed condition that I did not care to live. I was so low that I wanted nobody to come and see me." " Flow long had you been ill ? " "I had been suffering on and off for six years with a bad stomach, indigestion, weak action of the heart, and weakness generally, and I grew worse and worse, till my nervous system seemed to be completely wrecked." "Did not the doctors do you any good ? " "They gave me some relief, but I felt that I should never get bett de" "Then how did you come to regain your apparently healthy condition? You certainly don't look much depressed now." " Oh, no," retorted Mrs Heinle, with a bright laugh. 1. I'm all right now. I sleep well, and have a fair appe- tite, and think that life is worth living." " We never thought she would live," now put in her husband, "but one day some papers came to the house. We read them, and a friend c ime in and said, ' Why don't you give them a trial ?' referring to an article in the papers about Dr William' pink pills for pale people." ‘• We took the advice and sent for a box," said Mrs Helme. resuming the narrative. "It came, but I could not bring myself to take any till the day afterwards." "Did you derive any benefit from taking them ? " I soon began to feel much brighter, and I cared for my food." " Can you tell me how soon a decided improvement set in ? " " Well, I should say that it was about the' second or third box. Before I began to take them, you must remember, I was at my very lowest ebis. For five years I had not been able to do anything, but I per- waled with the pills, and now, as you see, I urn well and strong again. I take them on and off yet when I feel a little out of sorts. Ma indigestion has left me and the action of my heart has grown much stronger, while the distressing nervousness, which prevented me from sleeping, has entirely gone, and I can now sleep and rest well. Altogether I feel a different personoind my friends were greatly surprised at my rapid cure." " Are you willing that this testistiony should be pub- lished ? "Ye., Much good has been done to me by Dr Williams' pink pile, and I hope that others may benefit by my experience. My husband can also testify as to what they have done for me. I recommend them to everyone now." It only remains to be said that Mrs Helme is forty- nine years of age, and is the wife of a heald maker.</p>
