      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>FATALITY AT STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>THE FATALITY AT STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>A Dangerous Road.</p>
        <p>At the Red Lion Inn, Stockton Heath, on Saturday morning, Mr R Dobson, district coroner' conducted an inquest relative to the death of Florence Kirkland, aged six, who lived with her parents at 24, Leonard-street, Scockton Heath, and was killed by being run over on the previous Thursday. Lucy Kirkland, wife of Frederick Kirkland, said the deceased was her daughter, and was nearly six years of age. She was a scholar at the church school. Witness sent her to school on Thursday about twenty minutes past one, she being quite well. She heard of her death at halt-pa/It oae,f corn a little girl named Eva Maddock She thought her daughter was dead when she saw her. Ellen Juli in, wife of Edward Worrell Julian, 27, London-road, said that on Thursday she was standing at the door of her shop, when an organ grinder, who was a foreigner, came into the shop. He was , begging land she sent him away saying that she would send for the police. He went to tha opposite side of the road in!the gutter grinding his organ. The monkey, which was attached by a chain or cord, was on the ground. There were about thirty children round the organ. The man pulled the cord and the monkey ran out towards the children, who dispersed in all directions. The 'bus cams up at that moment. Mrs Ls on's (Appleton Hall) bus following immediately behind it. She saw the hind wheel of the latter go over f„the A child's head. The little one was picked up and taken to Mr Rathbone's shop. She (witness) did not think the coachman driving Mrs Lyon's vehicle, could see the child. The convey- ance was stopped, and Mrs Lyon came out to make inquiries. Both the vehicles were going towards Stock- ton Heath. The monkey was on the footpath, when the cord, which was about three yards long, was pulled by the man. Harold Bernard, 46, Gaskell-street, Stockton Heath, said that about half-past one on Thursday he was pro- ceeding down Old Landon-road towards Warrington. and saw a carriage coming up in th 3 opposite direction to which he was going. There were a number ef children in the road. As the carriage got almost level with the children they made a rush across the road. He did not see the organ. He heard the children scream, and the carriage lurch, and both tlie wheels on the church side pass over the child. Ha raised the child's head, and she was taken by a man , named Holmes to Mr Rathbone's shop. The road was rather narrow, and at the time of the accident it was nearly full of children. Mr Whitley, a juror, said he had known of four fatal accidents near to that part of the road. . _</p>
        <p>William Wilkins, coaehman to Mrs Eyon, said he was coming from Warrington on the Thursday. Be saw a crowd of children en hi right hand siae. was quite clear on his lett, and seeing tle steadied the horses. Just as he waschildren monkey roadh e passing caused the children to disperse. The deceatied suddenly a the rk ran backwards from the organ right into his carriage The deceased appeared to fall underneath the middle of Lis 'nue. Be tried to pull up but could not do so. It was done so suddenly that be had not sufficient warn- ing to do anything. The hind wheel went over the child and he immediately stopped, The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death," exonerating the coachman from all blame. Mr. Rose said that several accidents had occurred near the same spot, ad pointed out that a representa- tion ought to be made to some authority, with regard to the narrowness of the road which was scarcely suffi- cient for the ordinary traffic of the district, and also for the safety of children. The Coroner, on behalf of the jury, expressed sympa- thy with the parents of the deceased in their sad loss, and heped that expression would be conveyed to them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>DEAFNESS AND NOISES IN THE HEAD AND EARS.</head>
        <p>DEAFNESS AND NOISES IN THE HEAD AND EARS.</p>
        <p>The last issue of the " Journal for the Deaf " des- cribes some remarkable cures of Deafness and noises in the head by treatment according to the methods of Dr. Drouet, a wel.-known physician of the Paris Academy of Medicines. For the benefit of those who are inter- ested in the subject, an extract from the records of one cane is r2precluced here. This is the case of a proles- , siotal nurse, Jane Radbourne, of Lambourne. This patient suffered many years from a severe form of Deaf- ness caused by Catarrh of the middle ear, but the anhoyance from the noises in the head was still more troublesome ; in fact, the noises in the head affected her general nervous system to such an extent that she was quite unfitted for work. Ihe cure resulting from the use of the Drcust Treatment is related in the LI-lowing letter :</p>
        <p>SiAiabury Cottage, Larnbcurne. Oct. 26 1901</p>
        <p>I am pleased to thank you for your kindness to me. I was troubled with noises in my left ear for over twelve months, which made me feel quite P. At times I thought I should lose my reason. I was able to do but very little work. Now, lam thankful to say, I can work day with pleasure. I am not like the same woman, and my hearing is quite perfect. Yours very truly_</p>
        <p>JANE RADBOURNE.</p>
        <p>Those who are desirous of seeing the reports ot up- wards of 200 latf•ly cured cases ot Deafness and ear, nose, and throat diseases by the Drouet treatment are referred to the " Journal for the Deaf," a free copy of which can be obtained by cost on app:ication to the secretary. Drouet Institute, Regent's-park-road, Lon- don, N.W.</p>
        <p>Each journal is accompanied by. a Patient's Report Form which enables applicants to receive full partiulars for applying the treatment at home. any advice by , correspondence is absolutely free.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON -60-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Trading' of the Past Year.</head>
        <p>The Trading' of the Past Year.</p>
        <p>The mcrnbe rs of the Warrington Equitable lodustrial Co-operative Society, Limited, held tmir annual meet- ing in the Co-Dperativo Hall, Sankey-street, on 1141enday evrning. Mr T Birchen presided and the committee included, Alderman Shaw Gzen, auditor, and Mr A H Rathbone, managing secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>ANNUAL REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>The report and balance sheet were taken as read. We give the following extracts from the report to the mem- bers :—** Your committee have pleasure in submitting the following statement for your approval. The sales for the quarter amount to £30,402 193 101 being an increase of £3,309 las 51 over last quarter and £3,735 43 71 above the corresponding quarter of last year. The profits, alter providing for all liabilities, isterest on capital amounting to £5Ol 19s,depreciition on properly izc.j will allow a dividend of 34 in the £ on members' purchases. Our Lovely-lane branch is still maintaining e progress referred to lest quarter. A. glance at the cash account will show the steady increase that is being made. September quarter bAniy, twelve weeks, the sales amounted to £3,735 133 Id ; this qusrter being Lthi e usual thirteen weeks, the sales amounted to £4,834 . .</p>
        <p>In 9i, being an increase of £1,048 13s 81. Your com- mittee are much encouraged with the result. Out Stockton Heath members will be pleased to know than we take possession of the property in that district towards the end of Februsry, when we shall proceed at once with alterations to suit our requirements,and trus the members of the district will use their influence with friends in the neighbourhood to make this, our fifth broach, a complete success.</p>
        <p>on tne tnotiod ot the PRESIDENT, esconded by Mr W. JEFFREY, the report and balance sheet were adopted.</p>
        <p>CHAIR3UN'S ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>The PRESIDENT obi,erved that that was their 42 id annual meeting, and in this connection he gave some interesting information as to the progress of the society. He could remember the time when their capital was about £lOO ; now it was £56,000. That was certainly a step forward. In the early days they had a little un- plaetered warehouse in Cairo-street, and now they pos- cooed six or eight of the best freeholl blocks of pro- perty in Warricgtort and the neighbourhood, showing that the society was making sutstantial progress. For the quarter just ended there was an increase of £3,309 over the previous quarter ia the sales. These were fair samples of the headway cc-operation was making throughout the country ; it was, in fact, increasing by leaps and bounds. (applause.) He contended that this society was one of the most valuable societies for work- ing men that the town poisessed.and it was pleasing to him to notice that there was an increased tendency to thrift. He (the President) was a manager of the Savings Bank which was a most excellent institution for those who wanted to save small sums and who were not members ef a enciety like this. Over £360,000 passed through the Savings Bank as capital saved by the people of Warrington. (Applause.) Then there was the Post Office Savings Bank, and then our bank which was as safe as the Bank of Enland. Other ieformotion was given, and the Chairman concluded by observing that this society did its fair share in helping on the benevo- lent work in Warrington by its subscriptions from year to year. (Applause.) PRESIDENT FOR 36 TI3IES IN SUCCESSION. Mr BIRCHALL having vacated the chair, it was taken by Mr JEFFREY. 19119 proposed the re-election of the re- tiring president, of whom he spoke in high terms. Mr Massias seconded, and the motion was heartily agreed to. Mr BrecireLL, in returning thanks, raid his position</p>
        <p>was unique. There we/a nearly 2,000 Co-operative Societies in the kingdom, but there was ust another President who had been re-elected for 36 times in suc-</p>
        <p>cession, as he (the speaker) had been. Thera was a similar case in another town last year, but tho Presi• dent had since died, and him Lest bad been contested on several occasions.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr EIOIMEITON, seconded by Mr JNITHEY, Mr J Shaw Green was unanimously re-3lected auditor of the society. Some business of a routine character was then trans— acted.</p>
        <p>1,7))</p>
      </div>
