      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <p>VOLUNTEER RESIGNATIONS.</p>
        <p>The Adjutant States the Truth of the Matter.</p>
        <p>The Spirit of the Battalion.</p>
        <p>Warringtonians have come to look upon the local Volunteer Battalion as teing in the forefront as regards efficiency and it was therefore natural that considerable surprise should be:occasioned by the publication in &amp;morning contemporary of a re- pqrt that one hundred arid twenty resignations bad taken place in the Corps, and that this was " as- cribed in some quartera to the feeling that there would be considerable diffisulty among the local volunteers in meeting the requirements of the new War Office regulations es to efficiency." The announcement appeared, in a somewhat modified form in a local newspaper and in order to ascertain the exact truth of the matter an " Examiner" re- porter waited upon the Adjutant of the Battalion (Major F ii Boerne). Major Bourne at once expressed his willingness to supply the required information. he number of resignations, he said, had been one hundred and twenty, "But," added the, Major, "these ara the usual annual resignations and the percentage is smaller than .in previous years, all things con- sidered." Continuing Major Rooms informed our reporter that for the past two years members of the bat- talion had been holding back their resignations on account of the war. Tney felt it would not be honourable to resign during the war, but now that the campaign was drawing to a close they had sent in their resignations. Thus there were the resigna- tions of practically three years in one. The Adjutant also pointed out that the strength of the battalion had increased very much of late. Out of a total of 1,182 there were 120 resignations so that it would be seen that th'e percentage was very small, in fact it was smaller now than ever it had been. Speaking of the new regulations the Major said the commanding officer was perfectly sure thnt the men would comply with tee regulations. " In fact" he continued "it is no more tean they had to do last year. They had to put in ten drills before camp then and this year they will have to do the same. Trio Commanding Officer does not care to get men who cannot or won't do it." Again referring to the causes of resignations Major Rome said that men were continually leaving town and there were always some each year who wanted to resign. " But "he added, " I don't believe there are half a dozen in the whole lot who left on account of the new regulations. Tne spirit of the men is to be right up to the top—second to none. That is the spirit of the regiment." Major Roome also called the attention of our reporter to the Order on the subject issued by the commanding officer in which the, following pass- ages occur :—" The Commanding Officer is fully assured that the N.C.O.'s and men of the Battalion will loyally and thoroughly carry out these new conditions of efficiency, which, if extlinhaedmerefully, will be seen to be not more difficult toperform than the amount of work which has hitherto been carried out annually by the battalion', tine which has brought it to its present position in the front rank of the Volunteer Force, in the 30th Brigade of the Field Army. The com- manding officer is certain that every man under his command is determined to do his best not only to maintain, but to improve the high standing and good name of the battalion during the present year, so is confident of the result." •• The men think with the Colonel on the subject, I am cure they do" said the adjutant "01 course you won't find twelve hundred men without a few grousers among them; but we don't want that class of men." Our readers will be able to gather from the 'above that the matter has none of the special significance attached to it in other qurirters amid there is no cause to fear that the corps will not go on progressing in efficiency in the future as creditably as it has dope in the past.</p>
      </div>
