      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>England V. Australia. The Third Test Match.</p>
        <p>---- Victory of The Australians.</p>
        <p>Adelaide, THURSDAY, 12.0 p.m. I Play in the third test match was resumed this morn- ing in glorious weather. There was only a moderate attendance. The wicket kicked in places. The attack was shared by Jessop and Blythe. Trimble and Dar- ling continued their innings, playing keen but cautious</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>cricket.</head>
        <p>cricket.</p>
        <p>1.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>When Darling had scerad, 69 he was caught at mid-on by Hayward off Tessop's bowling. fie had played splendid cricket. (255 for five, Trumbie no out 29).</p>
        <p>3-p.m. Noble ran himself out after scpring 13. (288 for six Trumble not out 46.)</p>
        <p>3.40 p.m.</p>
        <p>Trumble and Armstrong remained together until the requisite number of runs had been made. Australia winning by four wickets. Trumble was not out 62,and Armstrong nine. Jessop took two wickets for 41., and Gunn three for 88. Braund had 79 runs hit off his bpwling for no wicket, Blythe 66, and Hayward 23.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>---- Captain G. R. Crosfield Again for the Front.</p>
        <p>It is tut a short time since Captain G R CrJefield returned to Warrington &amp;Ror serving at the front with the Manchester Company of Imperial Yeomany, and the Captain has now determined to go out to South Africa a second time. On Monday he left VVarrington for Aldershot for the purpose of joining the 32nd Battalioa Imperial Yeomanry, who will shortly proceed to South Africa. That Captain Crosfield will return home safely in the best of health will, we are sure, be the sincere hope of all Warringtonians.</p>
        <p>Local War Honours. South Lancashire Men's Gallantry. The Commander-ic-Chief in South Africa sends home a further list of officers " brought to notice in concoc- tion with operations." It includes the f :- Ist South Lancashire Regiment.—Lieutenant R C Trousdale, for the plucky way ho worked the Maxim gun during the attack on Fort Itala on 26th September, 1901, after both the men with him had been killed. Lieutenant Trousdale served in the 3rd South Lanca- shire Regiment (Militia) before joining the " Fighting Fortieth." let South Lancashire Regiment. —3972 Colour-Serge- ant Duffy, though vg ounded early in the action, con- tinued to fight, and exercised great control and influ- ence over his men. let South Lancashire Regiment.-4269 Private Gavin, promoted corporal by Commander-in-Chief, , for con- spicuous gallantry at Blood River Poor: on 17th Septem- ber, 1901, in holding position in face of heavy fire and overwhelming numbers till ammunition was all ex- pended.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>RICHEST COALFIELD IN ENGLAND.</head>
        <p>THE RICHEST COALFIELD IN ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>More Money For Miners Wives. The richest undeveloped coalfield in England is about to be opened up, and the increased work to be obtained will no doubt mean more money for miners and miners' wives. The new coalfield lies east of Rotherham and south of Doncaster and already a pit is being sunk at Dinnington. Quite close to this very spot mere has vecently been accomplished a retnarkable cure by Ch Forde'l Bile Beans far Biliousness. Mr Joseph Harrieon, an engineman at Krlahurst Chemical Wore, and residing at 9, Res.sington-atreet, Denabe Main, is the person who has such good cause to be thankful for his present state of health. A. "Rotherham Advertiaer " reporter who called upon him learned the following facte. Mr Harrison said :—" I have° been a great sufferer from biliousness and indigestion ; and these two complaints were for a long time the cause of much pain, worry and expense. My symptoms were well marked. In consequence of the obstinacy of the indigestion, I was unable to take a sufficient quantity of nutritive food to maintain my strength, and after even a light meal the pain I suffered was often intense. I grew very depressed and low-spirited,—a condition which was aggravated by my inability to obtain any sleep for night after night. My natural buoyancy of mind and body so forsook me that there was no pleas- ure either at home or at work. The doctor visited me and I took his medicine but it was of little use and I still continued in pain. Some weeks ago a friend advised me to try Chaie. Verde's Bile Beans as he said they had cured him of in- digestion and bilieusnese. I did as he recommended. Since then I have had good cause to thank him for his advice : for before long I began to feel that the Beans were effecting a core. I cornme -ced to eat and enjoy my food and gradually I progreesed until now—to use a north-country sportsman's expression—l feel "as well and hearty as a hare." I attribute this exclusively to Bile Beans." Chas. Forde's Bile Beans for Biliousness, which ac- complished the above cure are a certain cure for indi- gestion, biliousness, sleeplessness, colds, chill, rheuma- tism, actemia, constipation, piles, congestion of the liver, headache, neuralgia, fainting fits, attacks of dizzi- ness, flatulence, pains in the chest, loins or back, defective secretion of bile, wasting affections, female irregulari:ies, gout, and the host of ailments having a common origin in impurity of the blend, a general con- gestion of the system and less of vital force. Obtainab'e from all chemists or post free from the Bile Bean Manufacturing Co., 119 and 120, London Wall, London, E.G., upon receipt of prices, 048 ani three half -pence or two and ninepence.</p>
        <p>Sunday School . Teachers' Association.—The</p>
        <p>Rector and Religious Controversy. There was a large attendance at the quarterly 'Sleeting of the Warrington and District Sunday School Teachers' Association which was held in St James' Schools, Latchford,on Monday evening. The Rtv F E Powell presided, and the other clergy pre- were the Rector (the Rev Canon Willis) and the Revs E C E Carleton, L J T Dsrwell, Pollock- Rill, A N Taylor and Vale Owen. The Rector, in the course of his remarks said they had read the papers lately and had seen a little controversy go- ing on between three of them (the clergy) and the ministers of other religious bodies. Mr Carleton, Mr Powell, and he himself had been writing, and the result of this upon his mind had been to make him feel the immense importance of their teachers being thoroughly instructed. Some of them were very well instructed, but there was stilt need for them to attend these meetings, be- cause of the effect of their example upon others. They wanted to have next time not exactly a paper but an instruction to be followed by a:discus- sion, free and open to all. They were not afraid of any inquiry. They did not want anything to be taught that they were not perfectly sure would stand criticism. The subject decided for the next meeting was " The Church." The meeting would be held in April in Fairfield Schools. He would endeavour to get a competent man to deal with the subject, simply, plainly and fully, and he hoped everyone would say in the discussion just what they thought. So they might feel they were doing real good. Anxious as he had been that the subject should be as comprehensive as possible he did not want to make it so comprehensive that faith and truth should be watered down. lle would rather lose the good he had spoken of, for he wanted so much in his day and generation to try and teach the truth as far as he po4sibty could. (Applause.)— The Rev LJ T Darwall (Vicar of Walton) read a Paper entitled "A labour of love." That phrase, he thought, exactly represented what Sunday school work was when it was rightly understood—a labour of love on the part of those who gems their time And trouble not only freely and willingly but in- telligently. Sunday schoole were what teachers made them. The rev gentleman proceeded to give the teachers some excellent advice as to the pre- paratien of lessons, the value of a good example to the scholars, and discipline. At the close a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr Darwall for his paper, on the motion of Councillor A J Jolley, seconded by Mr Stansfield. TEUSS.—New washable waterproof is the most suitable for tropical climates ; it is light, clean, cool . and durable; can be worn whilst bathing. WHITE WEIGHT csmfortable. Surgical Instrument Makers. 93. Renshavr-straet, opposite Top of Bold-street. Llyerpool.—Advt. SALE commencing Wednesday neat. A grand opportunity for securing smart Millinery. Jackets. Mantles Waterproofs, Dresses, gc. ecc, at half price. See windows in Senkey Street and Legh Street. W T W ULLA 319. —Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <p>, • „ ; .: • '4.4. "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>MUNICIPAL TRADING.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL TRADING.</p>
        <p>The Penny Donkey.</p>
        <p>The cheapest donkey I ever bought in my life was that; he cost me one penny only. Perhaps you'll think he was only a toy,but you're mistaken; he was a proper one ; a real live one, can imagine I hear someone enquire. How did you manage to buy him so cheaply : It was in this way. I was walking along Market-street, Manchester one day, when I espied a beautiful donkey, grezing. on the I footpath in front of a barber's shop. His hide was as smooth as silk, and he'd such a merry twinkle in his eyes, and such a winning smile on his sweet face, that, being a lover of innocence, and an ad- mirer of all things beautiful, I found myself strok- ing him before I realty knew what I was doing. While thus occupied, a merry-faced lad came up to me. 'Will you kindly purchase a ticket air, they're only apenny each?' I was always very fond of lads, but somehow, this particular specimen of the two- legged species seemed to impress me in a some- what exceptional manner, so I purchased a ticke- without enquiring what it was for. Upon closer , inspection I discovered that it was araffie ticket for a donkey, and that the charming creature that had so suddenly won my affection was the very animal referred to on the ticket.lmagine my delight, I was so highly pleased that, after a few farewell words with my innocent friend I departed for Bella Vise, where I spent several hours in such amusements as boating, fishing, boxing, jumping wrestling, shooting and parachuting. I then re- turned by 'bus to the Central and thence by rail to Warrington. My wife seemed inclined to make fun of me when I showed her my ticket. She said I was softer than she had taken me to be; that I might as well have thrown the penny behind the fire; that the whole thing was a farce, a fraud, a trick and a catch-penny, that the"numbellof toy ticket was 13,784, that she'd never heard of such a thing before as a donkey raffle, and that they were the biggeatNonkeys who encouraged such things by buying tickets See. However, I bore the ordeal with patience, apd with a calm dignity befitting the occasion.. For several days the affair was relegated to the lumber-room of things forgotten, but was at length revived by an interesting item which accidentally caught my attention in the columns of one of the Manchester evening papers. 'I he item was to the effect, that,in the great donkey raffia 13,784 was the winning number, and that if the holder of the said ticket would communicate with SO M, No 82, Makeweight-street,Manchester, be would in due course come in possession of the aforesaid animal. As may be imagined, the effect of this information was semi-electrical. I chuckled and crowed over the affair until my wife actually threatened to take offence unless I became more moderate in my demonstrativeness. The following morning I presented myself and ticket at the door of S G m and after passing through the necessary tests as to validity Sec., took formal pissession of Neddy. It had not occurred to me that I should meet with certain embarrassments before getting him home, but when I arrived at the large streets I found 1 had an awkward task in hand. I could not for shame to ride through the city, and I had , also a secret fear in my mind that he might hack with me into a shop window or that he might take to wincing and leaping. So I took hold of his bridle and led him gently along, and at fr quent intervals encouraged him by a few soothing words such as: Whoa lad ! Whoa! Come up Neddy lad! Back. Whoa ! In a very short time I found I had attracted a considerable amount of attention, and several uncomplimentary remarks reached my ears. One ignoramus addressing another said. " Dust see you two donkeys ?" Another ruffian bawled out. "book at ewd Kruger and his donkey, just arrived from th" Transvaal l" 1 was very highly pleased when at length I found myself at the rail- way station, when I at once purchased a ticket tor 'my companion. Upon reaching the platform gate I was refused admission and was told that I should require a horse-box,and that it would cost me three guineas. "Nonsense" said I,"he's only a donkey,he'll sit Quietly by my side'!" "Yea,that would be very nice" said the official,"there woul i be two donkeys together." I was too indienant to respond to the , insult, and was moving quiedy away, when a gen- tleman who had overheard the conversation ad- dressed me in a very abrupt manner. "Am I to un- derstane,"eaid he," that you:are'a Warrington man and are contemplating introducing this innocent creature into an atmosphere that has become notorious foeits unwholesomeness, especially when I considered from a moral and municipal standpoint. I was not a little disconcerted, and gave consent I fear by silence. So assuming a somewhat puritani- cal style of address, he continued. "Is it possible that thou bast sunk so low, that, not content with dwelling in that infamous, that contemptible town thyself, thou would'st fain introduce therein an innocent, sinless creature; a creature that has. I presume been reared in the salubrious, and excep- tionally moral atmosphere of the city-of Manches- ter? Knowest thou not that he will naturally and instinctively shrink from those illegal and immoral fumes of municipal corruption which pollute the very air,even becoming so strong in some instances as to neutralize the ozone that is so generously wafted from Manchester, and that is carriel like an unmerited benediction to the inhabitants of wretched Warrington ;yes borne to them undeserv- ing as they are, on the kindly,placal ,bosom of the ship canal ! His eloquence was becoming a some- what disturbing factor in the situation, so seizing the donkey's bridle, I turned away and left the gentleman, I fear in a rather impohte manner. As I moved out of the station he stormed and shouted, and bshaved in a very excitable fashion. From what I could ear at intervals I gathered that he was a friend of a gentleman whom he described as Manchester's mightiest magician, the great muni- cipal revolutionist. But to this I paid little heed and was glad to get away, I had made up my mind that I would Ciey the Railway Company and ride to Warrington on the donkey's back. So I Walked him to a suburb of the city and then got on his back. To my surprise he went quietly and cauti- ously along. By and bye darkness came on,but at intervals the moon pept from behind the passing clouds, and shone forth from the glorious, starlit heavens with a majesty and beauty that made the little expedition most delightful. I was just heav- ing a sigh of relief,and was hoping that the troubles of the day were at length ended, when at a sudden turn in the road I was brought to realize that a man stood before me, and that his attitude was, to say the least, menacing. At first I feared that he was an apparition, but as he drew nearer I discovered that I was mistaken. He was a gentleman of ro- bust appearance and somewhat past the prime of life. "Halt I halt " cried he in a rather military style; "Who art thou? Whetce comest thou ? Whither guest thou? and whose animal is this ? " said I, "I am Anthony Swash-buckler, the son of Zechariah Swash-buckler, coming from the refined city of Manchester, travalling to the degraded town of Warrington, and this innocent creature is my i friend, that I have had the good fortune to win in a raffle." Said he," Drop thy sarcasm, or I will pun- ish thee as thou deservest. Art thou not ashamed to proclaim thyself a Warrington man? Is thy de- gradation so complete, thar, without trembling, or even blushing, thou canst admit in the presence of my self and thy innocent friend that thou earnest from Warrington? Man, art thou not aware, that at this very moment I that rotten, and contemptible borough is literallyll seething with excitement, through the great and ,1 deserving chastisement which I have administered, ' that only a few days ago the wild shouts of 5,000 men, some inside and some outside Parr Hall de- mended vengeance? What gayest thou to all this?" II assured him that the [information he had given I was new, and to some extent novel, and asked if he "would kindly allow me to pass on. "Nay," said be, "if thou art Ignorant of this, thou art the man, that we, of the No-municipal Trading Committee are in search. of. We desire theelo join our brother- hood, become our friend, and go forth at once to enlighten and instruct the people. If thou wilt only how down to me, and ts my pacified brethren' we will make of thee a man of great power and popularity. Next November thou shalt take the place of one of the 3EI dishonourables ; and if thou newest it, this innocent animal, seeing that he bath two eyes in his head, and two ears wherewith to hear, and a voice that can make itself heard, shall accompany thee. This one condition I must plainly set before thee, that, when thou art established, should a financial point be raised, the settlement of which may in any way appear to profit thy brother or his brother-in-law, thy uncle, cousin, or great- grandfather, thou immediately resign thy position. One privilege, however, will I allow thee, though contrars to the principle that 1 have hitherto laid dawn; thou may'st have a few shares in a certain limited company by _which iron and steel is nianufactured, even though basiness be transacted with the Corporation. (This Concession I have re-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY JANUARY 24, 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY JANUARY 24, 1902</p>
        <p>cently discovered to be necessary and con- venient). But witu limited companies that deal in bill-posting, slates, paper hanging decorating and white-washing thou shalt have no dealings. If thou art Wllflfi to r.gree to this 1 request that preparatory to thy initiation into the new brotherhood, thou remove thy hat, take off thy shoes and with thy face turned to- wards Manchester swear to me by yonder moon that thou wilt denounce and stigmatize whenever possible the place of thy nativity." By this time I was becoming a little impatient and even Neddy was getting uneasy. Said I, "I will nsither swear by the sun, moon or stars, nor will I promise thee what thou requirest." "thou blind fool !" cried he, "thou duckling !" At this point of the proceedings Neddy lost his temper and to my regret kicked the gentleman into the middle of a duck-pond that was close by. I gallantly offered to help him out, but only received a snubbing, so I resumed my journey and left bim to his fate. it was about 10 a m when we passed along Sankey-street and although very tired I was so proud of Noddy that I thought I would exhibit him a little and go some distance around instead of taking the most direct route homeward. So I went straight on through Sankey Bridges and was intending to turn along Ross-lane, but when we arrived at that point where Buttermilk brook flows at right angles to Liverpool-road the animal be- came exceedingly stupid. He had evidently been regaled with a sniff of compound oxygen mixed with those unique elements for which the brook is , famous. After a lot of trouble,discovering that I hadl zild in my pocket,l gave nine lads id each to assist me in carrying him over the stream, but when, half way across he broke loose and casting at me a look of withering scorn as if to say. "Is this what you have brought me from Manchester for ?"set off as hard as he could gallop towards Sankey-street. Upon arriving at Market-gate he took the wrong turn and bolted off by Horsemarket-street in the direction of Win wick. This I learned from the bystanders, for he must have been quite a mile a-head. When I raached the steps leading to the Central Station I could see him returning, so I napped around the corner while he passed by, for to tell the truth, I was afraid to meet his reproachful glances. When he had galloped past, a gentlemen on horseback said be had seen him running at full speed, in the direc- tion of Longford, but that, when about 200 yards from the destructor he began to kick and wince in a most unaccountable manner, and then turning' suddenly around, ran very excitedly in the opposite direction. 13% this time, though anxious to pursue I was quite breathless, so 1 walked quietly to Market-gate and again made enquiries. I was informed that he had rushed madly down Bridge- street, and after running three or four times around the Cromwell statue and braying most pathatically, had gone wincing backwards across the Mersey. I made frequent enquiries ; ran until out of breath, then walked for a while and ultimately arrived at the banks of the Ship Canal. There lay poor Noddy, quiet enough now. His last race was run. He was dead. It appears he had crossed the canal after making several attempts, but it was too much for him. He had survived the successive shocks that his olfactory nerves had sustained at Butter- milk Brook, at Longford and at the Mersey, but had fallen a victim to the deadly emanations of the Ship Canal. As a last tribute of respect I wrote for him the following epitaph :—" Noddy. the Penny Donkey. Died in Warrington, through the action of poisonous odours from Manchester." NABS WOOD.</p>
        <p>generally, I suppose medical men because of the uncer- tainty of their professional engagements could not be expected to undertake municipal duties to any con- siderable extent. But who can doubt that our municipal life is all the poorer for this deprivation? On Boards of Guardians a medical man may render very consider- able service. The Clergy are, I think, better out, as a rule, of political and civic strife. I know that on Boards of Guardians, County and District Councils and School Boards they have often done good and I suppose that every clergyman must judge for himself and of the conditions and circumstances of the place and occasion for his intervention. But unless these conditions and circumstances prove imperative it is better for him to hold aloof. But be this as it may—and public opinion is I think on my side—no such limitation belongs to teachers. Providing their educational fitness and capa- city is assured, and it is a pleasure to see this capacity and fitness increases yearly,teachers ought to be oldirect value in the management of libraries, art and science schemesoiecondary and technical education; and of pupil teachers' centres. But it is net only in that which belongs to pure educational concerns that their influence in civic life may be exerted. For teachers in wur elementary schools have remarkable opportunities for ascertaining much concerning the facts and effects of over-crowding and undefeeding, of the destructiveness to the health of the off- spring of those who are engaged in dangerous trades, of the feebleness of mind and deterioration of character which result from intemperance and other vice.. Given a shrewd, intelligent, sympathetic teacher the information his calling enables him to acquire should be of supreme value to that board or council which deals with sanitary, educational and other civic affairs. Toe CHAIRMAN remarked that the part of the clergy was to be, a " guide, philosopher, and friend " rather than the political "to is." The teacher was loyal in his work. He ;:the speaker) might bring no the young RI his school to he Censervativee,and Mr Upson, on the other hand, might bring up his bays; to be little Liberals but the teacher recognized his duty in that respect, and gave those under his care a certain ameunt of teaching in citizenithip. The subject of citizenship was one to which they might devote mere time. If the War Office would leave them alone, and not insist so much upon military drill in the cold weather they could do a little bit better in that respect. (Laughter.) He did not think they cultivated the spirit of citizenship as they ought, but he trusted they would try to do so more in future. (Hear, hear.) Mr UPSON remarked that Mr Po well had dealt with the question on high graunds. Toe teacher did not seek a prominent place in public life for the gratification of his own vanity, but rather that be might do good and help forward the nest interests of the peeple at large. He believed that that was essentially true. He re- gretted the apathy and exclueiveneae of many of the teachers of Warringtoo. Tau could not get them to attend to these meetings, or to tske that part they ought in the municipal life of the borough ;—but that Was—and he might say that without exaggeration— selfish. St Paul told them that " love seeketh not her own "—I. Coy. xiii, s—and if MIMp teacher were ani- mated by the motive which Mr 13 swell had so admirably put before them that morning, they would be able to do a great deal more good. Allusion was then made to the want of pravis;on for recreation and amusement. There was, he said, absolutely no pro- vision in his part of the town for the recreation of the people, and parents were often iniquitous because of the social conditions under which they lived. Teachers had no interests to serve, and they could put all these questions before the ratepayers in such a way that it weuld cemonl attention, fie was ple,sed that the address of Mr Powell would go to the public through the medium of the press. He proposed that the bet thanks of the mating be passed to him. (Applause ) Mr JEFFREY seconded, and said he reeretted that thoae teachers who wee suffering fro:n apathy had not been present to be aroused to action by the strong expression of opinion from Mr Pawell's lip. It seemed that nothing short of an earth- quake would arouse some of the Warrington teachers. (Laughter.) He believed that if the teachers had repre- sentatives on the Town Council, and if they t )ok their right place in the conduct of municipal life, they would be able to add very largely to the better government of the town and increase the purity of their town life. Be could not for the life of him see why it was that a publican should have a greater knowledge of educe- tioual matters than a teacher. (Laughter and applause.) Mrs STANSFIELD said teachers were looked upon as people who had simply to teach the young reading, writing, arithmetic. That was a position they had held for many years, but she thought their work was much higher and nobler. (Hear, bete.) She hoped teachers would realize their responsibilities in this direction, so that they might do a grand work. If united they might do something, not only for their own advancement but the advancement of others. (App,ause.) Mr BLACKWELL said that if they had a representa- tive on the Town Council a great deal of good weight be done-with regard to workmen's dwellinga. (Applause.) The resolution was carried, and the Rev F E Povrell's reply brought the meeting to a conclusion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Teacher as a Citizen.</head>
        <p>The Teacher as a Citizen.</p>
        <p>nteresting Paper by the Rev. F. E. Powell. The Municipal Trading Agitation.</p>
        <p>A meeting in connection with the Warrington and District Teachers' Association was held on Saturday _ _ . . _ _ _</p>
        <p>morning in the Reathside Boys' School. Mr Elden (the president) was unable to be present and Mr Stansfield (vice-president) occupied the chair. The negotiations which had taken place with refer- ence to the desirability of a representative of the association being appointed by the Corpotation as a member of the Museum _Committee were reported. Letters from the then Mayor (Alderman Roberts), Alderman Monks and the Town Clerk were read, and the results of interviews with them were given to the meeting, from which it appeared that the association had failed to secure a representative. It appeared that the R 99 Morley Stevenson had been appointed a mem- bsr of the committee, but it was stated that what was I wanted was a day school teacher. I Tee CHAIRMAN said it was the duty of the association</p>
        <p>to put themselves in a more powerful position' and they bad the means of doing so. It was said that the teachers were ant educational experts, and when they asked who were, the reply of the committee was " It is all right about elementary education, but we don't deal with it but with technital and secondary educa- tion." If day echool teachers were not experts he should like to know who was. (Hear, hear.) Mr Stansfield suggested that they should " run a teacher as a Town Councillor." It was ultimately decided, on the motion of Mr JEFFREY, seconded by Mrs STANSFIELD, to instruct the 800. Sec. and freasurer (iilr John Lipson) to write to the Museum Comsnittee asking them to appoint one of the representatives of the teachers, a member in the place of the late Mr John Peers. PAPER BY TRH REV F E P3WELL The CHXIRMAN, !called upon: the Rev F E Powell to give an address no " The teacher as a citizen," Th 3 Rev F E POWELL in the course of his paper said : —What is desired is to make citizenship in itself a more prominent end in the life of every citiz 'ult. For over and above a man's duty to himself, and to his own, and in his definite employment in life, he has daties to dis- charge to the community. And my complaint is that these duties are not recognised to anything like the extent that they should be. The fact that when a man does Seek to discharge this duty in any public action he runs a serious risk of being chargeable with some selfish obj;ct is a sad revelation concerning the dearth ot civic spirit in our midst. The mischievous consequence of this miserable condition of things shows itself in-two ways. The man in the street, who has 119 selfish end to gain, is ti meted to acquiesce in his civic indifference. What is be3t for the common good, what will tend to the greatest welfare of his fellow countrymen, what that is amiss in our ercial conditions can be removed or re- medied—these are ideals with which the man in the etrect appears to have little or no cencern. He may form more or less crude opinions as to what ought to be ' done, but the idea of strenuousle exerting himself to accomplish any reform scarcely enters his head. He Can hardly be induced to cross the street to record his vote. Whereas the man who has selfish interests to serve. " axes to grind " ia common parlance, is too often left a clear field to covet and fill such Offices as are within his reach. And if he is too busy or reticent a man him- self he will yet exert himself to put safe men into I power. Who—to take only one instance—cannot see how real is the danger of allowing our whole social life to be largely controlled whether locally or centrally by ethe liquor interest P—What then is our duty—the duty that is of those who recognize these shortcomings and defects in our sods' and pelitical life ? With the best intentions in the world it is not goed that any one class of mind—even though it be so practical and son; sible as the trading mind—should unduly control the affairs of State. I: is probable that Lord Rosebery was right when he suggested the introduction of more men of business into our Cabinet and othe.r cffiees of State. Political and military posts are still too much regarded as the preserve of the aristocracy to the detriment of efficient administration. But in our municipal life the difficulty is entirely the other way. Here menufac- tore and commerce hold undispufed sway, and minds and characters moul -ied under other conditions have not made their influence felt as they might and should have done. The more interests which are represented upon our Town Council the better—not indeed in order that these interests may be direcctly benefited, but that the com- munity as a whole may be served frern every point of view. The object, therefore, which the Municipal Non- trading Committee in formed to promote—viz , the need of civic purity in our town, should appeal to all citizene. How far any members of our Corporation have depar- ted from the path of integrity and probity I make no pretence of knowing. But oeposition to a public inquiry into any suspected malversation taises and con- firms enepicion, for which the obstructionists themselves are alone responsible. The best way to remove sus- picion and to retest charges is to court inquiry. And the result of such an Inquiry no honodrable man will fear But the situation demands provision for the future as well as judicious retresprction. Some system of regu- lation can easily be introduced whether by Parlia- mentary authority 'or with the sanction of individual Corporations, by which improper trading, however much covered by present law, may be effectually and prudently prevented. And there is more hope for this being done if the corporate conscience of the municip- ality is not the common factor of commercial naorall,y only. The common moral denominator is likely to be higher if other besides trading consciences are brought to hear upon public trading concerns.l say this without prejudice to the commercial conscience. I would say the same of the clerical conscience. Is there any special reason why you who are engaged in the science and art of teaching should take a share, and a definite share, in the municipal life of your town ? I think there is. You, with the medical men and clergy of a town, have op. pottunities of forming a just opinion of the needs of a community which other clarses have not. Speaking</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION SUNDAY</head>
        <p>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION SUNDAY</p>
        <p>SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Thew lis-street schoolroom was filled to overflowing on Monday evening os the occasion of the annual tea meet- ing in connection with the Woikingmen's Mi,osion Sun- day school. About 500 of the senior scholars and friends sat down to tea and on Tuesday evening, the junior scholars had their innings and thoroughly enjoyed them- , selves. At the subsequent proeviainos on Monday , evening Mr Arthur LI Grosfiald occupied tne chair. He was accompanied on the platform by Mr N Hough, Mr Ashley, Mr T Dale, Mr Taylor, Mr A 'Tilling,Mr F Grey, Mr Edwards and others. An excellent programme of a miscellaneous character was provided. Miss Burndred contributed a pianoforte solo Tancredi " and Mr Pemberton delighted the audience by a series of selections on a large concert phonograph. Mr T and Mrs Hannan each contributed two solos with taste and skill and Mr S Millington's robust voice was heard to advantage in " The little hero " He also sang in the duet " Excelsior "ia which he was joined by Mr S Hassell, and their voices blended harmoniously. Ties accompaniments were efficiently played by Mr F Atkins on. Mr TILLING presented the :annual report, which stated that the advent of Christmas and the usual festivities of the Cnristm ss season reminded them that the year's work was closed arm therefore it was their duty to present to the pubic of that neighbourhood and to their friends tueir annual report of the working of the institution during the year. As of yore they would at the very commencement place oa record their , sincere thankfulness for the way in which their</p>
        <p>_ Heavenly Father had led and blessed them in their labours. Their numbers had slightly increased during the year. The number of scholars on the books was 261 males, 227 females, total 491, an increase of 20 on the preceding year. The officers would urge upon the Parents of children the great importance of sending their children regularly and punctually to Sunday school and thus give them every opportun- ite of learniog to walk in the narrow path that learleth ant. life. They would also ask parents to question the chiliren at home on the lessons that are taught After all, home influences bad far more effect on the young mini than any other influences. Trio finances of the school showed a balance in hand of £8 17s 2ascl at the end of the year. The sehoel had promised £lO per aenurn towards the support of a native worker in the foreign mission field i.e India and they did not anticipate any difficulty in raising the amount required. With reference to the Band of Hope eciety, never was that institution in a more flourishing condition. They were pleased to know that temperance principles were being put before their ecaolars hi such a con- sistent manner as was done by the Band of Hope Society. Mess C Burch was again pioneering tne movement as secretary and they were looking forward with confidence to the continued success of this important branch of the Sunday school. The Christian Endeavour Srciety was still proving a blessing to the church and to the members of the society. The junior scholare were invited to enrol themselves as mem- bers of the Junior Christian Endeavour Society. A Mutual Improvement Society bad been instituted by the members of the Mm's Bible Class and there was every sign of it being a success. Mr George Crosfield had accepted the position of president and was taking a practical interest in its workings. At the suggestion of the members of thu Mutual Improvement Society, another movement was to be inaugurated for the benefit of the youths of the neighbourhood. It had long been noticed that a great number of boys in the district spent a great deal of their leisure time in playing about the streets. It was thought that it would be a good thing to provide them with the means of spending An enjoyable hour in the schoolrrom one night a week. Games weuld be provided and there would be a good supply of r/ ading realer. If the movement proved to be a saccess, a gym easium would be alerted. The Cricket Club had soother successful season, and was looking forward to still further successes in the coming season. Toe sewing class wee still in existence, but the attendance was not as good as was desired. The officers ft the year were : President, Mr N Hough ; vier-president, Mr T A3hley ; stewards. Mr T Dale and Mr A. Tilling ; secretary, Mr F Grey ; treasurer, Mr Wooiley ;ereenist, Mr F Atkinson. T hey had, in con- clusion, to make referecce to a matter which pained I them t ) mention. Few weeks ago preparations were , being made for the annual Christmas treat, when the sad news came of the &amp;ate of teeir patron, which n eceseitated the pestponement of the treat: Although perhaps, not altogether unexpected, yet the news when it did come was a severe blow to all. They realised that in the death of Mr Crosfield the town of Warrington had lost a friend, and they at Bank efuay, a kind bene- factor—one who took a deep interest in that district and lin that mission, particularly. He not only built the mission but he was always ready and willing to help it in time of need. Never had Mr Crosfield refused hist assistance when appealed to on behalt of the mission. (Hear, hear). His generosity and kindly sympathy gained for him a place in the hearts and minds of the people and one thing was certain, his name would never be forgotten by the people of Bank Quay. The inspira- tion they had received from the life of Mr Crosfield could not fail to simulate the work of the mission and although he had gone his influence would still be valued by them all. The seed had been sown, the result of which only Eternity could reveal. (Applause). The CHAIRMAN (111 e Arthur H Crosfield) who had a meet cordial reception, on rising to speak said : I thank you all very much for your kind welcome this evening and I must congratulate the Secretary on the admirable report he has just read. I eta uld like at the outset, to de as my father used to do and thank the teschers and workers in connection with this institution for the self-denying and really good work that they perform here Sunaay by Sunday—work that he always appreciated and for which their only reward is tee satisfaction they derive from doing good to this neigh- bourhood and improving the lives of the young people round about them. (Cheers.) In thanking them I would also like to thank the leader of the choir and the organist for their most valuable and excellent work. (Hear, hear.) It is rather difficult for me to refer, as I would like to refer, to the closing portion of the Secre- tary's report. I think it was in perfect taste and very beautifully put. I should like to aid also that had I only known in time I should not have missed the memorial service to my late dear father. I read a report of it afterwards and should like to take this opportur- , ity of thanking Mr Dale and. Mr Ashley for the very I beautiful and appropriate remarks they made. I know that Mrs Crosfield was very much tcuehed by it and I wish I could have been present myself. Although it ' may be a somewhat delicate thiag far me to speak of your late patron, I may perhaps be allowed to say that every ward they uttered was true. (Hear, hear.) I am not going to detain you by speaking at any great length or I could tell you much for, of course my memory travels far beck,but I can assure you of this that knowing intimately what my father bad very much at heart, he has many a time said to me that he never in his life spent money which gave him more pleasure than the money he spent in connection with those mission rooms. (Hear, hear.) I fef 1 leis loss very greatly and I believe you all d (Bear, hear.) In these somewhat cynical days there are now not:many left of that old school to which my father belonged. (Hear, hear.) One feels that the active interest he took in this school room and in the neighbeuring chapel was pro- moted solely by honest single-minded regards for the welfare of his fellow-townsmen, (Hear, hear.) There was to "axe to grind." (Elear,hear.) Pure goodness of heart was the only motive. (Hear, hear.) The Secre- tary in his report which I must again thank fer,hine, expressed the hope that my father's example might be an inspiration to those who have long known him here, and I can assure you that if that example is any en- couragement to the workers here to carry on the same good work, they will be doing the one thing that would p'ease my father more than anything else. There is no I denying it, though I Say it myself, his death is a loss to I this part of the town. (Hear, hear.) The best thing we can do is to try and follow in his steps and remem- ber as he did that over and above religious reasons after all the beet kind of patriotism—best for old ;Eag- land, best for the Empire—is that public spirit which strives for the advancement, moral, material and intellectual, of the people at large. (Cheers.) That was the root principle of my father's public life and we can ill afford to lose noble tradition, noble ideals, and noble examples of that kind. (Clear, hear.) I heartily congratulate you all here who are carrying on the admirable work which I can recollect them starting. (App'ause.) Mr DALE proposed a vote of thanks to Mr Crosfield for presiding and said they deeply appreciated his pre- sence under the circumstances. Mr AST[LEY seconded and said Mr Arthur Crosfield possessed that broadmiudedness and desire for the elevation of the masses which was a great trait in the character of the late Mr Crosfield. Mr N. HOUGH supported. lie referred to the late Mr Crosfield as the great friend of the Mission and said be had reason to believe that Mrs Crosfield would still be their friend. The Vote was carried with acclamation. Mr Crosfield acknowledged the complement and said he would be only too pleased tai supplement the assist- ance which he was Sure the Mission would receive from Mrs Crosfield.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. In connection with the Crippled Girls Industrial Branch of the Watercress alai Flower Girls' Chris- tian Mission, a grand Feral Exhibition will be held in Parr Hall on Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, and Saturday next. At the opening ceremony on Wed- nesday afternoon Mr F Monks, J.P., C.C., will pre- side. The ball will be tastefully fitted up as a huge floral exhibition, with a choice variety of plants, palms and flowers of every de4cription, suitable for conservatories, dining-table, and drawing-ropm decorations. The flowers are an exceedingly close copy of nature, and will afford pleasure to visitors, as showing the skill of these poor crippled girls. A contingent of crippled girls from London will be present making flowers. Vocal and instrumental music will be rendered. There will .be no charge for admission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>CHRONIC COUGH STOPPED,</head>
        <p>A CHRONIC COUGH STOPPED,</p>
        <p>BY VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>it. Mum', 84, Georgians-Street, Bury. writes : —" I have had bronchitis for three months and could not , work. I could not sleep at night without coughing until I got a bottle of VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. The very next night after taking it I was able to sleep all night without coughing, which was a great astonish ment to me. My cough has now gone, and lam able to go to my employment. You may make use of me name with pleasure and refer any patient to me you wish. VENG'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE stops an ordinary cough in one night, and cures chronic coughs,bronchitis, asthma, weak lungs, and whooping cough rapidly. Its vast superiority over ordinary cough mixtures .or any of the emulsions cannot be estimated. It has cured thousands of sufferers after they had been turned out of hosp,tale. Ask for VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE at chemists and medicine vendors. Price lsid and 2s 9d. If you cannot get it at one shop go to another.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EAR LESTOWN. t [BY A CORRESPONDENT]</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EAR LESTOWN. t [BY A CORRESPONDENT]</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>One of the finest exhiSitious of football ever wit- need on the Mesnes ground was the outcome of the meeting between Earlestown and Wigan United on Saturday, the game being fast and exciting from beginning to end, and no doubt when the final came both teams were glad to enjoy a well-deserved rest,</p>
        <p>Earlestown tried a new man in Porshaw, who partnered Barlow on the right wing, Bartley falling back to right half-back. however. after the inter- val, Bartley took up his old position again and Forshaw dropped back into the intermediate line, and the ensuing plays thowed that this policy was the wisest that could have been adopted. Straight from the kick-off both sides went at it hammer and tongs, and although Earlestown had rather the best of the exchanges, the interval came with a clean sheet. With a bit of luck Earlestowu would have taken the lead during the first moiety, almost the whole of the forwards taking ineffective shies at goal. Grime had also his share of work to do, one save of his in particular eliciting rounds of applause. The ball was worked down the field by . the United right wing pair, and a beautiful centre from the outside man left Stansfield with a perfect opening. Instead of shooting at once, the centre forward dribbled the ball n,4arer the posts,with the result that Grime came out to meet him, and picked , the leather up from his feet just as he was steady- ing himself for his shot. It was a glorious piece of work and fully deverved the applause which greeted the effort.</p>
        <p>The second half had been in progress about twenty minutes when the United to3k the lead a fine shot from Stansfield giving Grime no chance of saying. Earlestown went at it:with a will after this but it seemed that their efforts were all in vain until ten minutes before the finish a lovely centre from Bartley landed the ball well in front of Smith, and Leach and Dawson quickly bustled it past Smith. There was no holding the homesters after this, but the United were undoubtedly saved by their custodian, who saved innumerable shots from difficult angles, one shot from Bartley deserving a better fate, the ball coming in with terrific force from the right wing and being caught by Smith as it was entering the corner of the net. The United also had their share of hard luck, and the game was anybody's right up to the finish, the whistle blow- ing with the score standing one goal each.</p>
        <p>Following upon the six goals to nil defeat which the Wiganers inflicted on Earlestown a few weeks ago at Wigan, it looks very much as though the United caught Barlestown on one of their off days. such a crushing defeat can only be explained in this way especially after Saturday's game.</p>
        <p>There was not a weak spot in either teem on Saturday and turning to the attenlance there is no reason why two thousand people cannot come every home match when they are treated to such eacellent displays of football.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>TERRIBLE FATALITY. Boy Killed on the Railway at Sankey.</head>
        <p>A TERRIBLE FATALITY. Boy Killed on the Railway at Sankey.</p>
        <p>A Dangerous Crossing.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Lines Railway Station at Great Sankey was the scene of a shocking fatality on Tuesday morning. The circumstances were fully inquired into by Mr S 13righouse, the county coroner, at an inquiry held in the Sankey Chapel House Inn:3n Wednesday evening. The victim of the disaster was a schoolboy named Arthur Campbell Leonard, son of the Rev Thomas Arthur Leonard. Congrsgational minister and secretary of the Co- operative Holidays Association, of Row, Dumbar- tonshire.</p>
        <p>THE FATHER'S EVIDENCE. The evidence of the boy's father wal to the effect that on Taesday morning he travelled from Man- chester to Great Sankey in company with his son for the purpose of taking him to the Penketh . .</p>
        <p>Friends' School, where he Was to become a resident</p>
        <p>student. He arrived at Sankey by the 910 a.m train, and immediately went to the school. The boy afterwards went back to the station to see his father away by the 10 25 train to Warrington. Asl the train steamed out of the station the father, on looking out of the carriage window, saw his Ison run along the plattorm in the direction in I which the train was moving, and then suddenly turn across the line. At that moment the father heard an express train coming on the down line, and feeling that something had happened to his son he got out of the moving train and walked back to the station, where he found that the boy had been caught by the engin of the express train and killed instantly.</p>
        <p>The Cironer : Was your son a stranger to the place?</p>
        <p>Witness : Entirely. The Coroner: Was it his first visit to this place? Witness : Yes. TILE STIVONMASTER'S VERSION.</p>
        <p>Edward Hewitt Hodwell. stationmaster at San- key said he recollected seeing Sir Leonard joining the train and his boy stanning on the platform seeing him away. When the train started the boy ran alongside the platform but as the train in- creased its speed, the boy was gradually left behind. The boy was going in the direction of the steps leading to the bridge but suddenly he turned to go across the metals. Witness was by this time on the opposite platform. near the booking office. He heard the Hull express coming and shouted to the boy to step back. The boy, however, did not see his danger and stepped right in front of the approaching ex- press. It was a wonder how he got in front of the engine,for in another moment the train would have passed him. The boy could not see the engine on account of the curve.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : Passengers are supposei to go across the bridge? Witness : They are.</p>
        <p>Mr Cooke ;a juryman): Passengers have not time to go up the steps and along the high-road in order to get to the opposite platform. They would have to go ten minutes before the train was due in order to make use of the steps. The Coroner (to wifnees) : The father of the boy has told us that he had a presentiment that something was going to happen, and jumped out of the moving train. Did you notice that ? Witn-ss : I saw him walking along the line coming towards the station. I didn't sic him get out of the train.</p>
        <p>The Coroner The lad was Zkilled by the train instantly ?</p>
        <p>, Witness : Yes. The Coroner : Are thi-re any notice boards saying that passengers must cross the line by the bridge ? Witness: Yes, on both sides of tfle stacion in promi- nent positions. The Coroner : Do you find any diamity respecting passengers going across the line rather than making,use of the bridge ? Witness : Most passengers go across the line. The Coroner: Would it not be a convenience to have</p>
        <p>a foot.-bridge right opposite the booking office ?</p>
        <p>Witness : Yes ; everybody's opinion is that of course. • By the Rev TA. Leonard : Would not nine people out of ten when crossing from on platform to the other use the level crossing ?. Witness : Yes, nine out of ten go over the metal.. RE.v T A Leonard : Did the engine of the express I whistle as it went through the station ? Witness : Yes ; it never fails to do so. Rev T A Leonard : At what distance from the station did it commence to whistle ?</p>
        <p>Witness : I heard it whistling before it reached the curve.</p>
        <p>Rev T A Leonard : Did it continue to whistle ? Witness : It did. Rev T A Leonard : Where were you standing when the accident happened? Witness : I was on the ticket office platform. Rev TA Leonard : How did you get across ? Witness : By the method I al xays adopt. I crossed bz the level crossing behind the train ycu departed by. Rev TA Leonard : Did the porter cross by the same way?</p>
        <p>Witnts4 : Yes. By the Foremau (Mr George Thompson) : Haw many trains go through the station in a day ? Witness : I cannot tell. I should say 50 or 60. Theyoreman : I think it will be nearer 90. What rate of speed was the express train travelling in this case ? Witness : About 50 or 60 milts an hour. The Foreman : You think a bridge acrosa would be a great advantage to rmsengers ? Witness : I cannot say otherwise, of course. Mr Wright (a juryman) : There would still be many ' who would cross the metals ? Witness : They would be able to get across quicker. By the Foreman : A bridge near the booking office would save many from going across the line. REMARKS BY THE CORONER.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said it was a very painful case. He could quite understand what the feelings of Mr Leonard were at losing his son under such circumstances. He (the Coroner) would have been very sorrowful indeed and perhaps would never forget it as long as he lived. The boy naturally wanted to see the very last of his father on parting and ran along- side the train. In the exuberance of his spirits what were notice boards to him ? He saw an easy level crossing to get to the opposite platform and made use of it. Unfortunately an express train was 1 coming, and the jurymen knew what the resul: was. It seemed to him that a was one of those unfortunate 1 circumstances that came within the category of acci- dents when no blame was attached to anybody. They were not there to manage the affairs of the Railway Company, but to find out h,-.vr this particular person had come by his death, and make any suggestions they thought prow r respecting the provision of better facili- ties for crossing the line. He was sure they all sympathised with the Lereavediatiaer in the loss of his 808.</p>
        <p>- - THE FATHER ADDRESSES THE JURA% The Rev. T. A. Leonard, having been permitted to make some observations, thanked the Coroner for his kind and sympathetic words. As the Coroner had already said, it was competent for the jury to make suggestions arising from this event. If any good could come from such a tragic event surely it should be the adoption of some method whereby a repetition of it would be prevented. Had thcre been a footbridge oppo- site the booking office he would have gone over it in the first instance and the boy would have returned the same way. He came to the station as a stranger and crossed the metals by the level crossing and the boy unfortunately went back the same way. He pleaded with the jury to consider three cir- cumstances—first, the extraordinary number of express trains running through the s'ation ; secondly, that a sharp curve prevented passengers from seeing the approach of trains ; thirdly, that • number of boys at ' the school used the lips and needed protection. The Railway Company had no right to allow a death-trap to exist if any ordinary precautions could be taken to prevent it. He trusted the jury would recommend the Railway Company to provide a footbridge near the booking-office, and take all possible means to prevent such a disaster in future,.. The Coroner : Your argument is that this lad was simply returning by the way that you and he had pre- viously taken? mile Rev T A Leonard : Yes ; from the evidenle of the Btationmaster it is a recognized custom for nine out of ten paessogers to use the level crossing. Therefore if the jury have any power they should take some steps to prevent it in future.</p>
        <p>THE VERDICT.</p>
        <p>The jury were then left t,) consider their verdict. After a few minutes deliberation, The Foreman said the jury found that the deceased was accidentally killed by a passing train, and they wished to recommend to the Cheshire Lines Committee that a footbridge or subway should be provided so as to avoid similar accidents. If a footbridge were erected opposite the booking office there would be no necessity for passengers to cross the metals, He might say that he had been in fear many a long day that an accident would happen. It wculd be a great relief to them if the suggestion were carried out. The jury also wished to convey their deepest sympathy with the bereaved father. Mr Simpson, on behalf of the Cheshire Lines Com- mittee said he cordially concurred in the expression of sympathy. The Coronet : What about the bridge or subway ? Mr Simpson : I will lay the matter before the Com- pany. The Coroner said he was very much impressed with the arguments brought forward by the father. This,</p>
        <p>lad was returning the same way his father had gone without ans remonstrance on the pin of the railway company's servants.</p>
        <p>Mr Simpson : We cannot employ men 0 and there to stop them.</p>
        <p>The Coroner (continuing) said the bridge did not seem to be a portion of the company's premises. It was a bridge that carried the roadway over the line. Passen- gers had to leave the company's precnisss altogether and get on a highway, repairable by tOe inhabitants at la/ ge. _ _</p>
        <p>Piz. Simpson : We repair it and keep it in order. The Coroner : It is a high road and is subject to common dangers from horses, traps, &amp;c. Then a num- ber of boys from the school were always going to the station ar d they needed protection. Tlie company must provide for that particular class of traffic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown Police Station on Monday morning before Mr J W Emmett, William Dale, labourer of Tamworth-street, Earlestown, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Earles- town on Saturday afternoon and was fined 2s 61 and costs.</p>
        <p>_ P.S.A. The usual weekly meeting of the Earlestown and District P.S.A. Society, was held • in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Sunday afternoon. There was a good attendance, the meeting being presided over by Mr Ellis. An interesting address was given by the Rev C E Dove. Vocal selections were given by Mrs Mather, and instrumental items by Mr Edwards, violinist. Miss Williamson presided at the piano. ST. JOHN'S CHURCH MEN'S BIBLE' CLASS. A pleasant gathering in connection with the above class took place on Saturday evening in the Mission Hall, Earlestown. The Rev T R Matthews presided, and he was supported by the Rev J C Matthews and Mr Welch of St Helens. The first part of the evening consisted of vocal and instru- mental selections as follows:—Quartette, " Selene," Earlestown quartette party; song, "Oh that summer smiled for aye,' Miss Watkins : selection on musical glasses, Mr J Isherwood ; song, "The song that reached my heart," Nurse Irwin ; song. "Anchored," Mr J Powell ; song, "Eileen Alarms." Miss Clarke ; duet, " The two sailors," Messrs Powell and Edwards ; humorous song, Mr J Isher- wood. Refreshments were afterwards handed</p>
        <p>round and a short address ot welcome was given by the Vicar. A short address on the " Advantages of Men's Classes" was given by Mr Welch. The musical programme was again proceeded with as follows :—Song," 3he Better Land," Nurse Irwin ; ;quartette, " Hear, hear," Earlestown quartette party ; song, " Fairest Maiden," Mr J Edwards ; song, " Dream of Paradise," Miss Watkins ; humor- ous song, Mr J Isherwood ; song, " Wreck of the Hesperus," Mr J Powell. The gathering was closed with the singing of " The Soldier's Hymn," "The Evening hymn" and " The National Anthem." During an interval a collection was made towards insuring the lives of three members of the class who had gone out to South Africa with the Imperial Yeonlanry during last week, in accord- ance with the precedent established in previous cases. The amount realised was £2 83 Bd.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH AND 3dISSION SUNDAY SCHOOL, The prizes gained by the children attending the above schools, during the past 3et:r, were distri- buted in the Mission Hall on Monday evening. The room was well filled with scholars and their parents and friends, who enjoyed an entertainment ' consisting of an exhibition of lime light views des- criptive of the tour of the Duke and Duchess of York to the Colonies. The lantern was manipulated by Mr Lawson, and the pictures which were clear and distinct afforded an excellent panoramic view of the tour. The description was road by the Rev J C Matthews. The Vicar(Rev T R Matthews) pre- sided, and alter the lime light exhibition, Mr T Martindale(general secretary) submitted his report, in which he said that he felt no report had given him such great pleasure 89 that for the year 1901, owing chiefly to the magnificent Success of the Sale of Work recently held, lie might venture to say that the Sunday school stood to-day in a healthier condition financially than ever in its pre- vious history. The Sunday schools were divided into three sections, viz,, senior, junior, and infant, These were further divided into 49 classes. The number of officers was 11, teachers 45, scholars 1,471. The scholars were distributed as follows :—Men's Bible Class 294, conducted by Mr T Molyneux ; Women's Bible Class 182, conduc- ted by Surse Irwin; Young Women's Bible Class 64, conducted by Mrs Wilson; Girl's School 386, superintendent, Mr Burrows ; Boy's School 203: superintendents, Miss Bunting and Miss Crowther, Mission School 92, superintendent, Mr Hughes ; secretary, Mr Brown. The prizes for attendance and lessons won during the year number 192, as against 198 last year ; the average marks per prize winner were 98 out of a possible 104. The net income of the school for the year was £13115s id, and the expenditure £74 2s: leaving a balance iu hand of £57 13s Id. lie could not conclude that report without placing on record the heartiest thanks of the superintendents, secretaries and him- self to every teacher, worker and friend of the Sun- day school who had so nobly assisted them in the work, and whose harmonious libours make such a glowing report possible. Let them thea realise the work they had in hand, and with a strong pull and a pull together they would achieve even greater success in the coming year. (Applause).—The Vicar, owing to the lateness of the hour, then pro- ceeded with the diettibution of the prizes to the I children.</p>
